      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S</head>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S</p>
        <p>Letter To The Guardians On Conscientious Objectors</p>
        <p>LAID ON THE TABLE. Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., has intervened on behalf of two conscientious objectors' wives. To the Clerk to the Warrington Board of Guardians, on Wednesday, he wrote-7 " Representations have been made to me from several quarters that an injustice has been done by reports' (in the local Press) of meet- ings of the Guardians at which an alleged application for poor relief by conscientious objectors was discussed. I have carefully read the reports and they leave on my mind a distinct impression that it is alleged that such application has been made, though I do not, of course, Suggest that you are in any way responsible. As I understand the position, the Government will pay a separation allowance in cases which would, without such assistance, probably necessitate poor law relief. If this is the correct view you will agree that it is very far from an application for poor law relief. I would respectfully suggest that if my belief is well founded it might be graceful and kindly act to women, who are suffering much if you could lend your authority to a short communication to the local Press which might relieve the persons affected from the stigma which surrounds them as a result of the reports I refer to. I am quite certain that your Board would be much grieved if any un- necessary pain had been caused to the wives and relatives of the men concerned, and that they will give their considgation to my re- spectful suggestion that it may be in their power to put right what I conceive to be a misunderstanding." The letter having been read, The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) remarked: "Does the Board want to go into this matter at all? The facts have already been stated, and if someone else outside wants to misunder- stand our action, that is no reason why we should go. into it." The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said he happened to know something about the matter himself. In one case he was very much interested, and was very sorry that the particular course resorted to was adopted by the Tribunal. .The impres- sion was that an application had been made and Mr. Dale had flatly contradicted the state- ment. The Chairman: Mr. Dale was obviously wrong. He was speaking on a subject whith was not within his personal knowledge. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell added that he thought the papers gave colour to the sugges- tion that an atplication had been made, and they were perfectly justified. That impression was left on his own mind at the meeting, and he thought the Board ought to make it quite clear that there was some mistake. The Chairman: No mistake on the part of the Board. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Well, make it quite clear to other people in view of what Mr. Harold Smith has said.</p>
        <p>-t</p>
        <p>The Chairman agreed to put the matter to the vote as to whether another statement of the actual facts should be given, and Mr. Mitchell being the only one to favour this course, the Guardians turned their attention to other sub- jects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>BRONCHITIS &amp; WINTER COUGH!</head>
        <p>BRONCHITIS &amp; WINTER COUGH!</p>
        <p>Breatheable Peps To Save Thousands</p>
        <p>Of Lives.</p>
        <p>The deaths of nearly 100,000 persons in Great Britain are traceable every year to chest trouble. The bad weather brings on a cold, and through wrong treatment or neglect this settles on the chest, weakening the lungs until every breath is painful and made worse by coughing fits that are terrible almost to the point of suffocation. _ _</p>
        <p>- That is bronchitis, the fatal complaint which the Peps breatheable treatment has come to banish.</p>
        <p>It is useless to try and keep the inflamed breathing tubes free, or to strengthen the lungs against the attacks of "cold-germs," by swal- lowing bottles full of cough-mixture into the stomach. There is no direct connection between the lungs and the stomach. The only real way to doctor the breathing tubes is to breathe a medicine in, and this ideal method is made pos- sible for every bronchial sufferer by Peps. The powerful healing medicines that are given off as a Peps tablet dissolves on the tongue penetrate the clogged 'bronchial tubes, soften and release the accumulations of thick phlegm, and heal the sore, inflamed membrane which lines the way to the lungs. . The most stubborn bronchial cough is thus speedily soothed and cured by these healing Peps fumes.</p>
        <p>Peps so strengthen the chest that they put an end to those bad weather bronchial attacks that make the weak-chested afraid to even venture out-of-doors. The old folks who suffer so intensely with every change in the weather should _always have, a box of Peps by their bedside, to stop thd choking and coughing bouts, and to ensure peaceful sleep at nights. Thousands of people rely upon nothing else but Peps to see them safely through the bad- weather period. Peps are just as beneficial to children as to "grown-ups," and can always be relied upon to quickly stop a child's feverish bronchial cold or nasty barking night cough. Peps accomplish what cough mixtures can never do, and no home should ever be without them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>GOOD CYCLE DEAL,</head>
        <p>A GOOD CYCLE DEAL,</p>
        <p>To buy a bicycle for £9 10s. Od., ride it for two years and then sell it for £l2, is good business, yet this is what was accomplished by a storekeeper of Lindley-road,/Orange Free State, one James Nelson. The machine was a Raleigh, and this fact, coupled with the colonial idea that no price is too high to pay for the best article, was probably responsible for the "deal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Aye or Neigh ? A remark in the letter of one of last week's correspondents on active service reminded me of a tit-bit. A couple of years or so ago, when horses were being freely commandeered by the Government, two had been in charge of a carter whom I know personally and who was much attached to them. The man thereupon enlisted, and in due course was drafted to Egypt, whence he writes that, separately and at different places, he has met both his old charges. On each occasion he called the animal by name, and the intelligent creature at once recognised his voice and demonstrated its plea- sure unmistakably. Still Horsey.</p>
        <p>Of course the reader may think what he pleases about the extraordinary coincidence just alleged, but he can hardly need proof of the strong attachment existing between some drivers and their horses. I know of one big, fleshy animal that has become so accustomed to having its mid-day meal in the company of the carter in charge that nothing will now induce it to touch a morsel until he appears with his dinner and takes his seat close by. We have had wonderful dog stories by the score; why not give the (Mantalini's pet phrase) bowwows a rest, and let some of the other quadrupeds have a turn?</p>
        <p>I zaak's Disciples. From the advice to the angler in ¶' Albion, or the double 'Kings," I recall the line, "To mar youi fishing—less you are more careful." What is most calculated to mar a day's fish- ing? According to what I read of the Poynton match, not a few who had enteked for and travelled considerable distances to participate in that event were foiled by stress of weather. Once, however, on a very similar day, while on a railway journey from Otley to Bradford, my attention was attracted near Apperley Bridge to a solitary figure sitting motionless by the riverside, clad in oilskins, with collar turned. up and hat turned down so as entirely to con- ceal the features, reminding one of the modest violet of the poem which "hung its head, as if to hide from view "—and only rod and line exposed as clue to the thystery. By way of comment, I referred to the famous "come in- side" joke, whereupon a fellow-traveller waxed. exceeding wroth and informed me as an ex- pert that the day was one of the most suitable for the sport, and that the angler in question was not, as I evidently imagined, a fit subject for treatment in a mental hospital, but a happy mortal who was to be envied his leisure and mode of spending it. I fear I have not been able to carry the inquiry any farther, and must leave it as a drunken fellow left his hat which lay on the ground and which he apos-- trophised thus: "If I pick you up I fall; if I fall you will not pick me up; therefore I " and, having likewise no alternative, he did what I now do. The Eternal Problem.</p>
        <p>As I read the report of the annual meeting of the Guild of Help connected with Bank Quay Working Men's Mission, I thought of the larger question, which has so long exercised the minds of social reformers, of. the general treatment of the vagrant class. It seems to me that the war has practically disposed of it for the time being, many sturdy beggars as well as professional thieves having hastened to join the colours, and, after all, made men of themselves. Still, what of the defective sub- merged? As to these, assuredly the remedy is detention, segregation, protective care, and one great organisation to deal with the ,unfortu- , nates. A Counsel of Perfection.</p>
        <p>It is no new suggestion that all philanthropic societies that deal with the submerged should unite, co-operate, and systematise their efforts, and that all those private individuals who have money, time, or ability at their command should unite with them, that one great all- embracing organisation, empowered by and, if necessary, aided by the State, should be formed sympathetically ,to consider and wisely attend to the requirements of the class in question. I remember a Poor Law Commission .Report that seemed to point in the direction of some such effort. No doubt sometime in the distant' future we shall try it, but perhaps in this war- time it were "unprofitable all and vain" to discuss it seriously.</p>
        <p>Superstition. • In last week's local news I read of a "lucky" as well as of an "unlucky thirteen," and I thought of many superstitious observances. Will those who draw their blinds on the occa- sion of a deceased neighbour's funeral like to think that what they intend as a token of re- spect had its origin in the belief that if people watch a funeral from the window the dead person may take then with him? Years ago the then Vicar of Eccleshill, who had given much study to the subject, told me that was undeniably so. Why was it ever deemed to be unlucky to walk under a ladder? Because a ladder reared against a building forms a triangle—a way of expressing the Deity—and to walk right through was considered impious. With many among whom I have lived, the howling of a dog at night was held a sure sign of approaching death in the immediate vicinity. In one of my old districts, it was insisted that if a corpse remained in a house over Sunday, two other deaths would speedily follow in the neighbourhood; and whenever I set off to an inque# they warned me that there would be two others directly afterwards, the Coroner invariably having three (inclusive) within a day or two of the first.</p>
        <p>Pioneer Foreign Missionaries. The recurrence of the annual foreign mis- sionary services in these parts suggests the observation that European churches were re- presented on the distant foreign field much more than a century ago and long prior to the, memorable enterprise of the worthy Carey and his contemporaries. Several years ago I read how His Excellency Edward Wilmot Blyden, LL.D., Envoy Plenipotentiary of the Republic of Liberia, was "the guest of the evening" at the annual meeting of the Swedenborg Society, held in London in 1892, and in the course of an address spoke of "the very _liberal and advanced views put forward more than a hun- dred years ago by Emanuel Swedenborg . . . . as to the character of the African," and of the consequent peculiar interest with which his country was vested among students of the writings of Swedenborg. "The most eminent of these early students," said Dr. Blyden, "were Charles Wadstrom and Henry Gandy, of Bristol, who ,were interested, not only in the abolishing of the slave trade by law, but, in fact, in improving the country of the African. These two men visited the West Coast of Africa for the purpose of promoting a plan of a Christian settlement in that coun- try," The outcome of the voyage was a con- ference, the ultimate result of which was a foreign missionary enterprise. There have lately been efforts in Warrington in connection with the New Church, therefore I thought some might be interested in the foregoing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Company. ORDERS FOR WEEK ENDING NOV. 11th,</p>
        <p>By CAPTAIN W. PETER RYLANDS, Officer Commanding. • Officer on duty during the week.—Platoon- Commander Davies. Orderlies.—Lance-Corporal Ryder and Lance.. Corporal Hands. _ _</p>
