      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CORD CUTTER'S BUSINESS IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>CORD CUTTER'S BUSINESS IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>Did The Insurance Policies Go With Transfer Of Business ?</p>
        <p>On Thursday, in the County Court, before his Honour. Judge Barnard Lailey, K.C., an action to recover 0f..58 10s. under a business purchase agreement came on for hearing.`The plaintiff was Mr. George Roger, Bridge Foot, Warrington, and the defendant Mr. Peter Owen, formerly in business at Berkshire-street, Manchester.</p>
        <p>Mr. Artliur Browne, solicitor, for the plain- tiff, said his client is a cord and velvet cutter, carrying on business at Bridge Foot, War- rington, and the defendant Mr. Peter Owen, who formerly carried on a like business at Berkshire-street, Miles Platting, Manchester. Plaintiff -agreed to buy the business carried on by defendant in Manchester, and the sale was to take place on the 12th July, 1915. The agreement was not signed, or, rather, dated, until the .22nd October of last year. The word "Goodwill" was mentioned in the agree- ment, and his contention -was ,that part and parcel of the contract was not only that the business should be transferred, but that he should transfer all the insurance policies. His Honour: Is that mentioned in the agreement specifically F—No, sir. Mr. Browne went on to explain that the business was transferred from Berkshire-street to Warrington. On the 6th March one of the work-girls in Warrington, Annie Salisbury, was injured by an accident and her fingers were amputated. They felt themielves bound by the Workineri's Conipensation Act ,' and having paid the sum of £.3 55., it was agreed to pay a lump sum of £5O into the County Court to the girl's credit. Mr. Owen knew of all this and had many Conversations with the plaintiff as to the insurance policy under the Workmen's Compensation Act, which should cover the liability. They both saw Mr. Smith, the insurance broker, in Manchester, and the latter admitted that he did receive the pre- mium, but the policy could not be found. His Honour: Did the defendant pay the premium from July, 1915, to July, 1916?—Yes. So that your case is that the agreement was first of all to renew the premium and secondly to transfer the benefit of the renewal?—Yes. Mr. Roger, in giving evidence, said that de- fendant had acted as his manager in Man- chester since the 12th July. He told plaintiff that the insurances were falling due and that he would pay all moneys and carry on the business just as if it was his own, and charge plaintiff later on with what he had expended. The business was removed to Warrington at the end of August and defendant still acted as manager. After the accident to Annie Salisbury plaintiff went to look up the policy of insurarce, and Mr. Smith, the agent, ad- milted tb:.t he had received the premium. Defendant never disputed that he had in- structed NI r. Smith to transfer these policies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Defence.</head>
        <p>The Defence.</p>
        <p>By Mr. .!ilbert Jordan, who (instructed by Messrs..' Richard Higham and Co., Manchester) represented defendant: The business in Man- chester was carried on under the name of Stockton and Lee, and the insurances were in that name and issued in respect of the Berk- shire-street premises. It was understood that the business• would go to Warrington. Mr. Jordan said the agreement made no men- tion of any obligation on the part of the defendant to transfer these policies; and fur- ther submitted that an assignment of the policy would not bind the Insurance Company. His Honour said he had to satisfy himself as to whether defendant had arranged with the plaintiff to make such compunications with the Insurance Company as would *transfer the policy. Mr. Owen, the defendant, said that before the arrangement for taking over the business was made„ he told Mr. Roger that the premium on the fire and workmen's insurance policies ran out on the 10th July, and said he had better leave the transfer of the business until the same date, and then they could start! afresh. They took stock, and on the 10th July witness paid the fire and workmen's insurance for Berkshire- street, Manchester. At that time no arrange- ments had been made for the transfer of the- business to Warrington.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Browne: -Witness did not tell the in- surance agent, Mr. Smith, that Mr. Roger owned the business in future.</p>
        <p>His Honour reserved judgment until next Court day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>FOUND DROWNED.</head>
        <p>FOUND DROWNED.</p>
        <p>Old Fisherman Who Was A Good Swimmer.</p>
        <p>On Sunday 'forenoon the body of an elderly man was found in the old diversion of the river Mersey at Wilderspool, and was removed to the mortuary by Detective Anderson. It was subsequently identified as that of Peter Molyneux, aged 70 years, 45, School Brow, warehouseman.</p>
        <p>lir. F. A. Jones, Deputy Coroner, held an inquest on Tuesday; when a wiredrawer named William Mather, stated he had lodged with de- ceased in the same house for the past fifteen years. Molyneux left the house about 4.30 on Saturday, and was then in his usual good spirits. He used sometimes to complain that a man and a boy followed him to the door and shouted after him, but that was a delusion. He was a good swimmer* and was fond of fishing, though he had not done so lately.</p>
        <p>Detective Anderson said that with the ex- ception of a cap the. body was fully clothed. There was a quantity Of fishing line, fish-hooks, &amp;c. in the pockets. The jury returned a verdict of "Pound drowned," adding that they had no evidenee how deceased got into the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>HARVESTING IN FRANCE.</head>
        <p>HARVESTING IN FRANCE.</p>
        <p>How The Difficulties Of ‘Var-Time Have Been Surmounted.</p>
        <p>'Various expedients have been devised by the small farmers in Northern France in the effort to cope with the great scarcity of labour. With the co-operation of the civil and military authorities, however, and the self-sacrificing exertions of the old men, women, and children, the harvest has been reaped and threshed, and it is gratifying to find that the yield of grain in several departments near the battle zone is relatively little below that of 1914. The sys- tem generally adopted has been to " pool " all the available labour in each farming centre and to apply it to each holding in turn, with- out regard to ownership. Implements given to the indomitable peasant farmers of the Marne and the Meuse by British farmers through the Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund have once again played an impor- tant part in the gathering and threshing of the grain. These implements have also been " pooled " and have greatly helped to make good the deficiency of machinery resulting from the invasion. The peasants have again and again expressed their gratitude to English farmers, whose timely and practical help has proved so beneficial. Such aid the fund pro- poses to give in Belgium, Serbia and Poland, as the opportunity arrives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WRONG NAME.</head>
        <p>WRONG NAME.</p>
        <p>Cyclist Fined For Deceiving The Police.</p>
        <p>An illustration of the unswerving determina- tion of justice in pursuing the wrongdoer was afforded at the Borough Police Court on Wed- nesday, when Joseph Jones, boatman, of Mug House, Dallam, was charged with having on the 17th April last ridden a bicycle in the dark without lights, and also for giving a wrong name and address when stopped by the Con- stable.</p>
        <p>Defendant was fined 205., or in default 14 days, in respect of each charge-2 in all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TWELVE MONTHS FOR JEWEL</head>
        <p>TWELVE MONTHS FOR JEWEL</p>
        <p>THEFT.</p>
        <p>Twelve months' imprisonment was the sen- tence, at Liverpool County Sessions, on Tuesday, on ,Arthur Williams (29), labourer, who-had pleaded guilty to breaking into the shop of Stubbs, -Ltd., at • Warrington, and stealing a gold albert. Mr.- Lindon Riley said prisoner was found sleeping out with the stolen article in his possession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SHORTHAND NOTES.</head>
        <p>SHORTHAND NOTES.</p>
        <p>Produced, But Discredited On</p>
        <p>Cross-Examination.</p>
        <p>A most unusual case was heard in the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, when the evidence of a witness who produced shorthand notes was upset on cross-examination by Mr. Nicholls, chief constable. Francis E. Penketh, insurance manager, 42, Sankey-street, was summoned for failing to screen lights in his offices, and• which were visible in King-street. Defendant denied the charge as stated by P.C. Gibbs (who was cor- roborated by Inspector Hesp) and produced a witness named Hughes, who said that a light could be seen in, the room from the street, but that there was no reflection across the roadway. Witness further stated that the police constable told Mr. Penketh that as the windows were shaded half-way up he would probably hear no more about it.</p>
        <p>P.C.,Gibbs denied that he used these words, and the Chief Constable closely cross-examined witness on the point. Defendant then put forward his daughter, shorthand writer and typist in the office, who, he said, had taken down in shorthand the con- versation which the police officer had with him. • The young lady then proceeded ,to read out her notes, which were in the form of a report of what took place.• The Chief Constable intervening, said that it was impossible, judging from the style, that the reported conversation could have been taken down at the time.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Councillor H. Roberts): Are you quite sure you took the conversation down in shorthand at the time, or is what you have read a recollection of what occurred and was written afterwards?</p>
        <p>Witness said the conversation was reported at the time.</p>
        <p>P.C. Gibbs said the witness took no notes whatever. Part of the time she was standing looking at him with her mouth open. The Chief Constable asked witness if the notes she read were those she took down at the time, and she replied, "No." - The Chief Constable: I would ask the Court to direct an officer to proceed to the office and let this young lady hand over the notes she took at the time. It is an endeavour to do some- thing that is highly improper. The Chairman: This is a very serious matter giving evidence upon oath, and the consequence of making. any false statement is very seriou3. We are very anxious that you should not do yourself an injustice. Tell me, did you take any notes at all while the officer was present, or did you write down after the officer had gone away what was your recollection of what took place?</p>
        <p>Witness: I took some notes down while the officer was there, and when he went out I put down my own recollections.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: You have not got the original notes?--No.</p>
        <p>And you can't produce them?—l don't know where they are at present. The Chief Constable: This case is one of exceeding gravity and I cannot conceive for one moment why this young girl should be allowed to tell the Court that that note was made at the time the conversation took place, when, subsequently, she admits to the Court that•it was made afterwards. It is a disgrace- ful proceeding. Defendant: I have not asked the witness to say anything. The Chief Constable: I suppose you say you never mentioned it to your daughter. Defendant said he admitted that the light was not as much shaded as it ought to have been.</p>
        <p>• The Chairman: I think it is quite clear that you had not shaded them sufficiently, and, therefore, the lights were showing as the offi- cers stated. I do regret that this latter evi- dence was put in. The magistrates are of opinion that the case is proved, sip if it had not been for the way in which th 6 defence has been conducted—l am now speaking of the kind of evidence put in—the magistrates would have taken a very lenient view. They' must accept the evidence of the police, and, under all the circumstances, you will be fined 20s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>OTHER LIGHTING CASES.</head>
        <p>OTHER LIGHTING CASES.</p>
        <p>Other cases of failing to reduce lights were William Greenhalgh, 196, Knutsford-road, 205.; Joseph Lythgoe, 31, Aikin-street, 10s.; John Horne, 2, Dial-street, 10s.; James Lionel Jar- dine, 113, Bridge-street, 155.; Walter Brace- girdle, 100, Buttermarket-street, 10s.; Margaret Collins, 14, Old-road, 2s. 6d.; Michael Swift, 7, Cloth Hall-yard, 55.; Mary Williamson, The Cottage, Sankey, in respect of shop, 6, Bylands- street, 20s. _</p>
        <p>At -Thursday's Police Court the following fines were inflicted for breaches of the lighting regulations :—Evan E. Hughes, 10, Crosfield- street, 405.; Jos. Proffit, 178, Wilderspool- causeway, 10s.; Albert Griffiths, 88, Padgate- lane, 55.; George Davey-Smith, printer, Xing- street, 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>LAME SHEEP.</head>
        <p>LAME SHEEP.</p>
        <p>Fines For " Travelling " Them • At Warrington.</p>
        <p>"The Bench is quite satisfied that the case is fully proved. They are extremely dis- satisfied with some of the evidence which has been given." _</p>
        <p>Such were the remarks made by the Chair- man of the Bench (Mr. A. J. Jolley) at the Borough Police Court on Thursday, at the conclusion •of a case in which Cyril Shaw, son of Mr. Richard Shaw, 104, Orford-laiie, was fined 20s. for cruelty to three sheep by "travel- ling" them in an unfit state. Mt. T. S. Steel appealed for the defendant and pleaded "Not guilty!'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Inspector's Story.</head>
        <p>The Inspector's Story.</p>
        <p>Inspector Bonham, of the N.S.P.C.A., said that on October 17th. he visited the cattle pen at the London and "North-Western Railway Siding at Bank Quay and examined a number of sheep, some of which the defendant came to take away. Witness informed him that three of them 'were , lame and that he ,had better put them in the cart. He put one in and two others he drove. When asked to put the others in he replied: "I have put one in the cart; the others can walk." The animals could hardly walk at all, their legs being very much swollen.</p>
        <p>P.C. Bartley gave corroborative evidence. All three sheep, he said, were lame, especially one of them. ,</p>
        <p>Asked bi Mr. Steel as to whether he was aware the sheep were examined three times by veterinary surgeons before they left Birken- head, Inspector Bonham replied that that might be so, but it was quite possible for them to be lame on reaching Warrington. Mr. Steel: Amongst other sheep you eaam- ined that morning, were there some belonging to a butcher named Cross?—Yes.</p>
        <p>Did you tell the lad Cross that he would be summoned?—No; I only cautioned him. Questioned as to whether it was only a man named Meakin who came for the sheep, and not Shaw, witness replied that both of them came. Witness also denied the statenient that Meakin told him he could not pick out the sheep which were lame until they had walked a distance. The fact that the sheep had been penned up all the previous night would not account for their lameness. Ile cautioned Shaw before he went into the pen that the sheep were not fit to travel. An Indignant Defendant.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel said Mr. Shaw seemed rather *in- dignant regarding the prosecution. He had carried on his business for a great number of years and he endeavoured to treat all ani- mals with the greatest of kindness. They had done all they possibly could for the lame sheep. The .animals had had a rough passage from' Imlaxid, and, if lameness was detected at Birkenhead, the animals were always either detained or slaughtered immediately. In his evidence, the man Meakin said he went up to the pen himself, and Shaw was not with him.</p>
        <p>Cyril Shaw said he took the pony and float to meet Meakin and met a number of the sheep which had strayed' near the Froghall Railway Bridge. John Cross,' who who was also bringing sheep, said he saw Meakin at the pen, but not the boy Shaw,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Parades.</head>
        <p>Parades.</p>
        <p>To-morrow (Sunday), 3 p.m.—Cricket Ground, Arpley, Company Drill. Monday, 8 p.m.—Headquarters, Platoons Nos. 1 and 2.</p>
        <p>Tuesday, 8 p.m.—Headquarters, Platoons Nos. 3 and 4.</p>
        <p>Thursday, 8 p.m.—Headquarters, Platoone Nos. 5 and 6.</p>
        <p>Sunday, November 12th.—Mayoral Proces- sion. Special Church Parade; Fall-in Head- quarters 9.45 a.m. prompt. Recruits.—Monday, Tuesday and Thursday, 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>Musketry..—. Tuesday, Wednesday and Friday, 7.30 p.m., Legh-street Range. By order,</p>
        <p>F. H. SWALLOW, Acting Adjutant,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>THICK TYRES FOR WINTER. I have been experimenting lately with a pair of carrier tyres on a machine which I use entirely for what I may term " business " purposes, that is, as a means of getting about as distinct from pure pleasure-riding. Almost the only bogey eft' in cycling is that of punctures, and in a machine used for business, where one has to catch trains, or keep appointments, a puncture may be a much more serious matter than the simple item of the time spent in repairing it. It is not the puncture that matters, so much as the time lost in repairing it. The objection to carrier tyres is that being very thick and heavy, they undoubtedly slow the machine, i.e., make the work of propulsion harder. I have never noticed this in business riding. But I have tried the machine occasionally over country roads, and I find that I am distinctly slower than on the "jigger" I keep for pleasure purposes— which machine, incidentally, is fitted with light, open-sided tyres. On the other hand, the carriers have, so far, kept me absolutely free of punctures, and I can tackle the worst gravel-strewn roads with- out fear. That is a decided comfort- The result of my three months' trial is that for business purposes I shall stick to my carriers, and I recommend this to all who are using their bicycles largely in busi- ness. The cyclist who rides to and from business will find the carrier tyre a great convenience. It means a minute or two longer on the journey, but the business rider is not usually interested in time schedules so much as in the avoidance of "regret- table incidents." Any agent will get and fit these for you. They are a stock item with the leading tyre companies. THE MOTOR CYCLE TYRE.</p>
        <p>No One, of course, however careful he may be, can help an accident happening to his tyre. The most expensive tyre may receive a bad gash the first time it is used. On the other hand the cheap- est of cheap tyres may run for two or three thousand miles. and even more, without any mis- hap at all. But apart from an accident, one can lengthen the life of one's tyres very greatly by giving constant attention to the covers, to say noth- ing of saving continual trouble and annoyance with punctures. If the machine is being used pretty constantly at least once a week each tyre should be carefully examined, and any small repairs which may be necessary attended to at once. Small, sharp pieces of grit are a constant and fruitful source of trouble. If a minute piece gets into the rubber and is not extracted, it will rapidly work its way into the canvas and finally into the tube. During its passage, moreover, it allows water to penetrate, and this has a very deleterious effect cm the fabric. Each tyre • should be gone over and any pieces of foreign matter should be eased out with the point of a knife. A little solution worked into the cut with a knife edge will heal the wound. Some motor cyclists are extraordinarily careless as regards their spare tubes. On no account carry them anyhow, bat see that they are well protected from injury. Rubber Quickly perishes if' exposed to sunlight, or if it comes into contact with all. The simplest plan is to carry the spare tube in a waterproof bag, the inside of which has been well dusted with French chalk. A NEW LIGHTING ORDER.</p>
        <p>A new Order as to lights on vehicles has just come into operation, applicable to all parts of the coun- try, except the Metropolitan area. The restrictions prescribed by this new Order are the same as those which have been in force in the Eastern counties and certain Midland towns for nearly a year, but one change has been made which,-it is anticipated, will be of considerable convenience to many motor- ists—those who carry as sidelights only candle or oil lamps without lens fronts will be allowed to use their headlights, provided they are reduced and obscured as prescribed in the Order. The use of headlights on vehicles provided with electric or acetylene sidelights or with oil sidelights having lens fronts is still prohibited. If lamps with fronts exceeding six inches across are used, the fronts must be so covered that light can only pass through a central portion not exceeding 41 inches in diameter. All electric and acetylene lamps (in- cluding the central apertures of headlamps reduced as described), and all candle or oil lamps with lens fronts, must be obscured as at present with at least one thickness of ordinary white tissue paper or an equivalent medium.</p>
        <p>WHOOPING COUGH, and all Coughs in family Cured by Veno's Lightning Cough Cure..</p>
        <p>Mrs. Neville, 48, Earl-street, Walsall, says : u Nothing I have ever tried can cure coughs like Veno's Lightning Cough Cure. When my little girl had whooping cough very severely I gave her Veno's 'and it seemed no time till she was well again. Since then my husband and self have taken Veno's as well as the children, and it never fails."</p>
        <p>Trust Veno's to cure Coughs, Colds, Asthma, Bronchitis, Influenza, Catarrh, and Whooping Cough. Prices 111 d., 1/3, and 3/-, everywhere.</p>
        <p>The Duration of the War.</p>
        <p>" D'ye think the fighting 'll be over this year, Mrs. Brackett?" "Well I do, an' I don't, as the sayin' is. My ole Bill's a glutton when 'e starts an' e'll take some stoppin' now 'e's got the law on 'is side for onst."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ANGELS AT MONS.</head>
        <p>THE ANGELS AT MONS.</p>
        <p>Were Superhuman Powers Exerted For Allies In Retreat ?</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James, preaching his second sermon of a series on "God and the War" at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday evening, spoke on the now famous controversy respecting the angels at Mons. Very soon .after the tremendous fighting at Mons, said the preacher, strange stories began to reach us of visions seen by our Men in that critical period of this conflict. Angels it was affirmed, had been seen at Mons. It had, been visibly demonstrated, it was reported, that powers quite other than the human- powers ,Jf warfare had been exerted on the side of the British. Strange portents had appeared in the sky and had been clearly visible to many. The stories travelled through, the Press, and from mouth to mouth, until it is safe to assume that there is hardly a corner in this land of ours at any rate, which has not heard of the .angels at Mons. It seems to me that you have got to build the whole of these up with the long list of such narratives which history presents us with. "If you ask me whether men had a vision of angels at Mons, I shall say that it depends alto- gether on a study of the evidence available, whether you are to believe that. But if you ask me whether it was possible for men to have a vision at Mons, I shall certainly say that it was undoubtedly possible. I think the evidence, the modern evidence, existing for visions of, some kind is now too well sorted and attested for any denial-to be possible, and that strange powers and forces are at work in the mental and spiritual world of which we have very incom- plete knowledge, must he counted one of the certainly ascertained facts of modern research. Having said that, it is only right to say that to assert the possibility of a vision either at Mons or anywhere else is not to assert its ex- planation. There are many people who would agree that such a thing is possible who 'would, of course, flatly deny .that it portended any- thing except the result of the working of the mind in those who saw it, who would say that the mind, under great stress and strain, is un- usually critical, and desperate circumstances conjured up an idea, and that the idea took possession of them so strongly that it became clothed for them in a material and external form.</p>
        <p>4 The Workings of the Mind.</p>
        <p>"There are many who would be prepared to go further than. that. We are beginning to know more of the working of mind upon mind. Tele- pathy is beginning to tell us in ways which we did not understand before that mind can net upon mind without any physical means of inter- course, that the ordinary means by which we express ourselves to each other is not essential for the exertion of influence, and that there is such a thing as one mind communing with an- other over Space without any external means being employed at all. But it does not seem t`o. me that in saying that telepathy is telling Us anything that religious men and women did not know before; for surely the fundamental thing about religion is just the fact that we believe the mind of man can have vital and living con- tact with the mind of God. Speak‘to Him then, for He hears, and spirit with spirit can meet, Closer is He than breathing, and nearer than hands or feet.</p>
        <p>And it is at least conceivable that the contact between the mind of the unseen God and the mind of man may at certain times be of such a nature as to produce the effect of an external vision, a vision which in that case is more than the outcome of the imagination of a man's mind, but does represent the working of that mind as acted upon by the mind of God. , "I do not believe that God was more present at Mons if men saw a vision, than He would have been if they did not see it. God is present everywhere. He was at Mons. He is on the Somme. He is all around us. He is here now. The invisible spiritual force of the Unseen is always at work in God's world. Some men are very conscious of it. Some are quite uncon- scious of it. The only way in which you can become conscious of it is by making the ex- periment of entrusting yourselves to His keep- ing. The only way you can be more certain of God is by becoming more like him. Jesus did not say that you could get nearer to God by staying up all night gazing into a crystal. TTe said. 'Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LATE MR. BEN FEW.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. BEN FEW.</p>
        <p>Striking Tribute At Memorial Service.</p>
        <p>A memorial service to the late Mr. Benjamin Few was. held at the Golborne-street Baptist ChUrch on Sunday evening. There • was a very large' congregation present, and the Pastor, the Rev. H. Ogden, took for his theme, "The Heavenly. Multitude," which was based upon the text, Revelations xix., 6, "The voice of a 'great multitude saying,' alleluia." The preacher declared that Mr. Few would be at home. Where there was music Mr. Few would be at home. There was. a joy in remem- bering that the Redeemed had. passed to a land where happiness was not measured by the frailty of human lives, and where joy did not hang upon a mere thread. Who were this multitude? John elsewhere declared theria to be the redeemed; those who had washed their robes and made, them clean in the Blood of the Lamb. They were those; who had given eon- Seious homage to the Lord Jesus Christ. They were not those who were simply good. There was in Mr. Few's life an acknowledged suprerhacy of his Lord, and thus now that he had ascended on high he would be no stranger; he would be in the largest, fullest sense of the term "at home." His Voice would readily mingle with the angelic choir. Mr; Few's great contribution to life was his cheeriness. He dispelled 'gloom like a spring .morning. He could and did extract hope from the most de- pressing situation. He made the place of his business a help to those in, gloomy environs. His work for the Church was long and continuous. The Church loved him for his work's sake. For 28 years he was a deacon, and belonged to that rare order in these days, "The Twicers." Not once but twice on the Sabbath 'Day he was found in his accustomed place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Habit.</head>
        <p>Habit.</p>
        <p>I trust everything, under God, to habit. Make sobriety a habit, and intemperance will be hateful; make prudence a habit, and reckless profligacy will he as contrary to the nature of the child, grown or adult, as the most atro- cious crimes are to any of us.—:Lord Brougham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WHITECROSS HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>War Office Gratitude To Guardians.</p>
        <p>The War Office having been informed that the Board of Guardians are prepared to allocate 200 more beds for the use of wounded soldiers, a letter was received from the Army Council at the Guardians' meeting, on Wednesday, thanking them for their'patriotic action.</p>
        <p>A letter was also received from the Local Government Board, who wrote stating that they were prepared to approve the proposal, pro- viding that satisfactory arrangements could be made for the accommodation of the inmates of the Union Infirmary, who were to be displaced when the scheme took effect. Mr. Long had received a report from Mr. Elias (Local Government Board inspector) Amon the proposal of the Guardians to remove all those cases to the Workhouse premises, and it appeared .to him, as at present advised, that, espeeially having regard to the steep staircases existing in the Workhouse and the general arrangements of those premises, serious hardship would .be caused by the removal to the Workhouse of cases other than the chronic or bed-ridden and senile cases. Provision should be made in the Workhouse premises for poses of urgency. Other comparatively acute ,cases qt present maintained in the Infirmary should be removed as soon as they were fit to travel to such In- firmary accommodation as could be obtained in other Unions. The Board's officers wollild render every assistance in their power in secur_ ing such accommodation. Mr. Long understood that one matter influencing the Guardians in the direction of placing their Infirmary patients in the Workhouse premises had been that they would not be removed from the neighbourhood of their relatives.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. j. Jolley) .explained that a special committee of the Board had got to work, had two interviews with Mr. Elias, and already the outlines of a very suitable arrangement had been mapped out. The scheme would be' an economical one, although that would not affect the Guardians as the whole of the expenditure would be met by the military authority. They would try to avoid any un- necessary transfers to other -Unions, so that there would be as little hardship as possible, their intention being to make it as convenient as they could, both for the patients and their relatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>UNREPRESENTED.</head>
        <p>UNREPRESENTED.</p>
        <p>Guardians And Workers' Con- ference On Food Prices.</p>
        <p>The Board of Guardians shook their heads horizontally at a meeting on Wednesday in answer to a request to send delegates to a con- ference on food prices ',to be held in December at the Co-operative Hall at the initiation of the Organised Workers of the town. The letter. sent stated that a meeting of Organised Workers had already been held at the Socialist Hall, and it was proposed that another conference 'should be held, to which representatives of public bodies were invited. In the letter it was requested that a reply should be forwarded stating whether a repre- sentative would be sent from the Board of Guardians or otherwise.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley): Otherwise Mr. W. J. Shaw: Otherwise.</p>
        <p>Members nodded their heads with approval. All except the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, that gentleman rising and asking whether .they should not take advantage of an opportunity for discussing the very vital question of food prices. He did not see why the source from which the suggestion came should result in their cutting themselves off from a very profit- able discussion, and he would be prepared to move that the Chairman, as a fit and proper person, should attend and hear what had to be said.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shaw: Is there-no delegation from the parsons?</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Knowles (to Mr. Mitchell, who is a vegetarian) : Why don't you propose your- self and go and talk about the price of meat, for instance. (Loud laughter.) There was no seconder to Mr. Mitchell's pro- posal, and, therefore, the Chairman declared that the Board would 't3end no representative, but he added that it was open to anyone of them to take part in any conference of the kind if they so desired. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I don't think we need be afraid of it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Far from being afraid, some Guardians have been dealing with the matter for months Past. The Reverend Gentleman:- That is all the more reason why they should attend. Finis!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>REV. DR. .1. ESTLIN CARPENTER , AT CAIRO STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>REV. DR. .1. ESTLIN CARPENTER , AT CAIRO STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>A large congregation gathered in Cairo- street Unitarian Chapel on Sunday afternoon to hear the Rev. Dr. J. Estlin Carpenter, of Oxford, and nephew of the late Dr. Philip P. Carpenter, preach on the subject of "God and the War." Choosing as his texts "God is ft spirit and they that worship Him must wor- ship Him in spirit and in truth" and "For of Him and through Him and to Him are all things," he showed that these words contain the fundamental truths of religion. Religion, he declared,- is wrought out of experience. It must grow with our. growth; it must be nur- tured with our life out of the teaching and example of parents iii the home, out of vic- tories and defeats, out of endeavours .and disappointments, out of knowledge and ignor- ance, out of the mysteries of love and suffer- ing, out of the incitements which come to us unknown, unseen, unheard from day to day. "In the intensity of the present struggle," he said, "we must establish future security; liberty and peace, eliminate from the contest as far as it is humanly possible all motives of jealousy, hatred, enmity, revenge, and thut all theological, differences will fall away and a hundred avenues of helpfulness will .open on every side. Our own men have gone forth —thousands .of them—from our own group cf -churches, ready to lay down their lives for their ,country. What do we who rem' in at home do? Generous gifts, devoted and per- sonal labour, have not been lacking, but have we been more devoted, more humble-minded, more steadfast in prayer, more loyal to the spirit of Jesus, more willing to follow whither Ile has led the, way? These must be the Ob- jects of our churches: We must be joined in a common reverence as well as in common duty, faith and love, for then only can we commit our cause to God with surrendered hearts, when all things, even loss and pain and death, shall work together for good.";</p>
