        <p>Nephew Fred Rathbone, 112, Algernon-street (Parochial School).—" Collect money out of every street and all the works." ..</p>
        <p>Nephew Colin Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Collection boxes should be put in every shop in town. Children in the schools might be asked to contribute their coppers."</p>
        <p>Niece May Jones, 10, Chorley-street (Silver- street School).—" Three children from each school should act the character of men who have 'won the Victoria Cross. They should have a box containing an appeal for money for Christ- mas puddings to make our soldiers happy. The one with the box should take the character— Nurse Florence Nightingale."</p>
        <p>• Nephew Peter Appleton, 23, Heaton-street, would make a tray and sell flags and other articles, settling 'the proceeds for puddings, along with a letter to the soldiers.</p>
        <p>Nephew John Forsyth, 10, Heaton-street (Silver-street School), thinks money might be raised by writing a book, "The Retreat of the South Lancs. at the Battle of Mons."</p>
        <p>Niece Ada Shipley, 67, Cartwright-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Get the head teachers at all the schools to ask the children to bring halfpennies Or pennies, or what their parents could spare."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Freaney, 107, Cartwrlght- street (Sacred Heart School).—" Let children, while it is their holidays, go round to the houses and collect a little sum each. The very rich could give turkeys, geese, fowl, ales and other spirits, &amp;c., to be drawn for. Then everybody would help."</p>
        <p>Nephew Frank Holmes, 1, Hamilton-street.— "Sell flags with the South Lancashire badge upon them, to benefit a Warrington Regiment. Many office men and clerks, if they are true Warringtonians, would buy them. Another way is to decorate the motor omnibuses with red, white and blue artificial flowers and the badge, and ask the people to pay a copper or two more for their ride. Some Warringtonians would perhaps like to pay twopence for their "War- rington Examiner" till Christmas', and your agents would send you the contributions."</p>
        <p>Niece M. A. Lunny, 108 a, Bridge-street (Arp- ley-street School).—" Every scholar in Warring- ton should try to bring something that could be sold in a jumble sale of articles for which their parents have no further use. The splendid band of the regiment might play afternoon and night."</p>
        <p>Niece Dorothy Cotton, 117, Liverpool-road.— "Every school in the town should have a chil- dren's concert. No mother or friend would .mind going to se d their little ones doing theN, bit for such a good cause." Nephew Harry Heywood, 8, Matthews-street (Parochial School).—" Raffle a fountain pen; sell tickets with pictures of great men on them. Rich people could easily do without something for a few weeks and give the money saved to the fund."</p>
        <p>Nephew Chas. L. Geddes, 51, Laira-street (Hamilton-street School).—" School children could raise a large sum of money making house- to-house collections and selling flags, ecc. I, for one, would be willing to give my service in any form."</p>
        <p>, Niece Florrie Overendf 74, Lythgoes-lane (No. 824).—" Give all shopkeepers a collection box. People should do without luxuries and send all they can to the . Army Christmas Funds."</p>
        <p>Nephew Frank Overend, 74, Lythgoes-lane.— " The gallant South Lancashires, who have recently made their name, want providing with puddings for • the approaching Christmas. To do this the people of Warrington are asked to help' a little. The chief, elders of Warrington should submit' the Army Christmas Pudding Fund to the toiviispeoPle. A -social shoffild be held and flags should 1)e sold. Give shopkeepers a collection box."</p>
        <p>Nephew Joseph Spencer; 89, Laira-street.— " Give all shopkeepers a box; have a flag day; give each child an envelope to take home to bring their Saturday's half-penny to his (or her) school on the following Monday morning." Nephew Percy Gregory, 105, Orford-lane (Hamilton-street School), suggests a concert or something of that line in the Town Hall; with ,the admission of 6d. each, as every 6d. buys a pudding. - Niece Ethel Shaw, Surinyside, 82, Chester- road (Bolton Council School).—" A special con- cert, the prices of admittance being a little more than at ordinary times; or a bazaar." Niece Amy Reading, 26, St. Barnabas'-place (Evelyn-street School).—" By collecting from the schools or going romad from house to house; by getting up a draw or sale of work." Niece Lorna Reading, 26; St. Barnabas'-place (Eirolyn-street School).—" A draw; by the schools collection, which at present the majority of them' are doing; by the teachers getting up concerts. Children could put their money into the Christmas Pudding Fund instead of going to so many places of amusement and buying sweets."</p>
        <p>Niece Edith Carter, 174, Wellfield-street, Evelyn-street School (No. 873).—" Bazaars organised by the thildren of the local schools. Certain classes in those particular schools could</p>
