        <p>various orchestral playing in London theatres and music halls. It will be when peace comes that the women who have done so much to carry on in the absence of the men, will find a difficulty.l.in retaining the positions the war has opened out to them. A New Blouse.</p>
        <p>The blouse, despite its many rivals, is as popular as ever, and in crepe de chine is having an unprecedented vogue. Every type of the garment from the delicate semi-evening blouse to the severest tailored shirt, is this season of crepe de chine. Georgette, net and chiffon aro still to the fore for this type of blouse, but pride of place must be given to the older fabric, which retains all its time-honoured popularity. Every day some new feature is introduced, and one of the latest is the double basque. On a lace blouse the second basque, which is nothing more or less than a fussy little frill depending from a narrow waist band, is decidedly smart, if it is worn over a very deep basque of lace. Another new crepe de chine blouse has very long crossover fronts, which are brought to the back and caught in a buckle, the ends falling on a square full frill, which is placed at the back only, for there is no basque in front. Scotch Scones. -</p>
        <p>Take a quart of flour, a tablespoonful of butter, not melted, a large pinch of salt, a tablespoonful of soda, two teaspoonfuls of cream of tartar (two teaspoonfuls of baking- powder may be used instead of the soda and cream of tartar), and two tablespoonfuls of granulated sugar. Sift the baking-powder into the flour, and- rub the butter into it, adding the salt and half of the sugar. With new milk mix a soft dough. Flour the board and knead the dough until it will roll out. Separate it into halves, and roll each to an inch in thick- ness, making them round. Put them on tins, cut crosses in the top, and bake until a light brown. -Take the scones out of the oven and brush them over the top with the yolk of an egg beaten with a little milk, sprinkle over them the remainder of the sugar, and put them in the oven long enough to dry. Rice and Cabbage. •</p>
        <p>Required: A small spring cabbage, two ounces of rice, one- ounce of butter, one ounce of grated d,ry-cheesei half a gill of cream, and some croutons of bread fried in' butter. Boil the cabbage until tender in salted water, and when well drained chop it on a board. Rinse the rice through several waters; have a sauce- pan three-parts full of boiling water, put in a little salt, and throw in the rice. Boil it rapidly, and when tender, drain as dry as it is possible to do. Set it on a dish near the fire, and stir it lightly with 'a fork to assist the drying. Then mix cabbage and rice together, adding cheese and cream.• Return the mixture to the stewpan to get thoroughly hot. This done, arrange the fried bread round the inside of a vegetable dish, and pile the mixture high in the centre of it. The cheese may be omitted where it disagrees.</p>
        <p>sew small articles. Girls who are learning cookery could make biscuits and toffee: I am sure the teachers would be delighted to help them."</p>
        <p>Nephew Ernest Carter, 174, Wellfield-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Everybody can et, his share, for little bits make large things. Good painters could paint flags on silk ribbons and then sell the flags in aid of this local fund. Paper flags could be sold for id. each for this purpose, just as they have been sold for several other purposes. Concerts could be organised ny local friendly societies, and I am sure large numbers of people would support ,them if they knew that the proceeds were for the Christmas puddings for our local soldiers. Some scholars might organise some sports or fairs." Nephew Harold Dutton' 4, Owen-street.— "Everybody that can afford anything will In their uttermost in putting something towards the puddings for the gallant South Lancs." Niece Lily Williamson, 53, Priestley-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Every member should bring a copper for several weeks; there are a great many members now. lam sure their parents will help them." Niece Minnie Stockton, 389, New Wellfield- street (Evelyn-street School).—" Get 12 girls, plus one at the head, all nieces in your Corner, to canvass in their own ward for the Christmas Pudding Fund." Niece Emma Terry, 95, Cartwright-street (St. Barnabas' School).—" Schools should make weekly collections, and that the money collected should be taken to The Examiner' Office."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>King Alfonso of Spain. The King of Spain, like other neutral sovereigns, has had a difficult part to play in the great war. Personally, he is favourable to the Allies, but the .dwager Queen Christina, is a member of the House of Hapsburg, an Austrian, who naturally has pro-German proc- livities; and the same is no doubt true of the Infanta Don Fernando, and the Princess Beatrice of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha, who married the King's cousin. Queen Victoria Eugenie is, of course, on the side of the Allies, and with her</p>
        <p>are other members of the Spanish Royal Family, who are of French and Italian origin. Among the high officers of the Court are pro. Germans and pro-Allies, but the King has made each party understand that he is first and fore- most pro-Spanish, and there can be no question that the Spanish nation is opposed to any idea of participating in the world war.</p>
        <p>Tackling German Monopolies. The French, in common with the rest of the allied countries, are learning how to make most things manufactured before the war by the Germans, and of which the enemy has a monopoly. Clinical thermometers afford an in- stance. When hostilities started the supply ceased, and France bought of the United States, who drove a hard bargain, and charged fancy prices. The Service de Sante determined to make the thermometer &amp; in France, and as many of the .German prisWers were specialists in glass-blowing, their services proved very use- ful. As over half a million of these ther- mometers had been bought from America, there was room for the new industry, which will remain when the war is over. In this country women are now being employed in the glass trade, and it is hoped that the enemy may he driven from our markets, and that in the future Britain will supply herself with optical glass and glass instruments of all kinds, in- stead of buying them abroad as heretofore. • Women Musicians at the Halls.</p>
        <p>Mr. Oswald Stoll claims that he has opened up a new and suitable occupation for women musicians; which promises to be permanent. The performance of the ladies at the Colosseum, has so far given every satisfaction, and it is said that there is a distinct improvement in effect of the brass instruments, which are less aggressive when played by women, whilst the playing of the .string and other instrument is exceedingly good. The ladies are receiving exactly„ the same pay as the men, the leading violins earning nearly five pounds per week; the drummer, however, is the most highly paid of all the musicians forming the orchestra Women have been gradually replacing men in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>•4* 4* •:* •:. • c:. • • + 44: Children Corner. •</head>
        <p>•4* 4* •:* •:. • c:. • • + 44: Children Corner. •</p>
        <p>• ••• •:* •:* Conducted by UNCLE JACK. •</p>
        <p>SUGGESTIONS FOR HELPING CHRIST- MAS PUDDING FUND.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>I have received from you many varied and excellent suggestions for making money for the Army Christmas Pudding Fund ,which is being raised by "The Examiner." Some of the suggestions it would not be wise to carry outbecause the law forbids all to make public appeals for war charities unless they have a licende to do so. "The Examiner" has such a licence, but you have not. There is nothing; to prevent private effort being made and pass- ing the proceeds on to "The ExAminer" Office. What I want you all to do is to tell your parents about the fund and help them to send in their sixpences or shillings, just as they can afford. That is the reason I have given the prize this week to</p>
        <p>888 Niece Lizzie Lewis, 21, Samuel-street, who attends Evelyn-street School. You will see that her letter is a very good one. I should, of course, like all the teachers to co-operate, if they would, in the manner suggested by some of my young corre- spondents. _</p>
        <p>-Many nephews and nieces have expressed opinions as to the best way in which we could reward our soldiers for all they have done for us. That has made me think it would be an attractive subject for our next competition. So this week we Will have an essay on My Idea Of How Christmas Should Be Spent</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>At The Front.</head>
        <p>At The Front.</p>
        <p>. . That should bring me even mere letters than I have been receiving of late—and they have beet very numerous, I can assure you.</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>PRIZE-WINNING LETTER.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE-WINNING LETTER.</p>
        <p>'By Niece Lizzie Lewis, 21, Samuel-street; age 13; Evelyn-street School :</p>
        <p>" Dear Uncle Jack,—Last year my friend and I made some red, white and blue bows, and we went out selling them. We could make a con- cert up of our own, and get money that way, or, we could send sixpence to the Pudding 'Fund, which I saw in 'The Examiner.' I am sending sixpence for the Pudding Fund, and if every niece and nephew could do the same it would be a very great help. I go out nursing, and that is the way I earned my sixpence. We 'could save all our coppers, and buy something for the soldiers, aho are fighting for us. It will be very nice to think that our soldiers are enjoying their puddings while we are enjoying ours."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>I have had many other charming letters, from which I have extracted the following sugges- tion:—</p>
        <p>Niece Amy Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn.-street School).—" Let a day be set aside to be called "The Soldiers' Plum Pudding Day." Each school to have an exhibition of the children's work—painting, drawing, woodwork, mat and basket making, and needlework, &amp;c.; reciting And singing patriotic songs. No en- trance fee to be charged, but visitors expected to give a small contribution. Collections at all the shops, works and public-houses." Niece Nancy Adams, 27, Sanniel-street (Eve- lyn-street School).—" Inquire at - every, works and ask them to send every South Lancashire man employed by them a Christmas pudding; because, before he went to serve his King and country, he brought money in for them." Niece Emily Marshall, 183, Winwick-road, suggests an auction. . Niece Florence Hope, 45, Leicester-street.— "Make pin-cushions or mats with silk workings on them and then put them up for sale; or make up a small concert." Nephew William Watkinson, 7, Aeaton-street (Hamilton-street School).—" Flags, with the South Lancashires' cap badge or Crest in the centre."</p>
        <p>Niece Bessie Bandies, 76, Cartwright-street (Evelyn-street School), suggests a fund in the big works. Niece Eva Moores, 12, Cowdell-street '(Bea- mont School).—" Every member of our Child- ren's Corner should collect sixpence:" Niece Eva M. Wirral!, 47, Price-street.— "Nephews and nieces might DIA in their 'en- velopes a penny. or even a halfpenny and send it to you."</p>
        <p>Niece Ruth James, 30, Oxford-street, Latch- ford.—" A flag day and a concert." Niece Lily McLachlan, 8, Ash-grove, Latch- ford (Bolton Council, School).—" The top elasses of a school could make articles and sell them. An American tea is another suggestion. A jumble sale." Niece Ida Whitby, 5, Green-street (Evelyn- street Sehool).—" A grand concert; the sale of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>' 1055 Olive Beardmore, 39, Lovely-lane; age' 13., 1056 Eva Barber, 15, Pickmere-street; age 11. 1057 Harold Nevins, 46, Orford-avenue; age 12; Beamont Council School. 1058 Ernest Carter, 174, WeMeld-street; age 9; iirelyn-street School. 1059 Lily Edward, 52, Laira-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>THANKS FOR PRIZES.</head>
        <p>THANKS FOR PRIZES.</p>
        <p>I t have received the following very nice letters, one from a nephew, the other from a niece:—</p>
        <p>From Nephew Thomas Atherton, 50, Scott- street., Warrington :- "Dear Uncle Jack,—l received my prize from you yesterday just before one o'clock, and I am extremely pleased and delighted with and for it. When I saw my name in 'The Examiner' asone of the winners I could hardly believe my eyes, nor could I restrain myself from believing that it was all a dream. "I am the second boy who has won a prize in your 'Children's Corner' from Hamilton- street School, and I hope that I will not be the last one. The book is a very good one so far as I have read and is full of examples and illustrations of the trials of life. "It is a curious fact that when I was in Standard IV. at our school I won a prize, entitled 'Martin Rattler,' for the same com- petition. "It is full of adventure, and, like your 'Children's Corner,' is very educational and interesting." From Niece Lorna Reading, 26, St. Barna- bas'-place, Lovely-lane : "Dear Uncle Jack,—Many thanks for the beautiful'book whieh you sent me on Satur- day. I--have read the book and it is very interesting. It is the first prize I have ever won, and I hope it will not be the last."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>Presentation To Councillor Bro. John</p>
        <p>Chrimes, P.D.C.R.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the members of the Ancient Order of Foresters,, Court Warrington 430, held at, the Blue Lion Hotel, Market-place, on Saturday, Bro. H. Perdue, D.C.R., presiding, supported by Bro. J. Davies; S.D.C.k., and other District and Court officials, a very pleasing ceremony took place. His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Bro. Peter Peacock, P.D.C.R.), on behalf of the members, presented Bro. Councillor J. Chrimes with a gold hunter watch and a gold brooch for his wife. The inscription on the watch reads :—"Presented to Bro. Councillor John Chrimes, as a token of esteem for his services as _secre- tary for 21 yeais to Court Warrington, No. 430, Ancient Order of Foresters. October 28th, 1916." His Worship, in making the presen- tation, spoke very highly of the services Bro. Chrimes had rendered to the Court and Di,- tact.</p>
        <p>The Distriet Chief Ranger and other officials supported his Worship. • Bro. Chrimes replied, and also on behalf of his wife, thanking the members for their kindness.</p>
        <p>Bro. H. Roden, School House, Evelyn-street, Warrington, rs the newly-appointed secretary.</p>
        <p>TRe Two Lions.</p>
        <p>A lion keeper of a local Zoo applied for ex- emption on the ground that, if he were cora- pelled to serve some inexperienced substitute might leave the door open and let the lion out. The Tribunal decided to risk it and invited the keeper to join the British Lion, now 100, at the Front.—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
