      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4,1911 i</p>
        <p>THE HOPE CF PEFFAIANEtri</p>
        <p>PEACE.</p>
        <p>" Now, when victory seems to be coining into siglit," says LORD BRYCE, in a remark- ably enlightening article he recently wrote for the "Manchester Guardian" American Number, there is no reason why we'shpuld net soon discard our reserve and proceed to a dis- cussion of the means whereby the °nations of the earth may protect themselves against .a repetition of the frightful experiences through which we are passing. Our American cousins, who are not so closely 'engaged as we are by the pressing need of settling a great war, have been considering the question and have been somewhat disappointed to find that we have not been over-inclined to listen to them. We have, however, had the matter in mind, though we have been too intent upon finishing the war to be diverted by the claims of the future, and the statement by so keen an observer and so clear a thinker as Loan BRYCE is satisfactory in a double sense. It places him definitely on the side of those who regard victory as assured; and it sends a Message from us to the United States that we may soon be working in harmony with them upon a scheme to ensure the permanent peace of the world. Readers of "The Examiner" will be familiar with the idea that after the war there should be such a strong international combination with the object of curbing, by force if need be, the activities of ambitious and unscrupulous Powers, who are easily im- pelled by passion or greed to attack their neighbours. Several months ago SIR Alumni CROSFIELD foreshadowed in Warrington some such scheme, and last August, writing to "The Times" in support of the establish- ment of an Imperial Parliament to preside over the destinies of our great and rapidly expanding Empire, he said: "If the greatest world asset that can be created after the war as some set off to its appalling sacrifices and, deficit, is the guarantee of international peace among civilised nations by means of interna- tional co-operation, backed by international force, then beyond question the Imperial Parliament would represent the whole Empire in this priceless achievement." It would be unwise for us to assume that a powerful group of friendly nations can say there must be peace hereafter, and that end will be achieved. There will be opposition to the ideal of peace, and we shall need all the power we can get at our command if we are to write " Finis " to the programme of Kultur. We naturally look about for a list of the nations that would be prepared to set to work, -with- bona-fide intentions and a desire to succeed, •to create the international machinery that would be necessary to impose</p>
        <p>111111•11</p>
        <p>terms and- conditions upon potential interna- tional criminals. We may take it that all the Allies would agree to, and join in, such a benevolent combination—for tire they not peuring out their life's blood and -their treasure in unheard-of volume in the cause of freedom, civilisation and of mankind ? It would be inconceivable that any one of them should desire to 'revert to the uncivilised pre- war conceptions of the .relationships of one nation with another, and 'resume an acquies- cence in methods that allow barbarism to get so long a start- of common-sense. But out- side the belligerents stands the United States, rich and Powerful because so far she is un- bruised in the crash that has bruised so many. The United States, the -greatest of all neutrals, almost the only great nation now ranking as a neutral, would bring unrivalled influence and power to such a Tribunal. She is willing to take a hand, she is -even con- cerned that we have. not shown ..an anxiety to accept her advances and to go ahead with her at once. If that be so we on. our D'art have sometimes chafed at the. idea that the United States his stood apart from the actual fight- inc, when Freedom- has been put to hazard. We must not forget, however, that the United States and we look upon -this conflict from very different standpoints. As LORD BRYCE points out, a belligerent country Which feels the nobility and justice of its cause and lives in a state of high tension, naturally expects neutral nations to see things in the same light and to extend to it a full measure of that sympathy which it knows itself to deserve. But the people of the United States. hold that a nation is not, as a general principle, called on to take part in a war unless its own rights or interests are directly affected, and they point out that no nation has ever yet gone to war out bf pure sympathy with a righteous cause. But they supplied us. with munitions at a very critical period of our preparations for meeting the onslaught to which we are ,subjected., They have,. withstood thQ anger of the German-Americans, even on the eve of an election. They have condoned our action on the seas where We have been forced Lo use our power in a way that appeared to he harsh towards them. To appreciate what the United States has done for the general cause without actually stripping for the fight we must try to imagine the poorer position in which we should have been placed had she been as friendly in effect to Germany as she has been to us.</p>
        <p>We shall welcome with open arms the assistarieb f the United States when she comes forward to march shoulder to shoulder with is as the world's Peace-makers, ",The League to. enforce Peace" has Arcady been formulated by the American people, and We should. do well .. to examine, tentatively, its intentions. Its programme 'contemplates a treaty between the Great Powers of the world under which a judicial tribunal will be set up to hear and judge. all 'questions arising between members. All questions which can- , not be settled on principles of law and equity will be submitted to a Council of Conciliation for hearing, and a recommendation of coin- promise. If any member of the League com- mits acts of hostility against a member before these formalities-have been gone through, the remaining members of the League shall jointly use, forthwith, their economic and military forces against the member prematurely re- Sorting to war, and in favour of the member. prematurely attacked. " It will be observed," writes Ex-PRESIDENT TAFT, " that it is no the purpose of this programme to use the economic boycott or the jointly acting armies of the League to enforce the judgment de- clared on the compromise 'recommended. These means are used only to 'prevent the .be- ginning of war before there has been a com- plete submission, hearing of evidence, argu- ment and decision or recommendation. We sincerely believe that in most eases with such a delay and such a winnowing out of the issues and such an opportunity for the peoples of the different countries to understand the posi- tion of each other, war would generally not be resorted to. . . . If, therefore, after a full discussion and decision by impartial judges or a recommendation by earnest, sin- cere and equitable compromisers, a people still thinks that it must vindicate its rights by war, we do not attempt in this plan to prevent it by force." So far the Americans aim at preventing nations from going to war "prematurely," though, Da. EmoT, ex-Presi- dent of Harvard University, appears to go a step further when he declares: "War must be prevented by force exerted by a few nations which unitedly and firmly determine that there shall be no more war, and no more aggression by any nation on another, if they can prevent it." America is clearly prepar- ing, as becomes her record, to take a leading place in 'the nations Of the world not by war, but by virtue of a higher and more enduring form ,of conquest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WARRINGTON OPERA WEEK.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON OPERA WEEK.</p>
        <p>SaturdaY--'</p>
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Notices of births, marriages, and deaths, in- memoriam notices, and acknowledgments of sympathy are inserted in "The Examiner" at the following rates :—Not exceeding 20 words, 116 each; every aidlitional seven words, 6d.</p>
        <p>Deaths. - BRENNAN.—On October 29th. at S. Erwood-street, Patrick Brennan, aged 72 years.</p>
        <p>In Memoriam.</p>
        <p>HEY.—In loving memory of Harry Hey, who passed away October 30th, 1915. "At rest." His loving MOTHER and CHILDREN. 1, Boundary-street, Warrington. KENT.—In loving memory of our dearly beloved son, Private Harry Kent (Gordon Highlanders), who died of wounds in hospital November 3rd, 1915, aged 27 years. Some clay, some time, our eyes shall see The face we loved so well: Some day our hands shall clasp in his. And never bid farewell. He was not loved by one, but all, • He was forced to go when God did call; His life was short, but, oh, 'twas sweet, We hope in heaven with him to meet. Deeply mourned by his sorrowing MOTHER, FATHER; SISTER and BROTHERS. 42, Hoyle-street, Warrington TILLING.—In loving memory of Captain T. P. Tilling, lost in the Bay of Biscay, with the whole of the crew of the T.S.S. " Abhona," 7th Novem- ber, 1910. From the tempests of life and its wide heaving sea, Thou blest Rock of Ages, he's hiding in Th.. From his FATHER and MOTHER. Walton Crescent, Lower Walton.</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre</p>
        <p>Evenings 7.15. Matinee (Monday only) 2A5 Welcome Return of</p>
        <p>Tke HARRISON HECK OPERA CO.</p>
        <p>Monday Matinee—" M IRITANA." Evening—" RIGOLETIV." Tuesday—" CARMEN." Wednesday—" FAUST." Thursday—" IL TROVATORE." Friday—" CAVALLERIA RUSTICANA;" followed by " PAGLIACCI." " THE BOHEMIAN GIRL."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>Widocks !" as he approaches, there is the tone of affection.</p>
        <p>It is only a fortnight since the "Quarter" was over from France on a' ten days' furlough, And in a conversation with "The ExamiTier " man he talked for the most part of the pleasure of being home once more, and his thankfulness that after twenty-one months' Campaigning in France he still throbs with energy and has not been away from his battalion for sickness for an hour during the 'whole time. Since Janu- ary, when he was promoted, he has not been exposed- to continual danger—excepting on ex- cursions with food and kit he does not enter the front line trenches—but .previously he was sergeant of the machine gunners, and fought sternly in several engagements, and saw men falling by his side in groups. It is, no doubt, for this gallantry that he has at last been rewarded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CHRISTMAS PUDDINGS FOR "TOMMY."</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS PUDDINGS FOR "TOMMY."</p>
        <p>Would You Like To Entertain Him At Christmas?</p>
        <p>THANKS FROM HAIG.</p>
        <p>We hope our readers are of the same opinion as Alderman Tinnion, as to the Army Christ- mas Pudding Fund, for which "The Examinee is receiving donations in Warrington and dis- trict. The Alderman says he never knew of a more sensible scheme in his life. There is, he points out, no overlapping, no waste, no trouble for those at home, no fear of the pudding mis- carrying, and no fear of any man being left without his Christmas pudding on Christmas Day while his more fortunate neighbour may have several. _ _</p>
        <p>_ Under this Government scheme every soldier will have sufficient plum pudding served out to him; none will have too much. Every subscriber to the fund is entitled to and will receive a card from Sir Douglas Haig, through us, giving a drawing of "Tommy" enjoying his Christmas fare, and the message:— " On behalf of those whose Christmas will be made brighter by your kindness, I wish to</p>
        <p>thank you." On the other side will be the statement that you have become the host of one man at the Front if you have sent 6d., 25 men if you have sent 10s.. 6d., of 50 men if you have sent a guinea, and so on. The possession of that card will give you a. very pleasant and satisfied feeling for the rest of your lives. Several kind givers, have left -their money at the office and rushed away in their modesty as though they had done something wrong. Will they either call or let us know their address, so that we may send them one of these precious trench cards?</p>
        <p>Prominent among the donors this week are Mrs. Fairclough and her little boy and' girl, the wife and children of our gallant Colonel Brereton Fairclough. Mrs. Fairelough writes : ".1 hope Warrington will again rise to the occasion and find all the money necessary for the Christmas cheer foi- our own boys."</p>
        <p>A HAPPY CHRISTMAS FOR THE EDITOR. We have already sent a first instalment of -Q1 to London, and the Editor of "The Examiner" has received a trench card saying that that amount</p>
        <p>will provide Christmas Pudding for an Infantry Battalion. We are now well on our way to raising the necessary money for another Battalion. Sir Arthur Crosfield has sent a second gl is. for this second instalment, which we hope to send away early next week. Warrington is interested in at least ten battalions, so there is plentl- of scope for every- one to make himself happy by getting a card to prove that he has made a given number of Haig's splendia men happy . A message from the Army Christmas Pudding Fund says:— "We are more than grateful for the splendid way in which you are assisting us in our task of helping to feed our large family of 3,000,000 this Christmas."</p>
        <p>We are more than grateful to our readers who have enabled us to do so much; we shall be more grateful still if they will allow us to have the honour of supplying Christmas puddings for several more Battalions of our local Regiment at the Front.</p>
        <p>Send or bring donations at once to "The Examiner" Office, Bewsey-street, Warringto:l</p>
      </div>
