      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>POVERTY SLOPE.</head>
        <p>THE POVERTY SLOPE.</p>
        <p>Guild Of Help Prevents People Sliding</p>
        <p>Down.</p>
        <p>Presiding at an interesting conversazione con_ vened by the Guild of ,Help on Thursday, in •the parlour of the Cairo-street Schoolroom, Mr. C. E. Parker (chairman of the Executive) re- ferred to the obligation undertaken by . the• State through. the • Poor Law of sustaining those in dire' need. The State. he added, only recognised the obligation when the recipient. was in great necessity, when he or she was, as it. were, at the bottom. of the Poverty slope with. self-respect, strength and ability enfeebled. What the Guild sought to do was to maintain that self-respect and ability by helpful .methods to prevent any needy 'person beginning- to go down the poverty slope. (Applause.) In the course of his speech, Mr. Parker apologised for the absence of the President (the Mayor), who was engaged at another important meeting. He also referred to the loss the Guild had sustained through the remoi-al to London of the late secretary (Mr. Wilfred Rowland), and asked on behalf of Miss Ashworth, the new secretary, the co-operation and support of the workers.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charlton, the hon. travelling seereta,r7 for the League, gave an interesting and earnest address on the work of the Guild.</p>
        <p>During the evening refreshments were served and songs or pianoforte solos were' given by Mr. and Mrs. Sephton. the Misses Jarman. Snell- ing. R. Parker and Tanner. The thanks of the audience to the Chairman. Mr. Charlton and the artistes were voiced by the Rev. Irvine .Lister and Rev. Percy meson.</p>
        <p>It was a breezy .day yesterday. But, it being Friday, some of .us could not raise the wind!</p>
        <p>The Copamissioner of Police has given notice lat. the display. of fireworks on itivember sth ,s prohibited. Not so in France, ll,,,rever!</p>
        <p>The Town clerk wrote to the Board of Guardians pointing out that the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) is going to church on Sunday week with the members of the Cor- poration and inviting the Guardians to attend. Mr. Mather remarked that he would not be able to go as he had not got any long trousers. I can assure Mr. Mather that Alderman Pea- cock would eVen be pleased to see him in knee-breeches. I have seen the Lord Mayor of London in them.</p>
        <p>An irritating, rasping noise originating in a room below disturbed ,the Tribunal in the Council Chamber on Thursday. The members pursed their eyebrows in endeavouring to con- clude what it was. The Clerk (Mr. A .T. Holloway) solved the riddle with, "It's some- one filing an affidavit."</p>
        <p>Happily, the noise soon ceased, and the ,Tribunal did not have to silence the miscreant by filing him into the Army.</p>
        <p>Sales of work having been the feature of this week in Warrington, it is not unfitting to dig out of one's memory the following descriptive ebullition of a Junior Reporter: "On passing the refreshment stall in particular the smell of the good 'things 'thereon made on feel anato- mically empty!" Speaking of a very hungry man, Kipling once said, "And there was a great cavern within him."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>TheAMilitary Medal Awarded to Seven</head>
        <p>TheAMilitary Medal Awarded to Seven</p>
        <p>More Local• Men.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT SURPRISE FOR HOME FOLK.</p>
        <p>We have now official confirmation and details of our hint last week that the number of honours gained by local men in the South Lancs. Regiment was to be considerably aug- mented. The "London Gazette" on Saturday stated that the King has been pleased to award the Military Medal for bravery in the -field to the following:—</p>
        <p>1102 Sergt. J. Stout, Garsey-lane, Warrington. 2343 Sergt. F. Stephens, 34, Allen-street, War- rington. 1358 Pte. T. Littlefair, 109, Longford-street, Warrington. 2612 Pte. William Clarke, 8, Hazel-street, Warrington. 2117 Sorgt. T. Dwyer, 80, Athol-street, Ear:es- town.</p>
        <p>2119 Sergt. A. S. Lomas, 114, Haydock-street, Earlestovin.</p>
        <p>We regret to state that Sergeant Dwyer was recently killed.</p>
        <p>C.Q.M.S. WIDOCKS</p>
        <p>Has Been In France Almost From The Commencement.</p>
        <p>Pleasure is general that Company-Quarter- Master-Sergeant ErneSt J. Widocks, of Knuts- ford-road, Latchford, is included in the Honours' List, for his popularity in civil life— he was a much-nodded-to tramcar driver on the Latchford route—has suffered no diminu- tion on service. In the expression, '!Here's</p>
        <p>iiseount Privnte heir to Newton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SERGEANT • FRED STEPHENS:</head>
        <p>SERGEANT • FRED STEPHENS:</p>
        <p>Stuck To His Gun While Others Were</p>
        <p>Falling.</p>
        <p>Everyone who knows that splendid specimen of muscular manhood, Sergeant Fred Stephens, whose parents, Mr. and Mrs. William Stephens, reside at 34, Allen-street, will be delighted to learn that his prowess in the field has won him the distinction of a Military Medal. Although the plucky non-corn. was home from Manchester last week-end, he did not enter into any details as to the circumstances under which he had been brought under notice. He did not know until Saturday morning that the honour had been conferred and then, through the medium of the newspapers. He ..admitted, however, knowing that he had been recom- mended for something which happened in France on the sth September of last year. As he was a machine gunner in his section of the battalion, his plea of guilty to "sticking to our gun while others were falling" settles the point satisfactorily. It was either at the end of August or be- ginning 'of September, 1914, when volunteers were appealed for, that Fred Stephens, who stands over six feet in his stockings was eagerly snapped up by ,an admiring Army doctor, and passed into the South Lancashire Regiment. Considering his a—he will be twenty-five next February—his advance in rank is highly creditable. He was born in Warrington—Price- street—was educated at Wycliffe School. and attended St. Paul's Church and Bible Class.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT FRED STEPHENS. Military Medal.</p>
        <p>He worked at the Co-operative Stores, Sankey- street, and was another of the fine band of</p>
        <p>young fellows who went from that employment into the Army.</p>
        <p>In April last Sergeant Stephens was in- valided to England suffering from jaundice and another peculiar ailment which greatly puzzled the doctors. The nails of his fingers have dropped off and the medical men say that they wan't be replaced. But his hands are otherwise perfect, and he does not take any particular notice himself of the loss. He is now on the staff—Staff-Sergeant—at a large military con- valescent centre.</p>
        <p>Sergeant-Stephens was an all-round " sport " and keen footballer. His father, Mr. William Stephens, was in the employment of the late Dr. Adams for a long period, and his elder brother has just been called up fOr garrison duty abroad.</p>
