      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>PRIVATE THOMAS LUITLEFAIR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS LUITLEFAIR.</p>
        <p>Reputation For Good Work With</p>
        <p>Machine Gun.</p>
        <p>To )11r. and Mrs. Littlefair, of Longford- street, it was also a pleasant Surprise that their Son, Private Thomas, had been awarded the Military Medal. To Mrs. ,Littlefair• it seemed. to be too good to be true, and she hesitated to believe. A scrutiny of %he "London Gazette " announce- ment, and 'careful comparisons with his num_ her, full name, and battalion convincea her, however. "Yes, it's Tom right enough," she agreed at last. And then another idea occurred to her. "Some time two," she remarked, "they .got a story .out at ago," Bap- tist Sunday School that he had been given the D.C.M.: in fact, it was stated so from the plat- form. I didn't know anything of it, and thought</p>
        <p>it better to write to Tom. In his reply he said he thought he might be getting something as he had been recommended, but he did not want anything said about it. He is very quiet over such things as these. I have heard several people say how clever he is with a machine gun, but never himself, and it was some one else, too, who said he was the last man at his gun. That, I think, will be why he has won the medal."</p>
        <p>P,rivate Littlefair is only twenty-two years of age, and was mobilised on the outbreak of war, leaving his work as overlooker at Cockhedge Mills. He was a member of the choir at Gol- borne-street Baptist Church, and also attended the Sunday School, where they have already re- joiced over his ,bravery and honour, and will do so again now official confirmation has arrived.</p>
