      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Old Games at Raddon Court.</head>
        <p>Old Games at Raddon Court.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, All Hallow's Eve, the patients tt Raddon Court took part in some old- fashioned games customary to this festival, and much amusement was caused by the bating for apples and many other time honoured customs.</p>
        <p>Lights For Coming Week. Light up Obscure Vehicles. Lights. Saturday Sunday Monday Tuesday Wednesday , Thursday Friday Saturday Board of Trade Inquiry on Corporation Workmen's Wages. With reference to the wages of workmen in the Cleansing Department of the Corporation, the Board of Trade have appointed Mr. Stoker, K.C., as arbitrator, and an inquiry will be held at an early date.</p>
        <p>Condensed Milk at the Workhouse. In response to a letter from condensed milk manufacturers calling attention to the satis- factory resulta obtained by several institutions in the use of condensed milk, the Board of Guardians resolved on Wednesday that the Master of the Workhouse should give a trial to standard brands and report thereon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SERGEANT JAMES STOUT.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT JAMES STOUT.</p>
        <p>First Of Ills Battalion To Re-Enlist</p>
        <p>%%ten Time-Expired.</p>
        <p>Molly, his little- niece, came dancing her puppy at her heels, to the door in lane, and was asked whether it was her father who had won the Military Medal,</p>
        <p>with</p>
        <p>"Oh, no; it's Uncle Jim. Isn't it lovely ?" Her joy was ecstatic and she went skipping into the house for her mother. The puppy stayed on guard on the step. Mother arrived; a photograph was soon forth- coming, Molly continually exclaiming, "Isn't it • lovely?" She meant the portrait of • her uncle this time. Molly is her •uncle's favourite niece, and the admiration is mutual.. She is now very proud of him. Sergeant James Stout made his • home with is ,brother, Sergeant_ Joseph Stout. Both of them have been at the Front a long time, leaving the saw mills of 3Xessis. R. A. Naylor,</p>
        <p>SERGEANT JAMES STOUT.' Military Medal.</p>
        <p>Ltd., to serve. The Military .Medallist was mobilised at the commencement of the war, and was appointed signalling-sergeant, going to France in ,February of last year. "A long while since a General congratulated him on his work" remarked his sister-in-law, Mrs. Joseph Stout, in accounting for the honour which has just been conferred. He has survived the cam- paign until now without injury, and it is to be hoped that good luck will continue to attend him, for if ever there is a patriot he is the one. Time-expired in February, he could have left the Army, but he felt it his duty; to remain and, after a month's holiday at home, re-enlisted and went straight back to his comrades in the field. We understand that he was the first man in his battalion to re-enlist on his own accord. His brother, Sergeant Joseph Stout, who is in another regiment, enlisted on his own birthday.</p>
        <p>Viscount Wolmer's Post. Lord Robert Cecil has appointed</p>
        <p>Wolmer, M.P., as his Parliamentary Secretary. Viscount Wolmer, who is _the Earl of Selborne, represent s the Division in the House of Commons.</p>
