      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Rural Council's Benevolence.</head>
        <p>Rural Council's Benevolence.</p>
        <p>In the case of two employes of the Warring- ton Rural District Council who have been called up, it was decided, at Wednesday's meeting of the Council, to allow 2s. 6d. per week to each family. In one case there is a wife and three children, and in the other a wife and two children. Pr&amp;ions to joining the men were paid 255: weekly and 3s. bonus. Out-Relief Christmas Extras.</p>
        <p>The Rev, A. M. Mitchell informed the Board of Guardians on Wednesday that at the next meeting he will move that at Christmas grants of ls. and 9d. to adults and children respectively shall be made to those in receipt of out-relief. Mr. George Mather suggested that the grants might -be raised to 2s. and is., in view of the increased cost of food.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>St. Mary's AVidst Drive and Dance.</head>
        <p>St. Mary's AVidst Drive and Dance.</p>
        <p>A very successful tea party, whist drive and dance, in connection with the St. Mary's R.C. Church, was held in St. Mary's New Parish on Monday evening, over four hundred people being present. The M.C.'S for dancing were Messrs. Henry Houghton, J. Eckersley and A. Kilburn, and for whist, Messrs. T. and J. Dolan. The League of the Cross Band played for dancing from eight until two o'clock. •</p>
        <p>Guardians' " No " to Milk Contractor. The Board of Guardians, on Wednesday, approved oaf the action of the House Committee, ivho, after considering an application from Mr. A. Broome., milk contractor to the Workhouse, For an increase in the price of milk over the remaining period of his contract, recommended that the Clerk inform him that they were not prepared at the present time to make any alteration in his contract.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Adult Female Clerks:</head>
        <p>Adult Female Clerks:</p>
        <p>We specially commend to the serious con.- :ideration of ladies residing in the district, the iplendid facilities offered at Wood's College, 15, Bridge-street, in connection with short courses for rapid clerical training. Educated women )nxious to secure congenial employment "for duration of war" in one of the many first-class ,rnsiness or prefessional local houses are invited 4,0 write or call for full particulars. Apply, .'he Secretary, Wood's College, 15, Bridge- treet, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Tramway* Employ es and IVarrington</head>
        <p>Tramway* Employ es and IVarrington</p>
        <p>Infirmary.</p>
        <p>In response to the recent special appeal issued by the Working Men's Infirmary Com- mittee on behalf of this deserving institution, the sum of 24 3s. I.ld. has been subscribed by the tramway servants and forwarded by Mr. E. Plinston, secretary of the Tramways Union, to Mr. J. 11. J. Hampson, lion. secretary. In acknowledging the above sum, Mr. Hampson expresses the sincere thanks of the committee for their kind contribution. •</p>
        <p>The employes, in addition, have assisted "Our Day" loial fund (per Mr. Ruane, car shed fore- man), by sending £1 11s. 3d. to the late appeal of the British Red Cross Society:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Women Liberals.</head>
        <p>Women Liberals.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the President (Lady Crosfield) and the Committee of the Warring- tofi Women's Liberal Association and Sewing Class, the ward auxiliaries attended a• con- versazione at the John Crosfield Memorial Hall on -Monday. Over a hundred attended and the evening was spent enjoyably in whist, games and music.' Lady Crosfield wrote urging the members to fresh endeavours; and Mrs. Osborne, who spoke on the work done and to be done, said that the Sewing Class had sent over 7,200 garments of different kinds to the soldiers. The Misses Perdue and Hipkiss sang and Mrs. Pendlebury and Miss Wilkinson accompanied. Sir Arthur and Lady Crosfield Will attend the children's party, November 24th; and the Fairfield and Orford Ward social, November 27th. Other ward socials have been arranged: Howley and St. John's Ward, November 13th; St. Austin's and Whitecress. December' llth: The others will be arranged later.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM CLARKE.</p>
        <p>• One 'Who Always "Delivered The Goods."</p>
        <p>Private William Clarke has been away from the Front so long:--a matter of .sixteen months</p>
        <p>—that when our representative called at his home, 8, Hazel-street, the news came as quite a surprise to Mrs. Clarke. "I know he knows nothing about it," she said, "or else he would have mentioned it. It will be quite a pleasant shock for him. He hasn't been at the Front since June of last year, when he was wounded in the back, but I know from others that he did some fine work while he was there, but he never says so him- self." The modesty of the hero all over.</p>
        <p>The two children, home on holiday from Fair- field School, smiled happily as they pictured their father receiving this great honour. Private Clarke enlisted soon after war broke out, leaving his work as gardener and grave- digger at the Cemetery. He was attached to the transport section, and did particularly use- ful and gallant service in conveying food and requisites to the trenches amid much danger. He always "delivered the goods." It was on one of these expeditions that he was shot in the back in the early part of June of last year—just before the Hooge charge. The wound badly affected his heart, and he has been in England ever since, having been given light duty on his being discharged from hospital.</p>
        <p>Sale of Willows. Mr. Thomas Cowley's (Mawdsley) annual sale of willows will take place at eleven o'clock on Tuesday morning.</p>
