        <p>PRIVATE W. CLARKE. Military Medal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Matinee for "Our Day."</head>
        <p>Matinee for "Our Day."</p>
        <p>In aid of the "Our Day" Fund a matinee will be held at the Royal Court Theatre on Saturday, November 18th.</p>
        <p>Fewer Births; More Deaths. Last week there were 36 births, as against 40 for the corresponding week of last year, and 27 deaths, as against 22.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Sale of Work. The Latchford Baptist Church sale of work will be held in the Schoolroom on Thursday, and will be opened by Mrs. H. Roberts. Mrs. J. Fairhurst will preside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Raddcin Court Pound Day.</head>
        <p>Raddcin Court Pound Day.</p>
        <p>Pound Day will be held at the Raciilo-n .Court Red Cross -Hospital on Thursday. All gifts, especially provisions, will be gratefully te. ceived.</p>
        <p>South Lancashire Prisoners-of-War. Subscribers, helpers and others interested in the welfare of the South Lancashire prisoners- of-war are invited to attend a meeting at the Town Hall at four o'clock on Wednesday to consider what action to take to improve the conditions of the captured men.</p>
        <p>St. John's Church Anniversary. Anniversary services will be held at St. John's Presbyterian Church to-morrow. The preacher, morning and evening, will be the Rev. D. T. Davies, of Manchester. The annual congregational social will be held on Thursday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Successor to Dr. Adams.</head>
        <p>A Successor to Dr. Adams.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council, Mr. T. Antwis (chairman) presiding, the special committee appointed to consider the position of Medical Officer of Health reported that they had decided to ap- point a successor to the late Dr. Adams at a salary of &lt;2150.</p>
        <p>" Constantinopte ; Its History and Art."</p>
        <p>The Warrington Museum Commiltee announce that Mr. J. E. Phythian will deliver lantern lectures, commencing to-day (Saturday), and finishing Saturday, November 25th, in the Co- operative • Hall, Cairo-street, on " Constanti. nople : Its History and Art."</p>
        <p>British Women's Temperance Association.</p>
        <p>The annual business meeting was held on Wednesday afternoon in Bold-street Church Parlour. Mrs. Owen presided and after sing- ing and prayer the election of officers took place. Reports of the year's work were read. There was a good attendance. Gee Street Stables Sale.</p>
        <p>Mr. Herbert Johnson conducted a highly' successful sale at Gee-street stables on Thurs- day, when some excellent prices were obtained, particularly three coal lorries, bringing .218, £2O 10s. and £26 10s. respectively, and a greY mare, 35 guineas, and brown horse, 351 guineas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Roman Catholic Service.</head>
        <p>Roman Catholic Service.</p>
        <p>BY direction of the Pope, a solemn requiem High Mass is to be sung in Roman Catholic Churches throughout the country, for the souls of those who have. fallen in the war. The Churches throughout the country for the souls Mary's Roman Catholic Churcn at 11 o'clock to-morrow (Sunday) morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Emmanuel Church Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Church Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>The Emmanuel Church sale of work will be held in the Church Institute, Knutsford-road, on the 15th, 16th and 17th of November. The opening ceremony on Wednesday afternoon will be performed by Mr. Thomas Wilson; on Thursday afternoon by Mrs. James Dodds: and on Friday, at 7 p.m., by the Rev- E. T-4* Grosvenor.</p>
        <p>Housing and Town Planning Conference.</p>
        <p>At the meetin—g-of the 'Warrington Rural District Council, on Wednesday, the Chairman (Mr. W. Furness), Mr. George Mather (chair- man of the Town Planning Committee), and the Surveyor (Mr. B. S. Newcombe) were de.le; gated to attend the North-Western Count.ies Conference on Housing and - Town Plannuag, at Manchester, on the 23rd and 24th November.</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Sale • of Work at St. Mary's. _ Preparation having been going on during the last three years for St. Mary's sale of work, that event will take place on November 30th and December Ist and 2nd. The first day's proceed- ings will be opened by Lady Crosfield, and pos- sibly Lady Greenall will perform the opening ceremony on the second day. The sale will be held in the new St. Mary's Parish Hall. Mrs. Roberts's orchestra will supply the, music.</p>
        <p>Richmond's Employes Abroad. There are nearly 500 employes of the Rich- mond Gas Stove Co. fighting, on the various fronts, and the works war relief fund coni- thittee are desirous of sending them, as last year, a special Christmas, parcel. An adver- tisement in another column asks the friends and relatives of these men to assist the coot- mittee to get the parcels addre§sed correctly by' sending the correct names, ranks, regimental number and battalion to the Secretary, Mr. John Fairclough, Richmond's Works, Grappen- hell, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Foreign Missionary Anniversary. Foreign missionary; services were held at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday and Monday, the Rev. F. Hudson, late of • the Transvaal, being the preacher. Mr. T. B.Duck- worth, of Padgate, presided at Monday's meet" ing and the Rev. W. T. Derry read the annual report of the Wesleyan Missionary Society.- Mr- Sherloch, secretary of the local society, pre- sented the balance sheet, which showed that th? total . subscriptions during 1915 amounted t 9 £23 12s. Bd. This was not as large as in pre- vious years.</p>
        <p>First-Aid Lectures. In another column will be seen a nOtice of a course of lectures on first-aid. We trust that • as many as possible will avail themselves ef this opportunity of fitting themselves to help in the noble work of ministering to our wounded soldiers. The demand for nurses has been great, and will be greater, and the• is urging all who can to prepare for future emergencies. In response to the appeal for nurses, some of those who have been helping locally are :now, working abroad. This has dc- ”leted the local staff. The Commandant -of Raddon Court Hospital would be glad if those able and willing to fill the gaps would aPPIY.t° her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Threshing Machine Owner Sued.</head>
        <p>Threshing Machine Owner Sued.</p>
        <p>At the County Court on Thursday, before Honour Judge Barnard Lailey, K.C., Mr. C. IL Johnstone, of Great Sankey, sued Mr. Walter Fitter, threshing machine owner, for the re- turn of two covering sheets, or, in the alterna- tive, for damages. Mr. W. B. Forshaw, for be plaintiff, stated that as a result of a settle- ment of an action in that Court in November, 1914, defendant undertook to hand over to Plain: tiff the two covering sheets in question, bilL although plaintiff had called with his rant.:3r lorry for the sheets on several occasions, ...(!e" fendant had never handed them over. Spafford, barrister (instructed by Mr. A. J• Willett) for the defendant, raised the defence that they had been handed over to the plaintiff's man. His Honour gave judgment for the plain- tiff for £6 and costs.</p>
        <p>Discharged Soldier's Unique Generosity. . - One day last week a very pleasant evening 'was spent by the patients of Raddon Court as the result of the kind thought and generosity of a discharged soldier of the 4th South Lan"' Regiment. Having been discharged as medically' unfit, and having, after months in hospital, been able to take up business in civilian life, he gave the proceeds of his first day for the benefit of the Raddon Court patients. A little of the money was spent on prizes, at a whist drive on Friday, October 27th, and some extra refreshment, while the bulk of it is to be used for the purchase of games and probably sane easy chairs. The donor was present at the hes- pital, and made an interesting speech to the patients, who on their part gave him a hearty vote of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>DIED FOR THEIR COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Additional Local Casualties in the Various War Areas.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>PRIVATE F. LONGSHAW.</p>
        <p>Killed</p>
        <p>cousin was killed in the early stages of the war. Private Longshaw enlisted about 12 months ago, firF,t-goino• in for signalling, but his eye- sight being inefficient, he was given other work. It is a remarkable coincidence that his friend, Private A. Deeley, enlisted on the same day and was killed on the same day. Before he enlisted Private Longshaw was secretary to' the Red Cross. Society at Haddon Court. It is inter- esting to record that Private Longshaw's grand- father was in the Army at the time of the Battle of Waterloo, although not actually in the fray, arriving just as hostilities ceased..</p>
