      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>KILLED.</head>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>LCE.-SGT. T. R. RATCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>Identified By Means Of Blood-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Stained Letter.</head>
        <p>Stained Letter.</p>
        <p>Lance-Sergeant Thomas Ric hard Ratcliffe, South Lancashire Regiment, who has been missing since July 22nd, was only identified by means of a- blood-stained letter, which was found on him by a chaplain, the Rev. Arthur Longden, who, in returning it to his wife, Mrs. Ratcliffe, 44, School-brow, on October 11th, wrote: "The enclosed letter found by me was all that enabled me to identify your husband, who must have fallen some time ago in an attack on a piece of trench. I send it to you, as I know how hard it is to realise that the report of a stranger can be true. With deepest sympathy for you in your trouble." The possessor of two medals, received during the South African War, having served 28 years in the Army, Lance-Sergeant Ratcliffe re- oenlisted on the outbreak of war, then being in the employ of the Whitecross Co., Ltd. He was 41 years of age and was educated at Heath- tide School.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. A. GLARE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Sergeant Speaks_ in High Terms</head>
        <p>Sergeant Speaks_ in High Terms</p>
        <p>Of Him.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Clare, 19, Back Crossley-street, has re- ceived official news that her husband, Lance- Corporal Arthur Clare, South Lancashire Regi- ment; fell in action on October Ist. She has also received a letter from his sergeant, who speaks in high terms of .him. Lance-Corporal Clare enlisted two years last September and, after serving for five Months in</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL EDWARD GRAINGER. Killed.</p>
        <p>was killed in action on the 22nd inst. He was a splendid soldier and a thoroughly reliable N.C.°. As his. -platoon commander, I Can assure you that , I always placed the greatest trust in him, convinced that my confidence was not misplaced. His death came as a great shock :to me, and also to his platoon, with whom he was always a great favourite. It will be a con- solation fto you .to know that his death was instant, and he suffered no pain. May I offer ,you my heartfelt sympathy in this awful bereavement which you have suffered? Be died -a soldier's 'death, serving nobly his King and --country, .for whom he has made- the supreme :sacrifice."</p>
        <p>Corporal Walmsley wrote:—" I:.e was my best ,chum. .We have been in the same section and fought together ever since we came out here. I :shall miss him very much., so will all the other boys, as he was a favourite ,amongst us all." Lance-Corporal Grainger, who was twenty-six Years of age, was educated at St: James' School, ~and was formerly employed as a plasterer by Mr. Jos. Oxley, Stafford-road. -. As a pigeon fancier he was well-known, having flown birds in the Bath and French races. He won a first _prize in the former race, and was also . the Possessor of a diploma. He was a member of the Latchford Homing Society. He enlisted about twenty-two months ago, and went to the 'Front -twelve months last July. Two younger brothers are serving in the Army. Fred in the litiyal Field Artillery, and Harold in the South lanes. Regiment. ,</p>
