      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>LIEUTENANT P. B. SILCOCK.</p>
        <p>One Of Several Wounded When Near</p>
        <p>Enemy's Wire.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. S. P. Silcock have received in- formation from the War rlffice that their son Bryan was wounded on the 26th October. A letter from his commanding officer states that Second-Lieutenant P. B. Silcock was on patrol duty with a few men, and when close to the enemy's wire defences was bombed from their line. Several of his party, too, were wounded. He got back to our•own lines with some difficulty. He has several wounds in the aims, legs and chest, none of which the doctor hopes is very serious. Second-Lieutenant P. B. Silcock joined the Army in March, 1915 (Royal Fusiliers U.P.S.), and was sent to France in November, where he served about five months. He then came home and joined an Officers' Training Corps, obtain-</p>
        <p>LIEUT. P. B. SILCOCK. Wounded.</p>
        <p>ing a commission in August of, the present year, and being gazetted to the Cheshires. A fort- night after obtaining his commission, he was again sent out to France, and was wounded some six weeks after his arrival at the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PRIVATE J. W. FARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. W. FARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>In Base Hospital Wounded In The Left Lell.</p>
        <p>Lying in a Canadian Base Hospital, wounded in the left leg, is Private John William Far- rington, South Lancashire Regiment, and son of Mr. and Mrs. P. Farrington, 83, Pierpoint- street. He received his wounds on October Ist. Private Farrington, who is 19 years of age, and formerly worked for the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., received his education at St, Alban's School. Enlisting eighteen months ago, he had been in France a month when he was hit. James, his younger brother, joined the Sherwood Foresters, but has been discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>RIFLEMAN H. CHILTON.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN H. CHILTON.</p>
        <p>Five Brothers Serving: One Wounded, Another Missing. Mr. and Mrs. H. Chilton, 7, Rodney-street, have received news that one of their five soldier-</p>
        <p>sons, Rifleman Henry Chilton, of the Rifle Brigade, has been wounded in the left thigh. In the latest letter from hospital he says he is "getting on fine." Rifleman Chilton, who is twenty-two years of age, enlisted in September, 1914, and has been at the Front for sixteen months. He was educated at Heathside School, and was formerly a moulder for Messrs. Richmond and Co. His soldier-brothers are Ernest, King's Own Yorkshires, who served six years in India, was transferred to France at the outbreak of the present war, and was reported missing about six months ago-, Samuel, of the London Irish Rifles; William, Loyal North Lancs.; and Robt. William, Rifle• Brigade.</p>
        <p>„—,.„,•-sofei:::' ..,...,,i:;.,,,5,4iii44:u1tix*•!:7•:..: ' „4.,,,N041.t0g1itt1.t0,11t,t!'•.:v.:::: • • - .k,„--0,041164):144.**400.;(1- if 7•,••:•intitifts2:-. :, ... '''f 'AVigniiii,-**-kigirt:: .., :-./.• : :...-•:••••%*t i:?.;?.:z94.,itiar- ' * .tk-,•41:„. , -:4k1,•:4:-.; 1,••••Nf. ~--„:‘,:-%..,,,,n...,i,„0„.,..,:w , ~ „-:,.::•,. , ••:*::::::,..h:-.---.,,--• --..,E...5-, g':',.&gt;••ni§o-:-1,-- •- • ••• •-ii•-„•-••44m:•• 4 - :r.t.,-,4::, --......,K: tp..4:.•.7:*: ~, , •• * 4 1:::••• .. '..4.tatosZ:::::i'•4..&amp;.:`:'•;,:?:ik, .' . . .....••:t:-.-...:•••••&lt;:stkiwz,,4E/5;•:.-::;.. ‘,5•.;:.'m.,,/:::Akigets-•%.,, .. a..45,,5?".3,...• -,:!:•-•.::*:r4,4ii-,::::',.-•'. .-,,,:fti4awk::*pc,...i6A. • ~.., .... ks::?::••::k i&amp;k:' hokviglaixesi: :n.z. -,:i§N.-Nzuß.:l*:::43:l4: • ' ' &lt;: ~',, ',N•V::•:: :, *::, :.': ..,..* .::i:•:s„'iba.:*:'•.l;:Niiilta,l:• • .f., , ' ,:s.44:4*:::*i. • :: .•&amp; ...;:?- '&gt;&gt;Z%%V:A.' I'A:W.:Pe. ~ 4:f:: ' , • .ft*:'‘f•-44?;•::::•.:7 :.: ip.454.4pi•:?::•4,.:(:m.,•44:15 i';4s:, ~..' ,:tke!!-2a..•5:s :'.: iis,4444::iiz,::::.4:Wilti::fts • :17:.5.:‘4.:::.:::•'... , &amp;-: 4.%t•Nci,:it:§,,,s, 5,..„.•,.,..„,:,:, • ',' ••.t...,:i.,:r::,:,inf::;';:: :-,::1* v, ,ti,..44.,,,,1„..!.....,,,..:.,..,:„.:,...44„:.,.,3ii..:....f.kii4.,....4.....-..,.....,..„...i :...-:, 55t.,...,51/4k ,k,.....-,waw...,. Z.14.14R.:"..;;;,.;0:.ti5.'. ; V:kZ • ~.i• . \ ' t ••,,\s, '‘'' ' .0” ' .'s• -•'•Is ;•, t ..'::)::::;;•:„,;;i::§4i..; :,:i) 1 etai --cp ' - ~.;,, ~.•:-:%::.',,,m7A..::,;:NN,-.: . .4..::i.„.......: ...,...... ... „N..... ,;:::- • -....x....;,:.,,...-.44..:.&amp; 5t.45104.; ;-, 74s ~',.01kt;4.- • s , -:.,-..›-..:,4'44:05-: .- . .. ~.. . ... , 44.4 ,5„,,...,....</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN H. cimiroN. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PRIVATE T. FLYNN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE T. FLYNN.</p>
        <p>Officially Reported Wounded, But No</p>
        <p>Further News.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Hickling, 16, Carlisle-street, has been officially informed that her son, Private Thos. Flynn, South Lancashire Regiment, was re- ported wounded on August 28th. She has heard nothing further from any source, her son's last letter having been written so long ago as August 17th. In that letter he said he was getting used to being in the trenches.</p>
        <p>- Private Flynn, who is 20 years of age, en- listed in August Of last year, and crossed the water to France six weeks before he was wounded. He was educated at King-street School, Warrington, and then at St. Thomas' School, Preston. He formerly worked for the late Mr. Parkinson, farmer, Manchester-road, but later was in the employ of the Whitecross Co., ;Ltd. An uncle, Corporal John Peel, has been wounded, but is now back again serving with his regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS.</head>
        <p>PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS.</p>
        <p>'SERGEANT S. W. BOAST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Given Commission For Service In The</head>
        <p>Given Commission For Service In The</p>
        <p>Field.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will be pleased to hear of the promotion of Sergeant S. W. Boast, of the South Lancashire Regiment, to a commis- sion for service in the field.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant S. W. Boast is the eldest son of Captain Sydney T. Boast; M.C.' and a nephew of Captain J. S. Boast, M.C., both of whom are well-known in Warrington. He was a pupil at the Church-street School, and later at Beamont School, and was for several years a member of the Parish Church Choir. He was born in the regiment in which he now holds a commission, and enlisted as a boy into his present battalion. He proceeded on service as a corporal with the. original Expeditionary Force, was wounded in the first battle of Ypres, and invalided home. He rejoined after recover- ing from his wounds, and has served with his battalion ever since, attaining the rank of ser- geant some months ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Whitecross Military Hospital was the scene of happiness and revelry on ThursdaY evening, when a concert was given, arranged by Mr.. W. 11. Payton, for, the entertainment of the patients quartered there. The programme in- cluded songs by Miss Ida Mather, Miss Elsie Hewitt., Madame Jennie Armstrong, Mr. Fred Fieldhouse, and Mr. Joseph Poole, all of which were excellently rendered, perhaps the Most appreciated one being ." Boys of the Old Bri- gade," by Mr. Fred Fieldhouse. An old song cycle, "Flora's Holiday," by H. Lane Wilson, was well sung by Miss Elsie Hewitt, Madame Jennie Armstrong, Mr. Joseph Poole,- and Mr. .Fred Fieldhouse. Mrs. H. Haviour gave tl*vo .violin solos, and Miss Winifred Corney two recitals. A quartet, "Excelsior," was given in addition.</p>
        <p>A vote of thankn was proposed by Company- Sergeant-Major Blakely, and Mr. Payton suit, ably replied.</p>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly concert arranged by Mr. W. B. Randall ' wits given by Mrs. Dolan (soprano), Miss Lillian Davey (contralto), Mr. Caldwell ,(tenor),. Mr. Brobbin (concertina soloist), Mrs. Wolfenden Harrison (monologues) and Mr. Harrison (Hebrew humorist). The artistes, who did justice to their items, were well received by the soldiers, and a vote ..of thanks was passed to them. Private Markham and Mr. W. B. Randall ably accompanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>"Our Day" Fund.</head>
        <p>"Our Day" Fund.</p>
        <p>- In our advertisement columns we publish a list of donations received towards "thir Day" Fund. The Hon. Treasurer (Mr. F. Joynson) 7, Museum-street, is greatly pleased with the results so far attained,' but other Zonations have been promised, which will help to swell the total.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Dutton Military Hospital.</head>
        <p>Dutton Military Hospital.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians it was stated that the officer in charge had reported to the Military Hospital Committee that the chairs supplied were not according to sample. It was resolved that the Clerk refer the matter to the Military Authorities and make the best arrangements possible. A suggestion of the Chaplain was adopted that instead of having a soldiers' service on Sundays, the service to he held on Wednesdays at 5.45 p.m. It was agreed that extra dietary be provided for the officer in charge and the matron.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PRIVATE A. DEELEY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE A. DEELEY.</p>
        <p>Friend Of La...r.e Frod Longshav4 k:lned On Same Day.</p>
        <p>A bosom friend of Private Fred Longshaw, whose death in action we report elsewhere, Private Arthur Deeley, of the King's (Liver-, pool) Regiment, son of Mrs. Deeley„ a widow, 83, Longford-street, was himself killed on the same day, October 12th. It was a melon- coincidence, for they enlisted the same</p>
        <p>day,.alang with several . other friends, .and fought in Prance together. In civil life they were both engaged in . the Offices of Messrs. Richmond and Co., Academy-street.</p>
        <p>It is about a fortnight since the news was first received in Warrington, Mr. Pollitt, a Richmond's employe, receiving a letter dated October 19th from a Warrington man named Pearson, of a Liverpool Battalion. "I have met a fellow in hospital," he wrote, "und he asked me ,to write and tell you about -Arthur Deeley's death. A shell killed, him and four more a week yesterday. Arthur. Deeley asked me to let his people know if anything happened to him."</p>
        <p>-Since the receipt of this letter official news has come to hand.</p>
        <p>_ Of a bright and 'genial disposition, Private Deeley had made a host of friends, by whom he will be much missed. Twenty-eight years of age, he enlisted on October 26th of last year, and had been at the Front eight months. An "old boy" of the Hamilton-street and Secondary Schools, he was for cight , years in the office of ,Mr. H. B. white, magistrates' clerk, Egypt-street, be- fore going to Richmond's. A younger brother, Frank Deeley, served for seven years in the A.S.C, and was at the. Front for ten months before being discharged as a time-expired man. He has since received a notice to report him- self . for .home service. Another younger bro- ther, Sapper Albert Deeley, of the Royal Engineers, has been in France almost two years.</p>
