        <p>PRIVATE ARTHUR DEELEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PRISONER-OF-WAR.</head>
        <p>PRISONER-OF-WAR.</p>
        <p>Captured By Germans In 1914 ;</p>
        <p>Now In Switzerland.</p>
        <p>Private Win. Johnstone, of the Second' South Lancashire; Regiment, who has served part of his time in India, but has , been stationed at Orford, was captured in August, DK,. in the retreat &amp;mil Mons. In answer to an announce- ment that he, along with others, was starving in Germany, . Miss 'Rose Coulson,- 78, GOrsey- lane, "adopted" him and sent him parcels of necessaries for nearly two years. -Six Months ago 'being ill, he was one of the prisoners to be transferred to Switzerland, and he has now been in that comparatively happy' country for about ix months. From Murren he writes •to Miss Coulson to shy that he now receives all the com- forts • he requires—except cigarettes, and these are being sent out to him. We reproduce one of several photographs taken in Switzerland</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHNSTONE. (on the left) and a fellow prisoner.</p>
        <p>that Private Johnstone has forwarded to Miss Coniston, to whom he is not known.</p>
        <p>MISSiNG.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE F. J. CARLISLE.</p>
        <p>News Of AN'hereabouts Would Be Welcrinied.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Carlisle, " Kenilworth," Grappenhall, has. received an official. intimation that her eldest son, Private Frederick John Carlisle, Cheshire Regiment, has been missing 'Since October 9th. His mother will be glad to hear anything concerning hi!; whereabouts. Returning from Australia in October of last year, Private Carlisle enlisted on April 3rd, of</p>
        <p>this year, and crossed to France the second week in September. 7 His youngest brother, Walter, was slightly wounded on October 12th, but has now rejoined his regiment. Another brother, Private Frank Carlisle, Canadian Con- tingent; was killed on June 14th of this year, as was also a nephew, Private. James Carlisle, R.A.M.C.</p>
        <p>;E:7 • .; ,•••••. •*. : ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RIFLEMAN W. CAIN.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN W. CAIN.</p>
        <p>Left Leg Amputated)_" Other Wounds</p>
        <p>Are Nothing."</p>
        <p>Rifleman William Cain, Rifle Brigade, and son of Mr. and Mrs. Cain, 44; Jockey-street, who was wounded on October 7th, has written a letter, dated October 21st, from a French hospital, where he is now lying,'saying : "You seem to be worrying yourself now that you have heard of it all, but I tell you not to worry. Cheer up, for I am getting on all, right. My left leg has been taken off now over a week, and I have got over the worst of it, and I don't suppose it will .be very long before I am in England. My left arm and hand are only wounded slightly. I got a piece of shrap- nel in the elbow, but it. was very small. I expect these wounds to be quite better in, a fortnight or so. As .regards my right leg, there is nothing at all the matter with it now.. There was only a little cut on the knee, which</p>
        <p>PRIVATE WM. CAIN Wounded.</p>
        <p>healed • up In- a few days. • There is only one thing bad, and that is the losing of my left leg, but When I think how lucky I have been to come out alive „I cannot grumble and am looking .on the best 'side of things. • Enlisting two .years 'last September, Tillman Cain was drafted to the Front about fourteen months ago. • He was formerly employed by Mr. Tennant, brickmaker, and received - his education at :St. Benedict's School. He is 21 years of age, and his brother, • Private Francis Cain,. was killed on the 10th March of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>RIFLEMAN M. SHAUGHNESSY.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN M. SHAUGHNESSY.</p>
        <p>"Old Boy" Of Si.. Mary's School Reported Wounded. Rifleman Martin Shaughnessy, the Rifle Bri- gade, who resides with his mother at 12, Car-</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>lisle-street, was wounded on September 3rd. No additional news bas come to hand from any source.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Shaughnessy, who is an old employe of the Firth Co., Ltd., enlisted about two years ago and had been in France sixteen months. Hels 19 years of age and an "old boy" of St. Mary's E.G. School.</p>
