      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>PROCESSION OF 41's.</head>
        <p>PROCESSION OF 41's.</p>
        <p>AGE-LIMIT MEN EXEMPTED.</p>
        <p>"Tip " To Solicitors And</p>
        <p>Doctors.</p>
        <p>About twenty attested men filed into the Council Chamber at the Town hall on Thurs- day morning, and, producing for the inspection of the Triburtil birth certicates showing that they had attained the age of 41 years before June 24th, were exempted, the object of the• Tribunal being to place thew on the same footing as unattested men of tho same age. An interesting and important declaration was made during a cAse in which an appli- cant appealed through Mr. T. S.. Steel, soli- citor. A statement as to the turnover of the business was handed in by the solicitor, and, no proof accompanying it, tile • case was ad- journed for the production, of the: books. The Chairman (Dr. Joseph) remarked that proof should always be brought of statements made.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: Of course, whatever statement I make is on the instructions of my'crient. The Chairman: That does not rid you of responsibility. The Clerk (Mr. A. T. Hallaway) : In this sense: that 'solicitors ought. always to make sure that their clients are on sure ground when instructing them. They should. always insist on proof, and that is what we want in this case. Solicitors generally might note that point, he added. The doctors were also given a "tip." In a case where •a married applicant produced a doctor's certificate regarding his mother-in- law, whom he also lad to support, it was merely declared that she was " sUffering from illness." The Chairman: This certificate is not worth anything at all. It simply says- "suffering from illness." It has no value as a document.</p>
        <p>The Applicant: The doctor will give you full details.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: At present he has them locked up in his mind. The full details should be given here and now, not in the' future. Your case is being discussed now. Applicant: She has been an invalid for sev- eral years. The case was adjourned in order that fur- ther medical evidence may be forthcoming. War Against the Single.</p>
        <p>A formidable objection was submitted by a married man, a joiner, aged 35, with three children. "I don't think it right that I should go when single men are left in the shop,". he said. ." It isn't fair play to me." The applicant was asked to enumerate who the single men were, and, of the four he named, was told that three of them were going. "Does that satisfy you?" inquired the Clerk. Applicant: No. There is a conscientious ob- jector, who is a clerk, and has got a badge and certificate.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: If he is badged we cannot help that.</p>
        <p>The applicant added that his complaint was a general one regarding the calling up of mar rid men before all single ones had gone. HI did not want it to be thought that he just meani the place at which he was employed. He men tioned that he went through the Boer War 'and suffered greatly, having dysentery twice, enteric and pneumonia, but then he was even prepared to die for his country. "When the single men have gone," he declared, "I will go willingly I'm not afraid of the Germans." Alderman Tinnion remarked that the Tri- bunal probably thought that single men should go before married, but they could not help it.</p>
        <p>- The Chairman: That is a matter for the Legis- lative; and ,not this, Tribunal, but we sympa- thise with you to a large extent. . Adjourned for a week for medical examina- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Among the C2's:</head>
        <p>Among the C2's:</p>
        <p>A business appeal having been ,negatived, Mr. T. S. Steel submitted one on domestic grounds for a client. ,The wife had died shortly after a confinement in October of last year and he was left with the child then born and another aged eight years. - He Was a mPmber Of the V.T.C. and Volunteer Fire Brigade. Case adjourned until C2's are called up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>DiSIVIAL• FIND.</head>
        <p>A DiSIVIAL• FIND.</p>
        <p>Sad Death Of Walton Farmer's</p>
        <p>Son.</p>
        <p>In the half-misty light of Wednesday morn- ing, shortly before six o'clock, Joseph Dean, teamsman, employed at the Walton Arms Hotel, Walton, was crossing the yard beside the building when he saw something lying close to the wall. Yesterday,, before the Coroner, he said: I went up and spoke to it, but it did not answer. It was a dead body, and I then recognised it as that of Peter</p>
        <p>Peter Hall was the on of Mr. Peter Hall, Holly Hedge Farm, Acton Grange, and was well-known in the Stockton Heath and Walton district_</p>
        <p>Mr. E. A. White (coroner) held an inquest yesterday at the Walton Arms, when Superin- tendent Owen and Inspector Breese represented the police. Mr. T. S.. Steel appeared for Mr. Fred Broady, licensee of the Walton Arms. Mr. Peter Hall, Holly Hedge Farm, Acton Grange, identified the body as that, of his son, Peter Hall,. aged 33 years, threshing machine driver. He last saw him alive .between half- past nine and ten o'clock, when he was going to work. His son had pneumonia about last Christmas, - and was again attended for the same complaint by Dr. Joseph, Warrington, a fortnight ago. Mrs. Ada Duncalfe, Acton Grange, who knew deceased, said she last saw him alive on Tues- day night about 7.30 o'clock. He was sitting in the taproom of the Walton Arms Hotel. He had his head on the table and seemed to be the worse for drink. He asked Mr. Fred Broady, the proprietor, for a bottle of beer and was refused. He was refused three times alto- gether. Hall said: I can go somewhere else wherc? I can get it." He asked witness to get him some beer. She tried to do so, but was also refused. Hall went out about 8.15 o'clock. The Coroner: Why did you try to get some li2er for him if you saw that he was themorse for drink?—l thought he wanted to take it ham 2.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: What time did you leave the house ?--Five minutes after Mr. Hall left.</p>
        <p>Did Mr. Broady tell you not to come any of your tricks there?—No. , Did he say you knew the drink was for Mr. HallF—No; he refused to give it. Strange In His Manner.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fred Broady, licensee of the Walton Arms, stated that he knew deceased Well and last saw him alive about ten minutes past eight o'clock on Tuesday night in the taproom. He had then been in the house about twenty min- utes. When he came in he ordered a pint of beer, which was served He• had nothing else. He asked for a pint of *best beer in a bottle- to bring 'away, but witness refused him. Mr. Hall, sen.,' had been down to see witness on Tuesday at noon and told him his son was not working as he was on the sick list. The Coroner: You refused 'to "serve him be- cause he was ill?—Yes. .</p>
        <p>Was he sober?—He was sober, but he lboked rathr strange in his manner. Mr. Steel: 'Deceased was quite sober when he left the house?—He was.</p>
        <p>Joseph Dean then described how he found deceased lying close to a convenience beside the premises. -Witness informed Mr. Broady and afterwards the police. Constable Frank Asbury, stationed at Moore, said he saw the body lying in the manner de- scribed by the last witness at 10.15 on the Ist November. It was quite stiff and cold• There were two cuts on the left side of the forehead and no other signs of injury. In the pockets he found is. 3d. in cash, a metal watch and chain, and a military exemption certificate. Hall's bicycle was reared up by the porch wall. about eight yards from where the body was found. The bicycle lamps were burned out and there was no oil in them.</p>
        <p>By Superintendent Owen: The cuts might have been caused by deceased running into the wall or by a fall.</p>
        <p>By Mr. 'Steel: It was a very cold, dark night. Dr. Binns, of Stockton Heath, said that death was due to pleurisy and pneumonia. Mr. Steel produced a witness who stated that he went out to the convenience at 9.25 on that night and the body was not then on the ground. The Jury found a verdict in accordance with the medical testimony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>STAGE AND KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre.</p>
        <p>Like Adam in the Garden of Eden, dominat- ing the revue, " presented by Mr. J. W. Jacksou at the Royal Court Theatre this week, is that -popular .and old-time favourite comedian, Mr. Albert Brune. he is omni- present, but always changing his clothes if not hid nature. In the five scenes he appears in as many disguises. In the sixth scene—that be- tween the "houses "—on Monday night he ' appeared in another guise, that of himself, with " Foyer " as his audience. Off the stage and off with his wig, there is exposed oiled and retreating black hair as refined as that of Dennis Eadie's or Sir Edward Carson's, and tall as interesting 'and informa- tive as may be expected of one of his exten- sive experience. Mr. Bruno has been in Warrington several times previously, including a visit years and years ago with Frederick Maxwell, to the Theatre Royal, in Scotland-road. lie. scored his later successes in musical comedy. He also exercised himself in Drury Lane dramas, tour- ing in "Cheer, Boys, Cheer !" 'Life of Pleasure," "Run of Luck," and "The Derby Winner."</p>
        <p>Until two or tfir.,e years ago he was "starred" by Fred Karno for half a dozen: years, figoring very successfully in "Moses tin&amp; Son," "The Bailiff," " G.P.0.," " Perkins,. M.P.,") " Juatiee Perkins," and "Perkins in Paris.'. These last.. 4 named almost approached revues in style and, therefore, when Mr. Karno walked: into, the revue proper it was. an easy transition.</p>
        <p>With: Mr. Bruno in "Kisses" sit several.. of the chorus girls who disported themselves admiringly around him in previous productions, as well as other loyal friends in Mr. Sam New- berry, the stage mana.ter. am. Mr. Tom E. Conover, who partners him in patter.. "It is nice having these people with one," commented Mr. Bruno. "One gets better comedy because they understand one better than strange people. do."</p>
        <p>One ought to congratulate Miss Maree Olives and Mr. Ernest Hargreaves on their remark- ably ,fine singing;. therefore, one does. Mr. Harry Warburton, manager of the Royer Court Theatre, received this striking telegram on Thursday, from his friend, the manager of the Theatre s Royal, Glasgow, where the Harrison-Frewin Opera Co. is appearing this week: "Doing grandly. Go on 'boosting " Frewin Opera Co. productions. Business Ex- cellent." That speaks for itself, and promises great things for Warrington. Indeed, we are</p>
        <p>extremely fortunate in - having another visit from this grand operatic company, and next week 'the theatre should be crowded every night. For such a high-class entertainment the prices are remarkably low. The management are to be congratulated on next week's arrange- ments. The productions will be of a gorgeous character, and the scenery • being of the best in the midst of it will flourish a chorus of fifty The orchestra is to be greatly augmented, over twenty members having been engaged. Among the star performers are some of the greatest operatic singers of the present time, in- cluding Miss Nara D'Arg,el, the New Zealand soprano; Misses Raymonde Amy, Marie Lewis, and Ella Rees, all noted sopranos. The prin- cipal contraltos are Miss Christine Oliver, the famous Belgian, who was with the Carl Rosa Opera Co. last' year; and Miss Ethel Yunson, whose successes. on the oec.asiop. of the lest visit are 1--:•:2enl'oel:ed wit!' tb., greatest pleasure. The lisf of gentle7nen is ben,.'(l by rrabahr the thi- te7107, • )7,,, John l'larri,H t!' here last time), and Furness Williams. The famous Welsh baritone, Mr. Lewys James (of whom enconiums were general before), the American baritone, Mr. Robert Parker; .and Mr. Tom Griffiths, will also appcat. Truly, a galaxy of talent!</p>
