      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Palace wid Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Palace wid Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>A decidedly itne-6 enbrtain:licut is that given by Alberta fiahey, L.R.A.1v1.., anti- her tenor, - Alexander Webster, A.It.A.M., who is also an excellent pianist. The lady is possessed of exceptional skill and taste, and her violin pieces are rendered with admirable tone and delicacy. Their performance is much appreciated. . We do not know if the same piano figures in the show given •by • the "Three Scamps," American "Speed Boys "—at any rate, it bears the same name of a well-known Warrington firm. If it is it must be not only a delicate piece of mechanism, but also able to stand a vast Rmour:t of hurricane handlirg. In this notable ptnlcrmance one yc-ung .ulan pummels the piano in the -i•i;r all he is worth, while the other two, with trans-Atlantic elegance, sit on the lid—probably to keep the instrument from running away. _ • _ _ _</p>
        <p>Giordano, the description of whose wonderful gifts as an illusionist, spiritualist, hypnotist, &amp;c., occupy no less than ten lines on the printed programme, certainly performed some very clever conjuring tricks ,and the illusion of "La Senorita invisible" is contrived in • a most baffling manner. A right jovial turn is given by Fred Colley and Minnie Scott, while Jock Mills, Scottish humorist and raconteur, had a good reception. Myra Norman; contralto vocalist, and Norman Field, in ,"Frock, Frills and Music," intro- duce a clever turn.</p>
        <p>'Judging from the announcements we are to have n first-class variety show next week. The Courtiers will appear in an original musical comedy scena, and Stanelli and Carrodns pro- mise a combination of rag-time ,dancing and violin playing. Shirley and Ransome 'Will pre- sent an original "episode ", entitled "The Separation Order." A big draw is the Croisit Trio, whose wonderful acrobatic turn is of an extremely novel character. Lean Lynn, ladder expert; George E. Carnell, comedian; and the 12 Rainbows, are also included in the pro- gramme. The Kinemas.</p>
        <p>"'Audrey," a fine picture, featuring Pauline Frederick, now holds sway at the Star Kinema House, Church-street. Next week we are to have " Diplomacy " during the first half of the week, and "A Woman's Law" during the latter half. At the Grand Electric Theatre, Bridge Foot, "Her Great Hour" is attracting crowded houses, and next week there will be shown "Bought," and the twenty-third episode of the "Diamond From the Sky" during the first half of the week, and "Her Great Price" and the sixth episode of "Peg o' the Ring" during the latter half.</p>
        <p>"The Night Birds of London" is the title of a fine film now being screened at the Pavilion, Sankey Green, where next week we are to have "Helen's Babies" and the twenty- 'third episode of the "Diamond From the Sky'' during the first half of the week, and "Abide, With Me" and the fifth episode of "Peg a* the Ring" during the latter half.</p>
