      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ECONOMY AND VARIETY.</head>
        <p>ECONOMY AND VARIETY.</p>
        <p>Can orkliouses Depart Advantage- oiNl3 From Official Dietary ?</p>
        <p>The question of variety and economy in the diet of sojourners in Workhouses Wiis touched upon at Tuesday's meeting of Runcorn Board of Guardians—Mr. John L. Jones, vice-chair- man, presiding. It arose on a resolution for- warded by the Hemsworth Union asking the Local Government Board to allow Boards of Guardians in framing dietary tables to depart from the specified quantities set out in the regu- lations relating to dietaries, provided the total cost of the food supplied is not more than th..?. cost would have been if the quantities specified had been adhered to. They considered that if this departure were allowed they would be able to increase, say the quantity of butter for each inmate, and use a less quantity of some other article of food.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce, in proposing the adoption of this suggestion, said that their experience with re- gard to the Children's Home would justify them in passing the resolution. A lady inspector visited the home and made certain recommenda- tions, and when the committee went into the question they found that if her recommenda- tions were adopted there would be a saving to the Board, and that the dietary she suggeSted would be more acceptable to the children. A varied and yet not more costly dietary might be adopted. The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>RURAL GENEROSITY'.</head>
        <p>RURAL GENEROSITY'.</p>
        <p>National Relief And Belgian Relief Funds Benefit.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Furness (chairman of the -Warring ton Rural District Council) announced at Wed- nesday's meeting that the amount collected in the district for the Prince of Wales' Fund totalled to date £751 4s. 11d.; .086 16s. 9d. had been paid over. leaving a balance in hand of ,R 364 Bs. 2d. There had been £lOO more in hand since they reported at the last meeting, principally subscribed by the.Orforct-Tannery. It was decided to send another instalment of £250.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness also stated that £l2l 12s. sd. had been subscribed to the Belgian Relief Fund, and of this ,Rll9 had been paid out to the Central Fund, leaving £2 12s. sd. in hand.</p>
        <p>British Ingenuity. A Willesden boy of fourteen, arrested' by the police was found to have in his possession a pack of cards, every one of which could be identified from the back by pin-pricks. This should interest the pessimists who complain that we have lost the power to compete with the German in patient commercial enterprise.— "Punch."</p>
