      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1916.</p>
        <p>TRICT ilw3</p>
        <p>LAY MM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>The Lymm Tribunal is now having its busy season. Last Friday night the members were sitting from 4.45 p.m. until 10.20 p.m.</p>
        <p>And hearing cases all the time, most of which had to be left unsettled.</p>
        <p>If applicants would only endeavour to go be- fore the Medical Board before they go to the Tribunals they would save both themselves and the Tribunal a lot of valuable time.</p>
        <p>If the substitution scheme is going to prove a success it will be necessary for all applicants to be medically examined.</p>
        <p>Take my advice and do it. It pays in the end.</p>
        <p>The ranks of the British Army are now com- prised of men in all stations of life.</p>
        <p>Here is an amusing story sent by a local soldier to his father in Bridgewater-street.</p>
        <p>He is corporal in charge of a hut, and one morning the Pay Sergeant sent for one of his men.</p>
        <p>On arriving at the Pay Sergeant's hut he was informed that he bad not made his wife an allotment.</p>
        <p>He replied that he was quite aware of it, and had no intention of doing it, but the Sergeant responded that he would have to. .</p>
        <p>"Who says so ?" said the man. "The law says so," responded the Sergeant.</p>
        <p>"The law doesn't," said the man. "What do you know about the law?" furiously retorted the Sergeant. "Nothing," meekly 'replied the man.</p>
        <p>"What were you in private life before you joined up :-" asked the Sergeant. "Only a barrister-at-law" replied the man.</p>
        <p>The Sergeant, after thoughtful pause, told him that he was afraid he would have to do similar to other soldiers.</p>
        <p>"Well," responded the man, "I don't know where she is, and don't want to, and you will have to pay it through my solicitors. I make her an allowance of £350 a year, and if she wants another tanner let her have it."</p>
        <p>Whether she'got it I don't know.</p>
        <p>The school children have had three days' holi- day this week, and I was pleased to see that in many cases they have been used to advantage.</p>
        <p>Up the Higher-lane last Tuesday my atten- tion w.as drawn to three lads with leggings on that reached almost :to, their eyes.</p>
        <p>They were off to snend their school holidays picking .potatoes for the farmers.</p>
        <p>One of the teachers spends all her spare time</p>
        <p>nursing the wounded at Brookfield</p>
        <p>By the way, there is a soldier in the Brook- field Hospital with no less than 28 years' ser- vice to his credit.</p>
        <p>Acting-Sergeant Griffiths, one of our local con- stables, was complimented for his smartness at a special sitting of the Lymm. Bench last Thurs- day.</p>
        <p>I think that we will all agree with what the Chairman said, "He did' his work Well."</p>
        <p>Seaman D. Bradhurn, one of our local naval men, has been over on a short furlough this week.</p>
        <p>He looks quite a typical sailor</p>
        <p>What about " Tommy " this Christmds? Are you going to send him any puddings?</p>
        <p>He has been "bursting up" the Huns now for over heo years, so it's about time he had a "burst up" all on his own.</p>
        <p>The best way to ensure this is to send a con- tribution to our pudding fund without delay.</p>
        <p>There ain't no shops to shop in. , 'There ain't no puddings to buy, If you send them an " Examiner " pudding they'll thank you,— So will I.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>HIGH PRICES.</head>
        <p>HIGH PRICES.</p>
        <p>Are Our Farmers Playing The</p>
        <p>Game?</p>
        <p>THE VALUE OF WOMEN</p>
        <p>LABOUR.</p>
        <p>By S.D.M.</p>
        <p>Are farmers pia' ying the game? I wonder! Judging by the condition of things to-day I should say not. How •is it that their produce is so dear, and gets dearer every day? Have you ever put this question to a farmer and been told that it is due chiefly to the shortage of labour? And have you ever thought how far-fetched this excuse is? Just turn your minds back to the Tribunals and try to think how many farmers' men have been appealed for unsuccessfully. You will find that they are very few, for the Tribunals throughout the country have given the farmers the greatest consideration. What Women Have Done. The men who have gone have been replaced by women, and right well have these women performed their various tasks. They have been stirred, simply through the spirit of patriot- ism, to do things that in ordinary times we would have thought impossible. They knew that they were doing something of national importance, something that would help to beat the Hun, and keep the old flag flying. Well done, our women! You have right loyally done your bit. Now the farmer wants to tell us indirectly that you have been a failure, but we know better, for have not we seen you at your work, and cannot we now -see the results of your labour? Instead .of being &amp;failure, as the farmer would have us believe, you have been a treniendous success, and we men as British- ers take off our hats. Perhaps you wonder what I .mean when I say that he tells us in- directly that you have been a failure. Well, he tells us through the • exorbitant price -he asks for his produce. °For instance, take the price, of potatoes. When last I made inquiries they were 10s. per cwt. Simply an out- rageous price for this time of the year. During the last fortnight potatoes have advanced from ss. 6d. per cwt. to 10s. Then they try to tell us that the large prices are due to the shortage of labour. Increased Prices and Soldiers' Wives.</p>
        <p>An increase of 4s. 6d. per cwt. in a fortnight! If it was not so serious I should feel inclined to smile. I am afraid that after the war far- mers will -commence building houses. The Tribunals keep giving them exemptions, think- ing that they will keep the price of food in reason. Then at the end of the potato season they ask 10s. per cwt. Some say that muni- tion workers are getting such large wages that they can afford to pay. But what about our soldiers' wives? Can they afford to pay these prices out of the separation allowances they are granted by the State? These are the wives of the Men who have gone out to defend such men as farmers, while they stop at home and .make fortunes. Think! It makes one's blood boil. If they are not content with being allowed to stop at home, then let them go and fight, .and they will find that there are no fortunes to. be made in the trenches of Flan- ders. '</p>
        <p>I will now give you a comparison of the recognisal Prices of food from the commence- ment of war to. October, 1916. Remember, these were the recognised prices, but in several instances you had to pay more :- Sept., 1914. Oct., 1916. Milk, 3d. per quart Potatoes, d 4 per lb. Margarine, Bd. per lb. Fresh Butter, 1/3 per lb. Salt Buffer, 1/- to 1/3 per lb l/8 per lb. Cheshire Cheese, Bd. per lb. American Cheese, 10d. per lb. " Beef, 11d. to 1/- per lb Chilled_ or Frozen- R ibs, Rd. to 10d. Per 1b.... 1/2 to 1/3 per lb. Thin Flank, 6d. or 7d. per 1b.... is. per lb. British Mutton- • Legs, 1/- per lb. l/4 per lb. Breast, 7d. per lb. ,to 9,1 d. per lb. Frozen Legs, 9d. to .10d. lb. ....1/1 to 1/3 lb. Frozen Breast, sd. to 6d. lb. ... 81d. to 91d. Flour; 101 d. to 1/2 per 71bs. ... 1/44 per, Vbs. Best Flour, 1/2 per 71bs. Bread- 51d. to 6d. 411). loaf ... 81d. to 90. 41b. loaf Sugar, 34{1. per lb. Eggs, 1/6 per dozen Fish- Hake, Bd. per lb. Cod, 6d. per lb. - per lb. . Haddock, 6d. per lb. - per lb. Plaice, Bd. per lb. - per lb.</p>
        <p>Herring, 2 for Have you been paying the recognised prices?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>URBAN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Councillor C. E. Cowper's Complaints About Single Young Men. Coun. F. Lockyer presided at the monthly meet- ing, when there were also present Councillors J. H. Holt, W. G. Groves, R. 11 Gotobed, F. Ingham, G. H. Parker, C. E. Cowper and T. W. Warburton, Messrs W. Mullard (clerk), D. Mort.(surveyor), T. H. West (ggs manager), and C. I. Barnes (water manager) were in attend- ance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Fire Brigade Engineer.</head>
        <p>Fire Brigade Engineer.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Simpson, engineer of the fire brigade, wrote resigning his position.—On the motic:n of Councillor Warburton, seconded by, Councillor Gotobed, the resignation was accepted, with the council's expression of regret.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Sanitary Precautions.</head>
        <p>Sanitary Precautions.</p>
        <p>Referring to alleged lack of proper sanitation at a large local works, Councillor Cowper urged that the matter should have immediate atten- tion, in order to obviate the spread of sickness. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Mort said the firm had replied stating that the matter would have prompt attention. Councillor Gotobed said that a large amount of female labour was employed, and he moved that the firm be requested to make provision at once.</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper secondec, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Combing Out.</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper again referred to the number of young men whom he contended' should be taken into the Army before any Married men were called up. He wanted to know if he could get a list of these young men— these young single men. The Chairman: ''Perhaps the Clerk could supply you? Mr. Mullard : I am afraid I cannot.</p>
        <p>Councillor, Cowper mentioned the case of a woman whose husband had been shot at the Front, leaving her with four, young children, and here were these young men not offering their services. He thought every single man between 19 and 26 should be combed out.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor Gotobed thought that Mr. Cowper's duty, as requested by the Council, was to look after those who had nqt registered, and not those who had not joined the Army.</p>
        <p>" Frodsham " Charity.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mullard requested the Council to appoint four representative trustees on the Board of Management of the "Mrs. Esther Frodsham Charity and Clothing Charity to act for a term of four years.</p>
        <p>On the proposal of Councillor Parker, seconded by Councillor Warburton, Councillors Lockyer, Holt, Ingham and Cowper were appointed.</p>
        <p>Dr. Burrows's Mishap.</p>
        <p>The Chairman suggested that the Council should expres4" Sympathy with Dr. 11. A. Bur- rows, acting medical officer, on the accident which had happened when he had the mis- fortune to fall and injure his hand. They hoped he would be soon able to come back to his duties. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Cowper. . seconded, and the Clerk was requested to write Dr. Burrows.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>"GOD IS GOOD."</head>
        <p>"GOD IS GOOD."</p>
        <p>Driver James Dandy Writes Home.</p>
        <p>Writing to his parents at Moston-street, New- fields, Driver James Dandy, of the A.S.C., writes:—" Somewhere in France,—l. am very pleased to say that I received your nice box all right. It was fine, and Ithank you very much. You must not upset yourself about me, for I am quite all right, and, thank God, in the best of health. Just keep on smiling, for we are not downhearted. It's a long way ,to Tipperary, but my • heart is all the way with- you; Don't worry, for God is good, and He is with us night and day.'</p>
        <p>Driver James Dandy for several years worked for Mr. Harry Gibbon, farmer, of Higher-lane, but prior -to enlisting he was engaged as a carter for the Irlam Council. He was educated at Lymm School, and attended the Parish Church. fie is a brother to Sergeant Herbert Dandy, killed Nihilst serving with the Manchester Regi- ment on the Gallipoli Peninsula.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SECOND-LIEUT. IL A. BURDETT.</head>
        <p>SECOND-LIEUT. IL A. BURDETT.</p>
        <p>Grammar School "Old Boy" Receives A Commission. •</p>
        <p>The people of Lymm will be pleased to know that Mr. Richard Arthur Burdett has received a commission in the Cheshire- Regiment. Some years ago, when a pupil of Lymm School, be was successful in winning a competitive scholarship which enabled him to complete his education at the Lymm Grammar School. After leaving school he emigrated to Toronto, Canada, and.at the outbreak of war enlisted in the Princess Pat's Light Infantry. Shortly after arriving with this corps in England he entered the O.T.C. and went into training at Oxford. He is twenty-two years of age, and has this week been spendimg a short furlough with his sister, Mrs. Harry Bradburn, of Barr's Bank.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PRIVATE GEORGE CHAPMAN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE CHAPMAN.</p>
        <p>L3mm And Warrington Postman Comes Home Sick.</p>
        <p>Private Geo. Chapman has arrived in Eng- land sick. On a field card to his parents at Brookfield-terrace,'he stated that he had been admitted into hospital sick, and ,was doing well. This was closely followed by a hospital card sent by the officials of the Northumber- land War Hospital, on whicet they stated that he had been admitted into that hospital. No information as regards the nature of his illness has yet been received. Private Geo. Chapman is 20 years of age, was 'educated at Lymm School, and worshipped at the Congregational Church. Soon after leaving school he entered the service of the Post Office,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>and for several years he was a boy messenger at the Lymin Office: Later he was transferred to the Warrington office, and there up to his joinint, up he officiated as a postman. He enlisted in February of this year and joined the Cheshire Regiment, and was drafted out to France about June. His brother, Private Wm. Chapman, is at present on the water, believed to be bound for Egypt.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Lyrnm Platoon : No. 1 Battalion.</p>
        <p>B COMPANY.</p>
        <p>Orders for week:—</p>
        <p>Tuesday, November 7th.-7.30 p.m., Route March. Uniform or brassards. Every mem- ber to attend.</p>
        <p>Thursday, November 9th.— 7.39 p.m., Recruits' Drill; 8 p.m., Platoon Drill. Orderlies for week.—Sergt. S. D. Moston and Privates D. Mort and J. R. Burn. By Order,</p>
        <p>G. R. WILLIAMSON, Lieut.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Chairman, Mr. T. J. Ridgway; military repre- sentatives, Messrs. A. D. C. Parmiter and F. E. Evans and Capt. Hawkins, R.E.; Board of Agri- Culture, Mr. Newton Worthington.</p>
        <p>Substitution.</p>
        <p>Dealing with the great question of substitu- tion, Councillor J. H. Holt remarked that if men who had been classified in Class A, and were engaged on work of national importance, were to he substituted by men who had been,classified in Class 82, 83, or Cl, C 2, or C 3, the same thing should apply in the case of farmers. They had had a lot of farmers' men before them who were apparently quite fit and could easily be substi- tuted by men who were not fit for active ser- vice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Brothers and a Man.</head>
        <p>Brothers and a Man.</p>
        <p>Two brothers, joint tenants of a farm, com- prising 170 acres, asked for total exemption, and also for the exemption of one of their men. They were short of labour, and still had plenty of ,produce to gather. One of the brothers was a sole tenant of a small holding comprising 35 acres. The man appealed for was their teams- man, and: had to look after four 'teams of horses and sometimes five.—Adjourned for medical examination.</p>
        <p>Five Brothers and None Serving.</p>
        <p>A farm bailiff who appeared-to ,ask for the ex- emption of one of his men, stated that the farm comprised 80 acres,49 acres of which were arable. The man was single and 27 years of age. The owner of the farm had been on active service since the commencement of war. , Have you. ever tried women labour? Yes! last _ _</p>
        <p>Week, but they did not stop. My place must be</p>
        <p>too far away.—Have you ever applied to the Army for men? No. The Military Representative (addressing the man): Have you any brothers? Yes.—How many? I don't know; there's two in , two at , and one at</p>
        <p>A Member: That makes five. Are any of them in the Army?—No. The Military Representative (addressing the farm bailiff) : Does your employer know about this young man?—l suppose so. His wife does, if he doesn't. He told me to clear out all that could be spared, but he also said that the farm could not stand.</p>
        <p>Adjourne4 for medical examination. Substitution.</p>
        <p>The Chairman here remarked that if a farmer's man was substituted, the substitution ought to be approved of by the Board of Agri- culture representative, who is a practical farmer.</p>
