      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Widow's Successful Appeal For Son.</head>
        <p>Widow's Successful Appeal For Son.</p>
        <p>A widow who appealed for son, 38, and single, stated that her farm comprised 87 acres. He was her main support, and she could not carry on the farm without him. She had one other son at home, but he had recently met with an accident, and was practically useless. She also had another invalid son who was married, and she had to help to support him. They had no other male help.—Exemption granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Farmer For Man.</head>
        <p>Farmer For Man.</p>
        <p>A farmer who appealed• for his man stated that this was the only man he employed. He was a first-class man and was an all-round farm hand. The man, who was in Class A, was married, and had five children, the eldest of whom was only nine years old. In normal times he employed three men.—Temporary exemption, February Ist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Farrn,-,‘r For Son.</head>
        <p>Farrn,-,‘r For Son.</p>
        <p>Another farmer, who applied for his on, 22 and single, Stated that his farm comprised 85 acres. This was his only man, and they had IS cows, three heavy horses and one pony. They had 40 acres under the plough. He employed two women potato picking.—Tempor- ary exemption, December 31st; in meantime to go before the Medical Board. Employer on Active Service.</p>
        <p>A foreman farmer, who appeared to ask for the exemption of a man, stated that he was indispensable to the working of the farm, which comprised 115 acres. He employed two more boys and three women. A letter was read in support of the appeal from the man's employer, who is on a&amp;ive service.—Temporary exemp- tion, February Ist. Three Brothers In Army.</p>
        <p>'A. young man who appealed on personal grounds, stated that he. had three brothers in the Army, and he was the only son at home. His father suffered from rheumatism and neuritis, and was subject to fits. An appeal was also lodged by his employer, who is a small farmer.—Personal appeal, conditional exemp- tion, Ist March; employer's appeal, temporary exemption, Ist March.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Visitor's Death in Stockton Heath.—On Mon- day Mr. George Rose, 68 years of age, 17, Horne- street, Derby, retired railway guard, who was on a, visit to his son at 7, Bedford-street, Stock- ton Heath, took suddenly ill, and went to bed. He complained of pains in his chest, and Dr. Howard was called in, but he passed away about 11.30 the following morning. At an inquest held on Wednesday, the jury found that death was due to natural causes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL SINGING AT THE WESLEYAN SERVICES.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL SINGING AT THE WESLEYAN SERVICES.</p>
        <p>Anniversary services were held in Stockton Heath Wesleyan Church on Sunday, when the preacher at the morning and evening services was the Rev. T. Galland Hartley, of Liverpool. There were exceptionally large congregations and the collections were in aid of the Trust Funds. A special feature of the services was the beautiful singing by the -Choir, which, we understand, has gained much distinction. At the morning- service the choir rendered the anthem " Sing a song of praise," and in the evening "Judge me, 0 God." Mr. P. L. Rich- ardson was the soloist at the evening service and sang "Come unto Me" and "A " Dream of Paradise." In the afternoon there was a musical service, presided or by Mr. John Taylor, when selections from Haydn's " Crea- tion " were admirably rendered by an aug- mented choir. The -soloists were Miss E. Houghton, Miss Miriam Webb, Miss, May Webb, Mrs. J. A. Fairclough, Mrs. Scholes, and Mr. W. Warburton. A quartet party composed of Mrs. W. Warburton, Mr. J. Mills, Miss Carrie Plumpton and Mr. W. Warburton sang "God is a Spirit" and "The Lord is mindful." The whole of the musical part of the services was ably conducted by Mr. F. E. Penketh, while Mrs. G. Gould was the organ- ist, and Miss E. Turner was at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>PRIVATE WILLIAM SKINNER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM SKINNER.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Man Reported Killed.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Donnelly, of -31, Walton-road, Stockton Heath, has received information that her nephew, Private William Skinner, of the Royal Warwickshire Reserves, has been reported killed in action on the 3rd September. The news has reached her through her sister, the boy's mother, who is living in America, and to whom a cable was sent by the War Office. The presumption is that some letter with Mr. Skinner's address was found in the young man's possession. Private Skinner, a native of Stock- ton Heath, was 27 years of age, and joined the Warwicks over a year ago. He had been originally .an apprentice in Grime's Drapery Warehouse. Bridge-street, and went from there to Birmingham to develop his knowledge of the trade. He probably made so many friends there that .he decided to join the Warwicks. He was educated at St. Thomas' Parochial Schools, and attended that Church. His younger brother, Albert, is also in the Army, and stationed at present in Carnarvon.</p>
        <p>It is wortliy of mention that Mrs. Donnelly Ims no fewer ,than eight nephews serving with the Colours.</p>
      </div>
