      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>69, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>69, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Common Sense Treatment.—Rest .and relaxation, plenty of fresh air, plenty of milk, and plenty of water, together with the special help Doan's Backache Kidney Pills afford.</p>
        <p>Doan's Pills help to cure even advanced kidney symptoms such as :--</p>
        <p>• Rheumatism, Lumbago, Stone, Sciatica, Gravel, Inflamed Bladder, Dropsy,</p>
        <p>and other uric-acid and blood diseases. Doan's Pills do not affect the bowels. War- rington people, week by week, explain how Doan's Pills have relieved them of kidney complaints, and the evidence of a neighbour is something to rely on.</p>
        <p>Send for Free Book on " Moderation, Cheer- fulness, and Other Long Life Laws."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing sth November : Sunday.—N.C.O.'s Class, Chelford. Meet Victoria-square, 9 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Monday.--Company and Recruits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.45. Tuesday.—Recruits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.45. Thursday.—Recruits' Drill, 'Signalling Class, and Musketry Instruction to new members allocated to No. 1 Platoon, Headquarters, 7.45. Friday.—Swedish Drill, Headquarters, 7.45. Sunday, 12th.—Mayor's Parade, Parish Church, Warrington. Fall in, Stafford-road, 9.30. Leave Town Hall 10 o'clock. Orderlies for the week.—Sec.-Com. M. C. White and Private A. Millar. FRANK , WARBURTON, Lieut., Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER tREGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending Saturday, Nov. 11th: Monday.—Musketry at range, 7.30 p.m. Range Officer, Private J. F. Fairhurst. Tuesday.—Musketry Instruction at Head- quarters, 7.45 p.m. . . -</p>
        <p>Thursday.--quad drill at headquarters, 7.45 p.m. _ - • -</p>
        <p>Friday.—Musketry at range for recruits, 7.30 p.m. Names of those required to attend will be posted at Headquarters. Range Officer, Sec.- Corporal E. Pearce. . Saturday.—Company drill. Fall in at head- quarters at 3 p.m. Orderlies.—Sec.-Corporal F. Fox, Private T. Whyman.</p>
        <p>By order, F. A. FROST (Lieutenant) O.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>PENKETIL</head>
        <p>PENKETIL</p>
        <p>Death of Mr.. Chas. Houghton.—lt is with deep regret we have to record the death of Mr. Charles Houghton, third son of the late Mr. W. B. Houghton and Mrs. Houghton, Hall Nook. The deceased gentleman. who was 24 years of age, had been ailing for some consider able time, and passed away on Saturday evening, death being due to heart fnilure. 'rhe funeral took place on Wednesday afternoon at Sankey Cemetery, the Rev. J. R. Jones officiating.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—A very interesting lantern lecture, entitled, "Through Yorkshire Dales," was given on Tuesday night by Mr. W. H. Wilcockson, Latchford. The schoolroom was packed, and the audience showed their apprecia- tion of the lecturer's description of a personal tour thebugh di,stricts in which the scenery could well hold its own with many sought-after European health resorts. Mr. James Watson occupied the chair.</p>
        <p>Local Wounded Soldiers.—Private James Hayes, South Lancashires, eldest son of Mr. Joseph and Mrs. Hayes, Warrington-road, is in hospital in Oxford suffering from leg injuries sustained through falling in a shell hole at the Front, where he has been since the beginning of March.</p>
        <p>Private George Tucker, Grenadier Guards, youngest son of Mr. and Mrs. Tucker, Station- road, is at present lying in a London hospital recovering from wounds and injuries received at the Front in France. Shrapnel in the right shoulder has given considerable trouble, and other operations fqund necessary have delayed his long looked-for journey home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>HEADMASTER FINED.</head>
        <p>HEADMASTER FINED.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Edward Browne, principal of the Frienfts' School, Penketh, was fined £2 at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day for failing to reduce the lights at his home on October 23rd, the magistrates describ- ing it as a serious case.. Costs were also allowed for the appearance of a 'Special Con- stable, who gave, evidence. Mr. F. A. Frost (chairman), saying the constable appeared to be taking a great interest in his work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>War Relief Association.—There was a very large attendance at 'a whist drive which was held in the Church on Friday evening last week, the proceeds being in aid of the local War Relief Association. The prize winners were: Mrs: Hall, Mrs. Ellis, Mi. E. England and Mr. J. Worrall. Mr. W. Webb acted as M.C.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Sisterhood.—A meeting of the Sister- hood was held on Thursday afternoon in the Schoolroom, when an address was given by Mrs. Dodds, of Penketh. Speaking from the words, "Nevertheless at thy. word I will let down the net," she pointed out the necessity of obedience to God, and 'he importance of using the talents we possess, so that if we are faithful in the small things of life we Shall receive the promised reward.. A sweet rendering of the solo, "Just for to-day" was given by Mrs. Plinston. The meeting was presided over by Mrs. Dakin, and Mrs: Bate was t4e accompanist.</p>
        <p>Devotional Meeting.—The monthly devotional Meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Thursday evening, presided over by the Rev. A. J. Morton. A very interesting paper on "Wall Building" was given by Mrs. Dakin. The sub- ject was taken from Nehemiah, and dealt with the rebuilding of the walls of Jerusalem. A description of the desolate state of the city wa. given, showing how a leader was provided in</p>
        <p>the person of Nehemiah, who was a man at faith, prayer, couf age and perseverance. Luther, Cromwell and Langton were used as illustrations to show how in times of erises a leader is always forthcoming. The speaker mentioned that the work was acco.mplished bY earnest prayer, skilful planning and stead! plodding, riointing out that these elements av 3 essential to the successful upbuilding of char ter arid to the progressive growth of Church life. A vote of thanks was moved by Mr. Leach and Mr. E. England, and supported by Mr" South.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NEIGHBOUR'S ADVICE.</head>
        <p>A NEIGHBOUR'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>On November 28th, 1913, Mrs. D. Herbert, of 30, Parker-street, Warrington, said:—" A severe chill affected my kidneys, and for nearly three months I suffered terribly with my back. I was almost doubled up with sharp, cutting pains across the kidneys. One week I could not get about at all. I became nervous and depressed and lost all energy. Everything was a burden. Other depressing symptoms proved my kidneys to be affected.</p>
        <p>"I thought I would try Doan's Back- ache Kidney Pills, and I am very glad now that I did, for three boxes entirely cured me of all my aches and pains, and made me feel a different woman."</p>
        <p>--:----- (Signed)' " D. Herbert."</p>
        <p>On February 21st, 1916—over two years later—Mrs. Herbert said :1--" I am pleased to tell you I keep well, thanks to an occasional dose of Doan's Pills. They made a fine cure of me."</p>
        <p>Be sure you ask.for DOAN'S, and get DOAN'S, the Pills Mrs. Herbert had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>APPLETON.</head>
        <p>APPLETON.</p>
        <p>New Guardian.—A letter was received at Runcorn Board of Guardians on Tuesday frora Appleton Parish Council re appointment of 3 Guardian in place of the late Mr. Edwin Britch.—Mr. Hough, acting clerk, said that elections were not allowed at the present time in a casual vacancy, but it was left to the ,authority to fill the vacancy by co-option.—Mr• Edward Marsh, of Bradley Hall, Appleton, was co-opted to the vacancy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>PRIX ITE ALBERT FORSTER.</p>
        <p>Appleton Hall Gardener Loses A Leg- Private Albert Forster, of the Ist Cheshire Regiment, and a son of Mr. and Mrs. Jo iii h• n Forster, of Moore, was wounded by shrapnel the left thigh and arm on September 3rd. lie was kept in a base hospital in France for three weeks, and then transferred to the military hos: pital at Netley, where he still is. His thiff° was very badly fractured, and a number ot operations were performed in the effort to save the limb, but on SUnday, October 22nd, it found necessary to amputate the leg in order to save his life, and we are glad to report that he is now progressing favourably. Before enlisting Private Forster was emploYea as a gardener at Appleton 'Hall. He enlisted February of this year, and was sent to France with a (Waft on June 22nd. In Mg first engage- ment three weeks later, Private Forster 085 buried in a shell hole and lost dll his kit, 'Alt escaped without personal injury. Mr. and Mrs. Forster have two other sons in the Army. Sergeant R. Forster is at present hospital at Malta, having'undergone an opera; tion, and Private J. E. Forster is stationed au Salonica, and, as far as is known at preseat, is quite well.</p>
        <p>"For the Blood Is the Life."</p>
      </div>
