      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>A PICTURISED ROMANTIC Nova BY ROY tIfcCARCELL ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL-FLYING A** SERIAL FILM-</p>
        <p>BY ALEC J. BRAID.</p>
        <p>[Characters in this story are purely imaginary, living</p>
        <p>and have no connection with or relation to any person.]</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIII</p>
        <p>THE DOUBLE CROSS.</p>
        <p>"Let the coachman take the reins," cried Durand, subconsciously changing from his careless air of racegoer to the grave demeanour of physi- cian. "Lend a hand, Blair," he added, as the coachman worked around from the back seat by the groom to the driver's place: Blair and Vivian, assisting as best they could, aided Durand in dragging Arthur from the box. Seat to the second one. Here he collapsed, mur- inuring, "Esther F How .plainly I saw her in her giPsy dress. She spoke to me. The hunchback fellow was with her." " It was imagination," said Vivian soothingly to the stricken man, as the pseudo physician Durand administered the all-too-handy hypodermic. A touch of sun, wasn't it, doctor?" "Yes," said Durand, in equally soothing accents, "I think it was the sun and too much exertion. We shouldn't have let you drive, Mr. Powell. You are not strongsenough yet for these things." Blair said nothing. He could have almost pitied Arthur, that in so short a time as had elapsed since his injuries his character had been so weak- ened from his hurts and the drugs administered that he could be _convinced that the evidence of his own eyes was but a phantom of o, disordered imagination. Neither the coachman nor the groom had under- stood the significance of their master's collapse at the sight of two gipsy musicians with•pia.no, pony Cart, and monkey, by the roadside. The coachman and groom, like the other servants at the Powell mansion, only knew that their master had never been mentally the same since his injuries at the mines and his first home-coming to his new grand Mansion, a battered, shattered, insensible form on a stretcher. In a few moments the effect of the drug had calmed Arthur, and he was sitting with closed eyes on the second seat, with Blair, Vivian, and Durand. A passing motor car coming in the same direc- tion slowed ,down beside the coach, and the occu- Pants called to the driver and the tally-ho party that there was a man on behind. "He looks a tough customer, too," added the driver of the car. The groom on the rear seat looked down and ordered the tough-looking customer to get off. The tough-looking customer was Luke Lovell, who had come back to Los Angeles, sullenly determined to Make an asset of his secret and blackmail either Arthur Stanleigh or Blair—preferably both. Luke Lovell growled a curse back at the groom and held on. The coachman, with practised.dex- terity, lashed the whip back of the coach at a Signal from the groom. The thong cut Luke across the face twice before he could shield himself. But again and again the lash cut and stung him Cruelly on neck and shoulders, and, despite his hardiness and determination to stand the on- slaught, Luke was compelled to let go and drop off. He limped to the sidewalk of the quiet suburban residence street and sat in the shade of a tree by the kerb. -Near him a leaking fire hydrant trickled. Luke mopped his brow and the burning cuts across his face with cooling water. The pain subsided and Luke looked around him. There, far off down the street, but yet distinct to the keen eyes of the gips,. were two figures plodding-by a pony piano tart, two figures he knew as well as he knew his own—Esther and Quabba. Old emotions, old fealties stirred in Luke Lovell at the sight. Esther, daughter of Hagar, the Queen of the Gipsies; Esther, whom he had always gazed upon with pride and a dull longing. She Was not for him. Luke knew that. But yet. there *tined in him the old sense of affection, and, niingled with it, a sense of shame. Bad as the tiPsy was, there had always been something fine and tender in his regard for Esther. To injure her had been no part of his wild plans to make merchandise of what he dimly surmised was the Stanleigh secret—and Hagar's.</p>
        <p>Lukes mental processes were slow, but he was quick to recognise that he had best have time to think how he should meet with Esther agam did he wish to regain her confidence, as he now desired to do. Ere rose from the kerb even while cogitating lIDon these things and concealed himself behind a tree. But he could have stood plain upon the Sidewalk for all he would have seen, for Esther sad Quabba, their beadg bowed sadly to the dust, 'trudged on beside the patient pony, nor looked to right or left. The smart tally-ho rattled on, and ere long drew 1113 before the portals of the Powell mansion. and Arthur was assisted off by his whilom friends. such is the power of suggestion upon a drug- Weakened mind that Arthur had thrown aside all 4sPression and worry soon after entering his luxurious home. Physically his strength had al- Inost fully returned and the plotters rejoiced to see that under the stimulant administered and their cajolements the panic-stricken remembrance ,of his encounter with Esther had been dismissed by Arthur as an illusion. A half-hour later Esther and Quabba parted at the alley entrance that ied to Sankey's stables. Esther donned her cloak again to cover her gipsy attire and removed her head-dress, and thus garbed seemed a passing shop-girl, no did she attract undue attention as she slipped quietly to her room by the entrance of the hotel on a side- street. Quabba stood gazing after his young mistress Wistfully until she had turned from the street some blocks away. Then Quabba was roused by a hand limn his shoulder, and turned to see the swarthy face of Luke Lovell. "Well, what do you want?" he asked. "I am seeking revenge. I owe that gipsy and his crowd something for thrashing me on the Coach. I'll get my own back or be killed," replied Luke Lovell, and he poured out his tale of woe to Quabba. "To-morrow," Luke said, "I'll go and see them," and the following day he met Blair Stan- !sigh. ' "I want to see Arthur Stanleigh," he shouted. " I owe him something."</p>
        <p>"I am going to the office for an hour, and irill then be back," replied Blair. "Go as far as you like, and I'll stand by you." But such was the duplicity of Blair that when he reached the office Ile said to Arthur's secretary: "Wilson telephone the police to be prepared to send an officer to Mr.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>Powell's in an hour. Mr. Powell is being annoyed by a blackmailing stranger." Confident that he would be backed by Blair, Luke called at the mansions. The efforts of the supercilious servant to keep the uncouth gipsy out of the house were all in vain. Thrusting the man unceremoniously aside, Luke strode into the hall and demanded to be taken to the master of the house. Reinforced, the footman and his fellows pushed him out; but the gipsy was not so easily disposed of. Again he approached the door, and before the astonished footman could summon his faculties, had once more gained admission. By this time Blair had returned and the police were approaching the mansion. Into the great salon the gipsy burst: "I will see him. Where is your master?" he demanded of the startled men-servants. .</p>
        <p>"What is all the bother?" asked Blair, coming forward.</p>
        <p>"You know very well. I have come to see John Powell."</p>
        <p>"Here he is," said a languid voice behind them, and Luke turned to see the Golden Man, worn and bewildered, crossing the salon. "Don't you know me?" shouted Luke. "I am afraid I do .not," was the reply. "That won't do., You recognise me, yOu cur. Yesterday you ordered your coachman to thrash me. Now it is my turn. I know too much to be turned down. Ask that ugly beggar , standing there," and Lovell, with a grimy, outstretched finger indicated Blair.</p>
        <p>But it was not a, part-of Blair's scheme to have the Stanleigh secret even hinted at, and, beckoning to the police who were entering, he pointed to Luke, and the gipsy was seized and hurried away. Among other things in Luke's pockets was a band which had encircled the roll of notes given Luke by Arthur when he found him in the desert. The Golden Man's fortune had been built upon stolen money, thv money the robbers had secured from the express the day Vivian Marston lost the won- derful jewel. .</p>
        <p>Arthur could not allow that fact to be known, and he charged the gipsy with forcibly entering his mansion. The police regarded Luke as a dan- gerous character, and the magistrate, taking a similar view, sentenced him to a smart term of imprisonment. "Our wealthy men must be pro- tected against such men as you," he observed to the scowling gipsy.</p>
        <p>,In his ex,tr.iniity- Luke wrote to Quabba: "Have been double crossed ,by people we both know. Please bring Miss Esther." "Who is he that he should have me locked up? The banknote band he gave me with the stolen money he found. , His fortune was started with stolen money." "They have double crossed me," added -Luke, "but I'll keep my mouth shut and have my. re- venge in my own way—a gipsy's way."</p>
        <p>But, meanwhile, what of Santley Circus? Sant- ley, the coarse-grained tyrant of the show, lay dead in the morgue. Beside him was Splinters, the clown, who killed Santley and shot himself; and with them was the disfigured body of La Belle, wife of Splinters the clown, killed by the lion Lancelot. A triple tragedy, and all because of a woman's coquetry and The Diamond from the Sky. The new manager of the circus was optimistic. He was inclined to believe "the big blow off," as he called the tragedy, would be "great press stuff for the show." But the triple tragedy, the panic and the riot that followed it, had brought the creditors, who were numerous, down upon the show, and it was seized for debt, and was to be sold at auction. The disconsolate performers and attaches. their salaries long in arrears, realised it was a bad season for the show business, and were fully aware the returns from a sheriff's auction would pay little, if, any, 'of their salaries. Then Bill Hull, acrobat, remembered the great diamond Santley gave La Belle before the lion Lancelot killed her—inspired to the deed by the jealous clown. Splinters, the show believed. Gossip as to the value of this great gem had been rife about the circus since Williams, the billposter, brought it in from the woods, where he said he found it, and lost it to Santley at dice. If this diamond were recovered it would be an asset that would perhaps pay all salaries when sold. Bill Hull, the acrobat, remembered Sankey, the stableman, was one who inatched at the diamond. Hull knew Sankey. In a previous season, with a smaller show. some of the animals had been stabled at Sankey's.</p>
        <p>The sheriff was-informed, and the acrobat, don- ning an overcoat to hide his tights—for the sheriff's seizure had stopped the show—led the way to Sa,nkey's stables. With the sheriff and the acro- bat went a policeman and the manager of the circus pro tern. _ _ _</p>
        <p>They reached the stable yard in an auto just pre- vious to the arrival of Quabba with his monkey and pony and street piano cart that he stabled there. While Quabba faced Luke Lovell at the alley's mouth, the sheriff with the acrobat and others were in the stable. Sankey, had been in the loft searching vainly for the diamond where he hid it beneath the hay. He came down the ladder and faced the sunlight from the open door, and there, between him and the daylight, gleaming like a star, dangled from the hayrack over the pony's empty stall The Diamond from the Sky. Just as Sankey grasped the diamond from where it had been left, unseen by Quabba, in his pony's hay, the sheriff and his party were at the door, and grabbed at the slinking stableman. Out from the place they struggled, the diamond glittering in the sun, clutched in Sankey's grimy hand, that was in turn clutched by the others. In wresting it from the desperate stableman the diamond was passed almost against the faces of Quabba and Luke Lovell, who had hurried upon the scene from the alley mouth at the first sounds of the struggle. How well they 'chew that dazzling jewel! It had been a fortune for them both, a fortune briefly held and quickly lost. The next day the papers rang with the account of the recovery of the diamond, the aftermath of the great circus tragedy. "The value of the diamond can hardly be estimated," the .Los Angeles papers said. "There are conflicting stories as to how it came into possession of the dead circus pro- prietor. This only is known; it will be sold at sheriff's auction as an asset of the Santley circus, bankrupt." The greatest asset of the concern was now to be bid for.</p>
