      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Section.)</p>
        <p>A NARROW DEFEAT.</p>
        <p>Rangers Victorious With Doubtful Try.</p>
        <p>Warrington lost by the odd point in five at Broughton on Saturday, though, on the play, they ought to have come home victors. There was an element of doubt about the try scored by the Rangers. Iforrocks, with plenty of time to clear, had his kick charged down by Tighe, and, judged from the Press box, there .was a palpable knock on. The Irefexee, howiever, allowed the half-back to dribble over the line and make good the try. Tranter then came within an ace of scoring, but before the inter- val he dropped a goal.</p>
        <p>Perhaps it was too much to expect a high- class game on such a day, for what with the greasy ball and parts of the field being under water, the players experiencr.3d difficulty in maintaining their balance. Slipping rendered the vertical horizontal on not a few occasions. Warrington certainly had the better of the game on the general run of the play, and did not deserve to be beaten. A little more level- headedness in the second half wo.ild have won the match. The opportunities were there to he taken, and Warrington have only themselves to blame; especially for the way they failed to make the most of things in the last five minutes, when awarded a penalty. Then they 6id net know what to do. Tranter, who captalne:l the team in the absence of Jolley, looked round, first at the stumps, then at Brookes; in quite a be- wildered manner, and eventually, as a last resort, took the kick at god himself, only to fail rather lamentably, one thought. Tranter would be the first to admit that he is no goal. kicker and he might well have -allotted the task to, say, Potter, who used to kick goals regularly for Rylands's. It is no use .having men in the team who can kick goals if they are not employed. Yet it is questionable whether Potter has been called upon to exercise himself in this department since he enlisted in Warrington's team. A mistake! A good General should make up his mind at once, taking the situation in at a glance and, above all, knowing what every member of the team shines in individually as well as collectively.</p>
        <p>There was far too much aimless kicking throughout the game, and particularly was this noticeable in the second half, when Warring- ton had the wind behind them. At times it pays to get as much boot as possible behind the ball, but there are occasions when other methods are more profitable. And on Saturday one would have liked to see the touch-line used oftener. Two or three short kicks along that line would have had more effect than the pon- derous ones indulgqd in.</p>
        <p>During the whole of the second half Brough- ton were playing a man short, and were against the wind. Under the circumstances some credit is due to them for the stubborn way in which they defended. And one is glad to say that the accident which 'necessitated Nicholas's leaving the field a quarter of an hour before time was not so seliouS as at first thought.</p>
        <p>Horrocks was only moderate, and at no period did he impress one with confidence. He lay too far back and, as a result, had very often to encounter an awkward boloicing ball. If he had played closer up to his three-quarters he would have been able to get under the ball be- fore it bounced, and thus saved himself much unnecessary exertion.</p>
        <p>The three-quarters had a busier existence than in the previous week's match, getting more'of the "scrum;" and, though they never rose above the mediocre, they were very sound throughout. It was a sign who was behind the "scrum " when one saw the ball being flung about so freely. Nicholas and Young were quite themselves at half-back, doing nothing to belie their reputation. We could do with their combination regularly. One noticed Douglas in the crowd watching them keenly.</p>
        <p>The forwards were as vigorous as usual, in- dulging in many of their redoubtable dribbles. Coming away from the match one overheard a compliment by a Broughton supporter--" Our forwards met 'their match to-day." Of the bunch, none did better than Harmer, Cunliffe and Jones. Mention ought to be made of a par, ticularlY fine effort made by Cunliffe in the first half, for in quite a solo movement he carried the ball from the centre right up,to the line, when he was stopped, and only just pre- vented from scoring.</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers: Blan ; Mills, Scott, Barnes, Robinson; Tighe, Gatley; Price, Quig- ley,Roberts, Scott, Haworth and Larkin.</p>
        <p>Warrington: H-orrocks; Brookes, Potter, Tranter, Griffiths; Nicholas, Young; Hamer, Green, Cunliffe, Matthews, Jones and Wall. Referee: Mr. Andrews, Oldham.</p>
      </div>
