      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>ASSOCIATION NOTES.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION NOTES.</p>
        <p>Last 'Saturday the Town Club, having no League fixtures, were successful in arranging a visit by the 'Liverpool League Team's Reserves to the Hood-lane ground. The home club was 1411 represented, and gave further trials to Woodhouse and Eccleston, and, by arrangement, the Manchester United officials exchanged the services of Standfast, who hails from the Macclesfield district, for those of Siddall, the latter making the journey to Stoke. The visi- tors were not able to field as Strong a team as advertised, but they included several who have done 'service in their first team's. ranks. They arrived with only ten players, and Illidge, of the home club: filled the vacancy. The .weather conditions were not very favour- able, and the number Of spectators was not se large as expected. • The game proved :to he a very one-sided affair, and had it not been for the splendid goalkeeping, of Connell, the score Would have been 'Much- larger: He, along with Davis, Fairhurst and Taylor, were their only players worthy of note. For the home team, Kite and his backs were not seriously troubled until the last fifteen minutes of the game. Ditchfield was the strongest of the half-backs, and besides scoring a grand goal, he plied his forwards with many good passes and neat ground work, and a grand goal scored by Woodhouse will ensure for him a further trial, and the display of Eccleston on the extreme right, showed that he should be persevered with. Banner was seen to better advantage than in his previous games. Aspinall was. always on the look-out for openings, and he was a source of trouble to the 'visitors' defenders; Standfast gave a favourable display, , and may be seen again in the ranks of the Town Club. Stephens, by his hard and steady work in the half-back line, appears to have made his position secure. Csnnell gave a grand, display in the Liverpool goal. Curtis and Hunter showed too much finesse for back players: Fairhurst was a hard worker at centre-half. . Davis was the chief raider of the Liverpool vanguard. Will the Liverpool officials try and cast their nets on any of the Town Club's players? They were favourably impressed with one and prob- ably two of them. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>AMONGST THE JUNIORS.</head>
        <p>AMONGST THE JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>As was written in these notes last week, some keen games were anticipated, and such proved to be correct. The Cadets entertained the Grappenhall team in Victoria Park, and in the ranks of the latter were some former Cadets. The. game; was very keenly 'contested from .be- ginning to end, and goals were plentiful, e'ach- team scoring four times. The Cadets were handicapped by the absence of Millar, their regular goalkeeper, and Greenhalgh, who filled the vacancy, showed signs of nerves and allowed his opponents to score on two occasions when he had plenty of time to. have cleared easily. Gibson was in good form at centre-half, .and shot well when he got the opportunity. Daven- port found his shooting.boots, and scored twice. Massey and Sankey also played well in defence and attack. It is evident that the Cadets are moving in the upward iklirection. Knight, Watson, Ashcroft and Jones played well for Grappenhall. - -</p>
        <p>Another very keen game in the Park was that between the Recs. and Orford Juniors. The first half was very evenly contested, and Chisholm and Buckley each scored-a grand goal for the Juniors. Green scored for the Recs. In the second portion the Recs. hold the best of the game, but owing to faulty shooting they had to acknowledge defeat by the odd goal in three. Bellshaw, Witton, Austen and Chisholm were the pick of the Juniors, and Green, Will- cox, James and Cooper showed he best form for the Recs.</p>
        <p>The greatest surprise waS at Lovely-lane, where the Bewsey Adults got defeated. on their own soil by the Highfield Juniors; this &amp;ing the latter team's first victory. It will be re- membered that at thefirst meeting of these teams on the Tannery ground the Adults won a hard game•by four goals against three. The Juniors paid their return, visit' last week with their minds fully made up to reverse the previous result, and their victory was no fluke, as on the day's play they proved themselves the better team and deserved the credit for their win.</p>
        <p>Much interest was centred in the return game between Howley United and. St. Elphin's, who occupy the first and second positions in the League Table. There was a good crowd, and the game was followed very keenly. The " Saints " have now got a very useful team to- gether, and will require some beating, while ,in the United team several changes had to be made at the last minute. Hoarty and Hind were un- able to play, and Wadhouse was their most notable absentee.</p>
        <p>After the first twenty minutes it became evident .that the leaders had met their match, and there was much difference in the style of play adopted by the teams. Povey, since his introduction as goalkeeper for the " Saints," has greatly strengthened - their ranks and inspired confidence in his col- leagues. 'Furness shows improvement in his play in every game. Watson played a fine game for the ," Saints," and his centres were some ,of the, best features of the match. Lee scored the best goal seen - at the ;Steel-street ground this, -season. Jones delighted the crowd by his , fine dribbling. For the United Walters showed signs of ner- vousness in goal. Foster was the better of the backs, and Miller played a hard game at centre- half. Their forwards were disorganised, and could do little against the "Saints' " defence, Hassall and Humphries being the pick.</p>
        <p>Another keen game was witnessed on the Pen- keth Rec., the score at the interval being one goal each. During the .second half there were signs of roughness gradually creeping into the game, and the referee had 'difficulty in keepiN the players in check. The final score was in favour of the visitors, and Caused surprise in the home camp. Brown scored both goals for the visitors. Smith, Young and IThyard also played well for the Hornets. Goulden was the successful marks- man for the home team, this being his first appearance. White, Moss and Sankey , also played well. The Penketh lads are not playing up to their form of last season. Losing, points on the Rec. •is not a very encouraging sign.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the. League on Monday night at Peter-street Dining Rooms, there will be some important- business to trans- act.</p>
        <p>I am in receipt of a letter from the Secretary of the Sankey ,Athletic F.C., which states that owing to several more of their players having joined the Colours, the committee are unable to get a team together, and they have decided to disband the club for the season.</p>
        <p>The committee of the ,Warrington Town Association F.C. have decided to run. a second team, and have been successful in their applica- tion for membership to the St. ne:ens and Dis- trict League. Their first fixture will be player: at Hood-lane to-day, when the Earlestown West team will be the visitors. I am informed that the committee have between thirty and forty applications from aspiring juniors for trials, and no doubt from this number a good selection' ought to he made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN v. LIVERPOOL RES. There was a very fair number or spectators pres- ent when the following players lined out:— Warrington Town: Kite; Leah, Mil'ward; Stephens, Standfast, Ditchfield; Eccleston, "Whit- field, Aspinall, Woodhouse and Banner. Liverpool Res.: Connell; Curtis, Hunter; Northey, Fairhurst, McDonald; Wordsworth, Illidge, Taylor, Lomas and Davies.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. C. Woods. The visitors won the toss and Aspinall set the ball in motion down the slope, and straightaway led an attack on the visitors' goal. Curtis cleared when Woodhouse was close in. A minute later Ditchfield tested Connell with a splendid long shot, which was well saved. The ball was temporarily transferred to the home half. Leah returned the ball and slack play by the visitors' backs allowed their goal to be captured, Aspinall scoring after five minutes' play. The home team continued to habe the best of the exchanges, and good combination between Woodhouse and Banner again put the visit- ors on the defensive. A good shot by the latter caused Connell to concede a corner. This was well placed and Connell again saved well. The visitors' forwards gained possession and carried play into the home quarters, where Leah and Millward were reliable and got the ball away. Eccleston made a good centre, Whitfield shooting over the bar. Ditchfield sent in a beauty of a shot from 30 yards' range, but Connell made a clever save. The visitors now paid a brief visit to the home end, and Lomas shot' wide of the goal. Another attack on the visitors' goal resulted in a-penalty. kick being given against Hunter, Eccleston converting from the shot. From the centre kick the visitors invaded the home quarters and gained a corner kick, and this was easily cleared by Kite. After a short spell of mid- ffeld play, Aspinall scored the third goal from a centre by Eccleston. The visitors now showed a great improvement in their play and had an equal share of the game, and after another fruitless corner to the home team a similar result was obtained at the home end, where the visitors' forwards finished very weakly. A fine shot by Standfast grazed the crossbar. Play was now contested in the visitors' half, and Connell made several fine saves. Off-side against Whitfield and Aspinall gave relief, and a raid on the home goal saw Kite save a low ground, shot from Wordsworth. Half-time: Warrington, 3 goals; Liverpool, nil. The second half opened with a strong attack by the home team, and a rattling shot from Banner hit the crossbar, ,Whitfield • heading through from •the rebound. An attack by the visitors was cleared by Ditchfield, and Banner and Woodhouse trans- ferred to the opposite end. A weak shot by the latter was kicked away by Hunter, and Davies se- curing the ball, raced away on the extreme left, and a sustained attack on the home goal resulted in a couple of unproductive corner kicks to the visitors. After gaining relief the -Way was again contested in the visitors' half, and Woodhouse scored the fifth goal with a fine shot. A few min- utes later the visitors' defence was again in trouble, and Connell saved 'from Woodhouse at close quar- ters. Davies was the visitors' most dangerous for-- ward, and he led many a raid on the home goal, but did not receive much support from his colleagues. Aspinall broke through and scored the sixth goal with a fast rising shot, and a little later Whitfield added a seventh goal. The visitors, again led by Davies, 'became aggressive, and. Kite was called upon several times to clear his lines. Ditchfield scored the home team's eighth goal with a splendid long shot just under the bar. From the centre kick the visitors combined well, and Taylor scored their first goal with a fast low shot. In the closing stages the visitors gained a'couple of corners, but did not improveion them., but in a scrimmage in the home goalmouth they forced the ball into the net, and scored their second goal. Result: War- ington Town, 8 goals; Liverpool Res., 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>TOWN AT LANCASTER.</head>
        <p>THE TOWN AT LANCASTER.</p>
        <p>The Town journey to Lancaster to-day on the 11.41 train, to meet Lancaster. Town in a friendly Match. The team will be selected from: Kite; Leah, Millward; Stephens, Standfast, Ditchfield, Payne; Eccleston, Whitfield, Aspinall, Woodhouse and Banner.</p>
        <p>The Reserve team meet Earlestown West at Hood- ittne, and will field a strong team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>GOALS. P. W. L. D. P. A. P. 8 6 1 1 24 7 13 8 6 1 ,1 24 9 13 8 6 2 022 12 12 8 3 3 2 17 14 8 8 3 4= 1 23 17 7 8 2 4 2 19 21 6 3210 6 4 4 8 1 5 2 13 33 4 3 1 1 1 9 11 3 8 1 6 1 15 38 3 3012 3 4 2 3021 3 7 1</p>
        <p>Howley United St. Elphin's Orford Juniors Latchford Recs. ... Penketh Juniors I3ewsey A.S. Appleton Hornets Latchford Cadets . Grappenhall Highfield Juniors . Widnes St. Mary's Widnes Juniors</p>
        <p>PENKETH JUNIORS v. APPLETON HORNETS.</p>
        <p>On the Recreation Ground.</p>
        <p>Penketh: White; Thomasson, Moss; Boyle, San- key, Swift; Winstanley, Glover, Goulden, Atkinson and Watson.</p>
        <p>Appleton: - Millar; Thornton, Fozaril ; Tegg, Howard, Chedlow ; Smith, Young, Brown, Green and Woolley. - -</p>
        <p>Refe;ee, Mr. T. Smith</p>
        <p>By good combination between Brown and Green, an advance was made on the home citadel. White had difficulty to save a good shot from Brown, and Goulden was prominent in transferring play to the visitors' end, where Howard cleared with a big kick. The visitors' forwards were playing a fine game, and Sankey was conspicuous in breaking up their attacks. The home forwards got away by fine passing, and Goulden scored with a low shot, after fifteen minutes' play. The visitors became keener, and in a few minutes Brown made the scores equal. Half-time: Penketh, 1 goal; Appleton, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>- Excitement ran high in the second half, and several free kicks had to be given to both sides. Winstanley missed a fine chance of gaining the lead for the home team. Brown and Young were playing in fine style for the visitors, and forced Moss to concede a corner, Glover heading the ball away. The visitors continued to have the better of the exchanges, and Howard was prominent at centre-half. Brown secured the ball and beating several opponents gained the lead for the visitors. Final score: Appleton Hornets, 2 goals; Penketh Juniors, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>ST. ELPHIN'S v. HOWLEY UNITED.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S v. HOWLEY UNITED.</p>
        <p>On the Steel-street ground, before a good crowd of spectators. St. Elphin's: Povey; Tunstall, Furness; Cooper, Miller, Gleave; Watson, England, Lee, Jones and Holden.</p>
        <p>Howley United: Walters; Rowlinson, Foster; Fitzgerald, Miller, Winstanley; Hibbert, Shake- shaft, Davies, Hassell land Humphries. Referee, Mr,- H. Berry. The United set a very fast pace and at once made an attack on the Saints' 'goal. Humphries hitting the post. From the goal kick Lee secured the, ball, and opening the game out enabled the Saints to become the aggressors. Jones did well on tile left, but met with strong opposition by Foster and Rowlinson. After a fruitless raid on the home goal Watson placed the visitors' goal in danger, and after twenty minutes' play Jones opened the score for the Saints. Play was again taken -to the visit- ors' end, and Watson centring accurately. Lee met the ball and drove it past Walters in a splendid manner, Walters being completely beaten. Half- time: St. Elphin's, 2 goals; Howley, nil. On resuming, the United re-arranged their for- ward line, but tlif Saints' half-backs were Ott the lop of their form, and defeated all their efforts to break through. England, Lee and Jones, the inside forwards of the Saints, showed grand combination from midfield, and repeatedly had the Visitors' defence in difficulties, 'weak shopting spoiling their efforts. Holden secured a centre from Watson, and was only inches' wide with a low shot. Holden again dribbled through and Walters only partially saved the shot. Watson rushed in and scored the third goal. The visitors could do very little against the home defence, and they were chiefly in their own half. Jones was playing a grand game for the Saints at inside left, and Lee cot through, but his' final shot hit the post, and dirpetiv after Holden hit the crossbar. Final: St. Elphin's, 3 goals; Howley United, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>BEWSEY A.S. V. HIGNFIELD.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY A.S. V. HIGNFIELD.</p>
        <p>At Lovely-lane, the ground being in a very heavy condition. Bewsey: Stephens; Main, Molyneaux; Atkinson, Dickenson, Perks; Worrall, Robinson, Schofield, Fear and• Roylance. 7 Highfield: Johnson; Worrall, Bell; Wishlade, Hankey, Barnes; Pritchard, Moulsdale, liorton, Ford and Tomlinson. Referee, Mr. T. Ford.' Before a good number of spectators the Highfield lads set the ball in motion, and at once attacked the home goal, Horton missing an easy chance of scoring. Then the Bewseyites got into their stride and made an advance on the visitors' goal, and after keen tackling Bell cleared strongly, and Tom- linson, transferring to 'the opposite end, was re- pulsed by Main when getting dangerous. Play was now in the visitors' favour, and Stephens showed good judgment in dealing with shot from Pritch- ard. Another attack by the visitors saw Worrall clear several times in fine style, but eventually Tom- linson was successful, and registered the first goal for the visitors. At the interval the score was:— Highfield, 1 goal; Bewsey, nil The second portion commenced with the Juniors attacking strongly, and in spite of the good form shown by the Adults' defenders, Ford increased the lead for the visitors. A strong effort was now made by the home forwards to reduce the arrears, Wor-</p>
        <p>rail and Johnson kicking well. A corner kick to the Adults was of no avail, and play was transferred to the home end, and Horton- added the third goal from a centre by Tomlinson. The home team doubled their efforts and forced a corner kick, At- kinson scoring /for the Adults. Another attack on the visitors' goal resulted in Schofield shooting too high. Result: Highfield, 3 goals; Bewsey, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>ORFORD JUNIORS v. LATCHFORD RECS. Played in Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Orford Juniors: Conley; Gibson, Doughty; Aus- tin, Witton, Rothweil; Buckley, Chisholm, Hurst, Clare and Belshaw.</p>
        <p>Latchford Recs.: Lockhart; Willcox, Dutton; Warburton, Cooper, Ward; Hcesom, A. N. Other, Green, A. N. Other and James. Referee, Mr. Burgess. Green set the ball rolling for the Recs. and the game opened very fast and keen. Each goal was quickly visited, and after three minutes' play Chis- holm scored with a grand shot for the Juniors. The Recs. played with more energy and a mis_kick by Doughty. caused the Juniors' goal to be in jeopardy, but the ball was eventually cleared away. The Juniors again took up the offensive, and Lock- hart showed fine judgment in clearing a centre from Belshaw was showing good form on the Juniors' left, and Hurst ought to have easily im- proved O'ne of his centres, but allowed himself to be robbed by Cooper. The Recs. then made a strong attack on the Juniors' citadel, and Green equalised with a low shot. The Recs. continued to have the better of the game, and Warburton narrowly missed. Play was again transferred to the Recs.' quarters, and Buckley, receiving the ball, close in, gave the Juniors the lead. Half-time: Orford Juniors, 2 goals; Latchford Recs., 1 goal. The second half opened in favour of the Juniors, and Hurst mulled an easy chance with only Lock- hart to beat. From the goal kick the Recs. made strenuous efforts to get on equal terms, and Conley saved finely from a crowd of players. Then Green forced Gibson to concede a corner kick, but this was not improved upon, and a few minutes later the Recs. gained a penalty kick, Willcox failing to convert. Chisholm now broke away on the Juniors' right. and Buckley, meeting his centre, hit the post, and during another attack Buckley was given off- side when well placed. Another rally by the Recs. severely tested the Juniors' defence, and James Missed by inches. Play was in midfield when time was called, the score being: Orford, 2 goals; Recs., 1 goal.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD CADETS v. CRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park,</p>
        <p>Cadets: Greenhalgh; Massey, Crannigan; Apple- ton, Gibson, Hope; Davenport, Jonas, Clarke, San- key and Mayers. Grappenhad: Hadkiss: Aikenhead. Rigby; Ash- croft, Jones, Willoughby; Williams, Smith, Knight, Kenwright and Watson.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. J. Wright. The Cadets were soon attacking keenly, and Davenport 'scored after five minutes' play. The visitors made the scores equal after fifteen minutes' play. Keen exchanges were witnessed, but the visitors proved very strong in their attacks, and added three more goals in quick succession. The Cadets then took up the running, and the visitors' goal was subjected to heavy bombardment, and Clarke and Davenport pierced the enemy's defence, the interval arriving with the score: Grappenhall. 4 goals; Cadets, 3 goals. • The second half opened very vigorously, and each defence was tested with keen shots, but they failed to find the net, and at threequarter time the visitors were still leading. Then following another assault by the Cadets a free kick was gained, and Gibson, with steady and sure aim, succeeded in sending the ball past Hadkiss and made the scores equal. The Cadets were the most aggressive and scored again, but the referee would not allow the point for in- fringement of the off-side rule. A hard-fought game resulted in a draw of 4 goals each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WIDNES JUNIORS V. WIDNES ST. MARY'S.</head>
        <p>WIDNES JUNIORS V. WIDNES ST. MARY'S.</p>
        <p>Therst meeting of the above clubs in the above competition took place last week on the Tanhouse ground, before a fair number of supporters. The result was .a draw of. one goal each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Orford Juniors v. Howley Uniied; Mr. J. Wright. Latchford Recs. v. Appleton Hornets; Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. St. Elphin's v. Widnes Juniors; Mr. Burgess. Widnes St. Mary's v. Bewsey A.S.; Mr. T. Ford. Highfield Juniors v. Grappenhall; Mr. J. Smith, jun.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S v. WIDNES JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>At Steel-street,</p>
        <p>St. Elphin's: W. Povey ; W. Tunstall (capt.), V. Furness; D. Cooper, R. Miller, H. Gleave; W. Wat- son. H. Shelmerdine, W. Lee, A. Jones and J. Holden. -</p>
        <p>Reserves: G. England and J. Barton. We are informed by the Orford Juniors' Secretary that they have obtained peroaission. to play their fixtures on the King and' Queen ground, and will play Howley United there instead of Lovely-lane, as previously arranged.</p>
        <p>CP.OSFIELD'S JUNIRRS v. LIVERPOOL FARM</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At Newton-le-Willows.</p>
        <p>Cresfieiti Juniors: .J. Tabernacle; J. Jones, S Hardy: A. Mason (capt.), C. Harrison, S. Ravens croft; D. Morrison, D. O'Brien, H. M. Beswick, J Sudlow and H. Bissell. Team meets at 2.30 at Winwick Church.</p>
        <p>Village Misanthrope: " Lordy ! 'Tis always mina vulishness. First 'tis all football, and now this yere war be all hte go!"—" Punch."</p>
        <p>MMIN</p>
      </div>
