      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>" SMOKES " AFTER BATTLE.</head>
        <p>" SMOKES " AFTER BATTLE.</p>
        <p>Captain S. T. Boast's Story Of South Lancashire Regiment.</p>
        <p>In the course of a letter to the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), Captain S. T. Boast, South Lancashire Regiment, says : "Gifts of ' smokes ' have recently arrived here, through you, from our friends in War- rington and district. I ask you to convey the sincere thanks of our men to the subscribers to your hind for these acceptable gifts. Several of the cases arrived yesterday and were parti- cularly welcome. We took part in the recent big fight and were withdrawn from the line on Sunday night. We have been marching since then; yesterday we marched through heavy rain and everyone was soaked, but won- derfully cheerful. Thanks to the arrival of the 'smokes,' I was able to make an issue, which the men were very grateful for, as their stock was very low. . . We have been inspected by our Corps Commander to-day and all ranks were complimented on the good work done by the Battalion in the recent successful operations. . . . The objective was gained in grand style and the new position. well consoli- dated. The Battalion collected quite a large number of prisoners, all of whom seemed very glad to get safely back behind our lines. Our casualties were not very heavy, many of them being just slight wounds."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Poor Mistress.</head>
        <p>The Poor Mistress.</p>
        <p>SIIPERIOR GIRL (steady) Wanted as General in small family: eozhfortable home: wages £24; cood refergtees.—lsiorris. Wyngate-road. Hale Cheshire.</p>
        <p>THE 20TH CENTURY PILL CO., , DEPT. D., 244, GT. COLLEGE-ST., LONDON, N.W.</p>
        <p>The total proceeds on Thursday were 01.</p>
        <p>"Here's an account of an accident," read Mr. Brown, "that happened at White's house. The servant girl put some gunpowder in the fire, and she was blown through the roof." "Poor Mrs. White!" sympathised Mrs. Brown. "She has so much trouble with her maids. That makes the fourth that's left her without giving notice."</p>
        <p>. . .</p>
      </div>
