      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>CHURCHES' AUTUMN SALES.</head>
        <p>CHURCHES' AUTUMN SALES.</p>
        <p>Money Raised Amid Pretty Scenes hi Schoolrooms.</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR ON NONCONFORMISTS AND ROMAN</p>
        <p>Tno DAY SALE.</p>
        <p>£llO Raised By The St. Peter's Parishioners.</p>
        <p>The long and low writing forms tucked away into the class rooms and the erection of delicately-contrived structures on which com- modities for all tastes were assembled, created a pleasant and attractive stage for St. Peter's Church sale of work, held in the John-street Schoolroom on Monday and Tuesday. Mrs. J. Charlton Parr, of Grappenhall, journeyed specially from Hereford on Monday to declare the sale open, and as she stood with purse in hand ready to perform the opening ceremony, the Vicar (the Rev. B. Percival) re- ferred to the necessity of the sale to keep the organisations of the parish going strongly. Last year, from a similar event, a grant of £65 was made to the Curates' Fund, £25 to the Church Army Helpers' Fund, £l4 to the Church- wardens' Fund, and £l4 to other organisations. Continuing, he hoped that there would be a great spiritual awakening in the Church. They had had a splendid example set them by the nation in going to war for the right, and Church people must consecrate themselves in like manner and do unselfish work for God, who, he believed, would in time grant them an ,overwhelming victory. The Vicar spoke appreciative words of Mrs. Parr, who, he said, had every other year opened the sale for them. Mrs. Parr then declared the sale open in a charming and graceful manner. Mr. T. S. Steel, vicar's warden, proposed a vote of thanks to her, especially congratulating her on having made so long a journey in order to be present with them that afternoon. He hoped she would be rewarded by a very suc- cessful sale of work. The parishioners were delighted to see so many old friends amongst them, and he would remind them that just as much money was required •as ever. He hoped that they would be successful throughout the sale. (Applause,) Mr. T. Reddish, people's warden, seconded, remarking that it was very kind of Mrs. Parr to come amongst those who were following humbler walks of life, and associating herself so kindly with their work.</p>
        <p>Amongst those present were the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) and Mrs. Willis, the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, the Rev. B. V. and Mrs. Pigot, Mrs. BracecazaP, Mrs. Harrison, Mrs. W. P. Rylands, and the Misses White. The stallholders were:— Vicarage Stall: Mrs. Percival, Mrs. Greaves, Mrs. Kay, Mrs. East. Refreshment Stall: Mrs! Lindley, Mrs. Grounds, Mrs. Atkinson, Mrs. Reddish, Miss Grounds, Miss Hanson, Miss Dickens. Miscellaneous Stall : Mrs. Steel and Mrs. Cow- den.</p>
        <p>Flower Stall: The Misses Young and Miss Crossland. •</p>
        <p>Sunday School Stall: Mrs. Smith, Miss Hughes, and the teachers.</p>
        <p>Sweet Stall: Miss Percival, Miss Houghton, and Miss Fletcher.</p>
        <p>Bran Tub.: The Misses Hardy. Competitions in charge of Mr. E. A. Padmore. A mong the competitions were games of hoop-la and darts, wounded soldiers who attend the church being active in the control of the latter. Their instructions how to aim straight were in- variably attempted, and in some cases fraught with success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>CATHO LICS.</head>
        <p>CATHO LICS.</p>
        <p>Of the four sales of work held in the borough this week in connection with Churches, that in Fairfield Schols in aid of the funds of the Parish Church was the most extensive—it almost assumed the proportions of a bazaar— and interesting because of the observations of the Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis). Remarking that there were in the parish 20,000 souls, all under the care of the clergy, the reverend canon remarked that some might say there were a good many Roman Catholics and Nonconformists, but, for all that, he had always looked upon every soul in the parish as belonging to him and under the charge of the clergy. He looked upon the Church of England as the one and only true branch of Christ's Holy Catholic Church in this land. He did not consider the Roman Church was the Church of the land. A well-instructed Roman Catholie would say, "I believe the Vicar of Rome to be the head Of the Church." "We should not acknowledge that," commented the Rector, going on to remark that if he were to ask Nonconformists whether they believed in the Priesthood of the Church they would reply, "Yes. Everyone who is baptised is a priest." "There is a Priesthood of the Laity," the Rector went on. "You could grant that and at the same time say that there is a Clerical Priest- hood. So I look upon all those within one limit as under our charge." This was a prelude to the Rector's appeal for support of the Assistant Clergy Fund. They were told, he said, that the labourer was worthy of his hire, and he felt that they ought to aim at £2OO a year for every priest and £l5O for every deacon. There being four assistant clergy, £BOO a year was required. He was ask- ing for £650. The parish, through its officers, had promised him £2OO a year—£loo from the offertory and £lOO from the sale. For what the clergy did at the Training College £5O was re- ceived from that source. "I am responsible for £400," added the Rector. "1 do not mean to say that I give that. I did give it for many years and crippled myself in order to do so. I could not go on any longer, and now I have to appeal to generous friends to help me out. If £4OO is provided from these sources then £2OO is not too much to ask from the parish, and I, therefore, hope you will help as liberally as possible to day." (Applause.) . The Hon. Mrs. Willis (of Halsnead) declared the sale open in an interesting speech, and emphasised the necessity of also supporting liberally the Church Repair Fund, the Missions and the Schols. Her husband, Mr. R. A. D'Anyers Willis replied on her behalf to the vote of thanks proposed by Councillor Craik (Rector's warden) and Mr. Edward Sherwood (people's warden). During the evening concerts were given, the artistes being Mr. and Mrs. J Sephton, Miss Reynolds, and, Master N. Davies. ' At the opening ceremony yesterday, by Mrs. Coxon, Statham Lodge, the Rector announced with great satisfaction that the proceedings of Thursday's sale were £ll5.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Coxon delivered a noteworthy speech, in the course of which she said that as the teaching of the Church had sustained the men out at the Front in their hour of trial, it was the duty of those left behind to see that the Church was kept in a vigorous condition. A vote'of thanks was passed to Mrs. Coxon, on the proposal of Mr. R. W. Antwiss, seconded by Mr.. T. J. Hopkins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>ST. JAMES'.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES'.</p>
        <p>2.,H0 Realised In One Day's Sale.</p>
        <p>What portended to be a successful day's labour was heralded by .a great assembly of parishioners at the opening of the annual sale of work at„St. James', Latchford, on Thursday. The 1?., IL B. Firth, in the course of a few explanatory words describing the objects of the sale, mentioned that the amount realised would be divided among several, organisations in the parish in need of financial assistance. Miss M. White, of Warrington, then in a few well-chosen woods declared the sale open. The Vicar having proposed a vote of thanks to Miss White, she replied with a remark which was succinct and happy: "I hope you will all empty your _purses." _ _</p>
        <p>The stall-e: were laden with innumerable useful and ornamental articles. The success of the sale was in no small part due to the efforts of the collectors of the Sale of Work Bank.</p>
        <p>The stalls were in charge of the following:— Congregational and Church Stall: Mrs. L. Harris, Mrs. Tatlock, Mrs. Woodhead, Mrs. Davies, Miss Cruthers, Miss Parry, Miss Tay- lor, Mrs. Ingham. Sweet Stall: Miss P. Gibbs, Miss M. Acton, Miss P. Allen. Crockery Stall: Miss N. Foulkes. Refreshment Stall : Mrs. G. Bowcock, Mrs. J. Reynolds, Miss H. Bowcock, Miss E. Bow- cock, Miss Kilpack. Flower and Fruit Stall: Miss 0. O'Brien, Miss D. Hill.</p>
        <p>St. Hilda's Mission Stall: Miss Peers, Miss Isherwood, Mrs. Williamson, Miss A. Thorn- ton, Mrs. Bennett, Mrs. Plumb, Miss Ker- mode.</p>
        <p>Tea Room: Mrs. Fargher, Mrs. Hands, Mrs. Grant, Mrs. O'Brien, Mrs. Brayshaw, Mrs. Davidson, Mrs. E. Sykes, Mrs. Dykes, Mrs. MacCallum, Miss M. G. Horton, Mrs. Clarke, Miss N. Miller, the Misses Highley, Mrs. Wil- liams, Miss E. Harding, Miss D. Horton, Mrs. Harris.</p>
        <p>Refreshment Helpers: Miss M. Bailey, Miss J. Jefferson, Mrs. W. Lingard, Mrs. E. Astley, Mrs. W. Davies.</p>
        <p>Ice Cream: Miss G. Gibbs, Miss A. Foulkes.</p>
        <p>The whole of the proceedings passed off satis- factorily, and the total amount realised was £l4O. • The secretary for the day of the sale was the Rev. H. H. Corshie, a former curate of the parish; while Mr. J. F. Fargher acted as treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>GOLBORNE STREET BAPTIST.</head>
        <p>GOLBORNE STREET BAPTIST.</p>
        <p>Two Days' Sale To Conclude To-day (Saturday).</p>
        <p>A two days' sale of work—on Thursday and to-day (Saturday)—is being held this week in connection with the Golborne-street Baptist Church, to reduce a debt of £940 on the church buildings. Thursday's opening ceremony was performed by Mrs. Ogden (wife of the pastor), and with her on the platform were Mr. J. Taylor (chair- man), the Rev. H. Ogden and the Rev. G. Curtis.</p>
        <p>The Pastor thanked Mr. Taylor for presiding, and also his Worship the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock) for a cheque for £5. The Mayor had always been kindly disposed towards the Church. Some people were asking why hold sales of work in these times? He (the speaker) sometimes felt in sympathy with those people, but at the same time they could not repudiate debts which were contracted before the war. They needed better premises for the Sunday school work, and it was for the sake of the children that they constructed the new buildings and entered upon those liabilities. It was ex- tremely necessary that at the present time the Church should be adequately equipped, and not staggering along under financial burdens she could not bear. (Applause.) Mrs. Ogden then opened the sale. All re- ligious functions, she said, deserved their utmost support. In these days people must remember the work of the Sunday school, for as the war was now so sadly destroying our manhood, we must, in the 'coming years, be ready to train the young. (Applause.) On the propos,ition of the Rev. T. T. 'James, seconded by Mr. William Owen, a vote of thanks was accorded the Opener, Chairman, and Messrs. W. A. Naylor, who had kindly given the wood to erect the stalls, and has been very generous in times past. The sale will be continued to-day (Saturday). when the opening ceremonY will be performed by the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>HAPPY AUGURY FOR SECOND DAY.</head>
        <p>HAPPY AUGURY FOR SECOND DAY.</p>
        <p>The opening ceremony on the second day was performed by Mrs. Segar Owen, to whom Mr. 'l'. S. Steel extended a hearty welcome. They were very anxious, he added, that they should maintain .the good record they had of raising the amount of money they needed each year. -Under the existing circumstances they had so far done very well. Announcing that the previous day's effort had realised ..t6l Bs. Bd. a statement which was received with applause the Vicar (the Rev. B. Percival) said that there was a slight- falling off in the amount, which was easy to account for. Lady Greenall was not able to be with them, as she was so exceedingly busy in Prance, where she was doing great work in connection with the war. She. was expending a great deal of .her gener- osity in that direction, and so had not been able to send them so inch as in past years. In conclusion, the Vicar said they were all very pleased to have Mrs. Owen with them, remark- ing that it was her first appearance at a sale of work.</p>
        <p>In a charming little speech, Mrs. Owen con- gratulated the workers on such a good display, which was all the more gratifying ihen they remembered that their time was greatly taken up in looking after their relatives at the Front. Mr. T. Reddish proposed and Mr. G. F. Greaves seconded a vote of thanks to Mrs. Owen.</p>
        <p>The total proceeds realised £llO 10s. 5d</p>
        <p>A RANK IMPOSTOR.</p>
        <p>Winwick Patient Gets Six Months' Hard Labour For .Stealing Jewellery.</p>
        <p>ABUSED HOSPITALITY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. Alfred Watkin (in the chair) and Mr. Samuel Davies, at a special sitting held at the Lymm Police Court on Thursday morn- ing, Joseph Cadoza, a deserter from the Oxford and Bucks Light Infantry, was sentenced to six months' hard labour on a charge of enter- ing the house of Mr. J. H. Holt, Kildonan, Cherry-lane, and stealing jewellery to the value of Al 19s. 6d. Prisoner was wearing the uniform of a corporal of the Canadian Forces. He pleaded "Guilty." Mr. Holt said prisoner was introduced to him by his family. They had tea together at his house, and prisoner then told him that he was having ten days' furlough and was going to spend the time with some friends in War- rington, but he would much ,rather spend it in .Lymm. Mr. Holt then told him that if he could get lodgings in Lymm he could come up to his house for meals. Prisoner went out, but returned in about ten minutes and told him that he had obtained lodgings at Austin Cottage, Booth's Hill. 'During the next fort- night prisoner came quite regularly to his house. On Saturday, October 21st, after they had had dinner, they told him that they would lie out during the afternoon, but would be back again at 5 p.m. for tea. Witness re- turned about 3.45 and was surprised to find the back door open. The scullery window was undone. He went outside and noticed some marks on the wall that had apparently been made by a man's foot. Later in the evening Mrs. Kennedy, of Austin Cottage, sent for him and told him that she was afraid the soldier had gone away without paying his lodgings. They missed a gold watch and other, articles of jewellery, and 'a lady's dressing case, valued at £2 10s. Prisoner had told him that he had been in hospital at Harrogate. At the conclusion' of his evidence Mr. Holt complimented Acting-Sergeant Griffiths, who, he said, had done his work most excellently. Acting-Sergeant Griffiths' stated that after receiving the complaint he made inquiries and found that prisoner had been seen going in the direction of the station, carrying the lady's dressing case in his hand. Later he found that several of the missing articles had been pawned with Mr. E. V. Morton, pawnbroker, Knutsford-road, Latchford. Prisoner was ap- prehended by the Leeds police at the instiga- tion of the Lymm police. Witness went to Leeds to bring the man back, and when charged prisoner replied "All right." Two bangles, one pendant, and a gold albert had not yet been recovered. A gold lever watch, gun-metal watch, gold' .albert and a lady's dressing case were recovered. A statement made by prisoner was read, in which he pleaded, for leniency, and asked to be sent to the trenches again to redeem his good name. He had travelled over 14,000 miles to enlist in the British Army. He came over from America to France with a consignment of horses, and tried to enlist in France, but did not succeed. He returned to America and saw the British Consul in New York, who sent him to Liverpool, where he enlisted in the Oxford and Bucks Light Infantry. He was promoted lance-corporal in the field and was sent to the Lord Derby War Hospital at Win- wick, suffering from shell shock and an attack of gas. He had never been arrested before and had a good character in the Army. In reply to the Chairman, prisoner admitted being several months absent.</p>
        <p>A Persistent Thief.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Sutherland said prisoner had been: a deserter since the 13th of June and had been living in various parts of the country ever since. He had been "taking in" people who had been treating him well. At Birming- ham he absconded with £25 worth of jewellery which he stole from friends. The necklace (produced) was part of the haul. He had a pawnticket in his possession for two diamond rings, which were pawned at Liverpool; also a ticket for a ring at Warrington. He had been staying with some friends at Church Stretton, who had treated him well, and when he left he took all he could lay his hands on. He had been keeping company with a respect- able girl in Lancashire, with sad results. A letter from Wales stated that he had been there looking for the widow of his officer on the pretence. of giving her some jewellery that her husband had left which was all lies, and he was nothing but a rank impostor." Self-Confessed " Rotter."</p>
        <p>Prisoner confessed he was "a rotter," but the Lancashire incident was not true. He had always been decent with ladies. When he told a lie he had to tell a hundred to cover it up. He always took the jewellery because he was frightened. He had not the nerve and enough pluck to go back to the Army. In sentencing prisoner to six calendar months, with hard labour, the Chairman re- marked that he was an utter impostor, and it was quite clear that if he had not been in trouble before he ought to have been. He was a 'disgrace to the King's uniform. He had been going about the country taking ad- vantage of people who had befriended him. They also wanted to confirm Mr. Holt's state- ment as regards Acting-Sergeant Griffiths; he had done his work well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <p>Warrington Market Prices.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's Warrington Market wheat was 9s. 6d. to 9s. 9d. per 701 b. roats, ss. 3d. to ss. 4d. per 451 b.; flour, 495. to 525. per 2401 b.; thirds, 2s. 9d. to 2s. 11d. per 201 b..; bran, 2s. id. to 2s. 3d. per 201 b.</p>
        <p>Addresses on "The Christian Church." Commencing to-morrow (Sunday) the Rev. Irvine Lister will, on every Sunday afternoon during this month, turn his attention to that interesting and vital subject, "The Christian Church." Mr. Lister's Sunday afternoon ad- dresses have become noteworthy events in the religious life or the borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday, presided over by the Rev. T. T. James. Mr. Metcalfe gave an address upon "Forgetting the past and looking forward to the things before us." Miss Pitt sang, "0 rest in the Lord" and "The gift of peace," which were greatly appreciated. Mr. Payton presided at the organ. Mr. D. McEachern and the Secretary, Mr. B. Farrar, took part in the meeting. Gunner W. Hilson and Private G. T. Yeates wished to be remem- bered to the brethren. Private F. Brandwood returned thanks for letter of sympathy sent to him. A vote of sympathy was passed to Mr. Wm. Daniels and wife in the loss of their son, who was killed in action at the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Whist Drive for Thelwall Heys.</head>
        <p>Whist Drive for Thelwall Heys.</p>
        <p>An extremely well attended whist drive and dance was held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening in aid of the funds of the Thelwall Heys Military Convalescent Home. It was announced that whist would be played from 7.30 to 9 o'clock andthat dancing would be &amp;gaged in from 10.30 p.m. to 2 a.m., but in order that the wounded soldiers, to the number of 23, who were present from the Home,' might enjoy a dance before leaving by the Latchford tram at 10 o'clock, some lively measures were brought on at an earlier hour. When the Band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment was responsible for the dance programme it is easy to be assured that A. capital treat was pro- vided. Mrs. F. A. Frost, commandant of the hospital, and a large number of the staff of ladies were present and looked after the com- fort of the guests. Mrs. Densham and Mrs. Kerfoot presided in an 'able manner over the refreshment department, while Mr. Haughton and Mr. jowett, who acted as M.C.'s and made the arrangements, worked indefatigably. The hall was beautifully decorated by Messrs. Peter Walker and Son (through Mr. Hutton). Twelve handsome prizes were offered for com- petition and great interest was centred in the whist drive at nearly a hundred -tables. A large box of cigarettes was presented as a. special prize amongst the soldiers. His Wor- ship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) and the Mayoress were amongst the players, and the Mayor kindly presented the prizes. There are thirty soldiers in the Home and the greater number of tickets for Thursday's entertain- ment were sold by the men themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>FARMERS AND THEIR PRICES.</head>
        <p>FARMERS AND THEIR PRICES.</p>
        <p>Statements And Figures In The Coming Dispute.</p>
        <p>It was alleged by Mr. G. B. Stanton in the House of Commons on Thursday that farmers in. Lancashire and other *parts of the country are holding back potatoes for higher prices, despite the higher prices they are offered, and Mr. Runciman (President of the Board of Trade) replied that if there are farmers or merchants who are in a position. to put potatoes on the market and are unreasonably withholding them the Government have power under the existing law, and will not hesitate to use that power if cases are brought under their notice. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>AN INTERESTING COMPARISON.</head>
        <p>AN INTERESTING COMPARISON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Mr. Weir said that as Potato Market on Wed- a farmer he was glad to nesday the average price say there was little to ruled at 11s, per cwt. grumble at at the present This time last year the time, and they were cer- price was from ss. 6d. to tainly, as their landlord 68. per cwt. said, "getting a little of their own back."—Speech at dinner of Walton Estate Tenants in October.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>MARRIED •ON FURLOUGH.</head>
        <p>MARRIED •ON FURLOUGH.</p>
        <p>Private William Dennett And Miss Nellie Williams.</p>
        <p>While over on short leave from France, Private William Dennett, of the R.A.M.C., met Miss Nellie Williams, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. T. J. Williams, 11, St. Mary-street, Latchford, at the altar at the Parish Church on Wednesday, and the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) conducted the marriage ceremony. The honeymoon is being spent at Southport and ontMonday the bridegroom. will return to France. In civil life Private Dennett was a school attendance officer, a member of the Parish Church Choir, and was well known as a referee in Junior Association Football circles.</p>
        <p>The wedding was a pretty one, the bride, who was given away by her father, being charmingly attired. The bridesmaids were Miss E. Williams (sister) and Miss J. George (cousin of the bride). Mr. C. W. Davenport acted as best man. Among the delightful musical selections rendered by Miss Reynolds on the organ was Mendelssohn's Wedding March.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>CRUEL PARENTS.</head>
        <p>CRUEL PARENTS.</p>
        <p>Sent To Gaol For Neglecting Their</p>
        <p>Children:</p>
        <p>Sentepces of two months and one month respectively was imposed upon Mary Alice Kirk, 24, King-street, and her husband, John Kirk, at the Borough Police Court yesterday, for neglecting their three children. Mr. A. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., and said there was a sum of 50s. a week coming into the house, tlie man being engaged as a tanner, and the woman going out charing. The family had lived with another woman, and there being only two rooms, the children had slept on the sofa and on chairs. They were all ill-clad and in a filthy condition, and had been removed to the Union Hospital. The woman had been found by the police wandering the streets in 'a filthy drunken con- dition. and her children had been taken in by Councillor Broadhurst, and fed. In sentencing the prisoners. the Mayor (chairman of the Bench), said the woman was more to blame than her husband.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>ALLEGED FALSE STATEMENT.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED FALSE STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Music Hall Artiste's Appeal For</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>Exemption.</head>
        <p>Exemption.</p>
        <p>A,case which attracted unusual attention vin9 heard at the Borough Police Court on FridaYs when William Briscoe, a music hall artistes was charged with (1) Making a false statement for the purpes° of obtaining exemption for himself Under the Military Service Act, 1916; (2) Being the holder of a certificate he unlaw- fully did fail to give notice to the militarY authorities that the conditions named therein are no longer satisfied; (3)Being a person deemed to be enlisted under the Military Service Act, failed to report self. The Chief Con4able asked for a remand until the following day, when Captain Mc , prosecutor, would be present, and also would Mr. T. S. Steel, who was interested in the case - Outlining the facts of the case, he said the Phail the prisoner put in an application for exemptio° 'under the Military Service Act. The statement he made was as follows: " I have been married for the past eight years,, and have two young children, aged seven years and two years respectively. I have been in business on my own account as a grocer, pro- vision dealer and fruit and vegetable dealer, for the past six years. My wife has been in a delicate state of health for some considerable time, and is under a medical attendant for fainting fits. I carry on my business at 41, Buttermarket-street, Warrington, in .the tiara() of Wm. West. I pay an annual rent of £3O and rates for my business premises, and MY weekly turnover is from £3O to £4O. I estiixiate that the value .of my business at the present time is at the least £5OO. Prior to the war had one employe, but he obtained emploYmell as a munition worker at Dallam Forge, and have filled his place with a boy aged about years. I have been unable to obtain any other assistance, and I have to do the lifting of the heavy cases of goods myself. I Use my prenliSeg as a lock-up shop, and rent a house at 13, Bo 4" street at Bs. 6d. a week. My mother, who 15 partially dependent upon me and who resides at Manchester, is an invalid, and I allow h_ towards her maintenance, on an average 75. tw• per week. My mother-in-law, Mrs. Walker, had four sons in the Army, but one was killed. I reside with her, and have to help to keel). her." The Chief Constable said the whole of the facts were nothing but a fabrication. For the past eighteen months the prisoner had seldorn , been out of employment on the stage. He bad fulfilled engagements at Leeds, Sheffield, Mary chester, Halifax, Blackburn, Glasgow, Brixton, Liverpool, Brighton, Kingston-on:Thames; South London, West •Ham, Warrington, en several other places. He wanted to-disconneel' himself from the statements. Mr. Steel, wh" had represented the prisoner, appeared to have been misled by the statement. The prisoner was trying to establish that he was responsible for the carrying on of the business, whereas IT had been away week after week, and montn after month. The case was adjourned as requested.</p>
