        <p>FIRST CHIME. Runcorn Union Official Congratulated On Approaching Marriage. , Mr. W. E. Hough, acting clerk to the Run- corn Board of Guardians and Rural District Council, wag congratulated at Tuesday's meet- ing on his approaching marriage. Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., referred to the man- ner in which Mr. Hough had performed the duties in Mr., Ashton's absence. He deserved the highest credit, and he was always willing to give information to any Guardian that might require it. He (Mr. Linaker), as a Guardian and District Councillor, greatly ap- preciated the courteous way in which Mr. Hough always treated every member of the Board and District Council. (Hear, hear.) He hoped he would be happy, in his married life, and he could assure him that he would find joys in married life and companionship which the single man could not imagine. They had in Mr. Hough an excellent acting clerk; everything had run smoothly and he had done the work in a most creditable manner. In moving a resolution .of congratulation, Mr. Linaker hoped they would be able to present him with a more tangible proof of the esteem and good will in which he was held. Mr. Morris, as the senior Runcorn Guardian present, said that Mr. Hough had done every- thing well in respect to the work of the Guar- dians. Mr. Woodward remembered the time when Mr. Ashton held the position that Mr. Hough now occunied. 'He wished that Mr. Rough's future might be one of great prosperity, happi- ness and success. "I hope," added the speaker amidst laughter, "that all his difficulties will be little ones." Mr. John L. Jones, in putting the motion, heartily endorsed all that had been said. Mr. Hough was very much obliged for the kind words that had been spoken. His connec- tion with the Board of Guardians, although not a Very long one, had been a very happy one. During the period that their own Clerk had been fighting with His Majesty's Forces, he (Mr. Hough) received many kindnesses from the members individually and collectively, and he hoped those kindnesses would be extended to him during the time he might be acting as an official of the Board. He specially men- tioned his indebtedness for the able assistance given him by the staff. THE WEDDING. On Thursday • morning the marriage took place at the Runcorn Parish Church of Mr. W. E. Hough, solicitor, of 54, York-street, Runcorn, and Miss Edith • McLean, of Rose Cottage, Higher Runcorn. The event was of a -quiet character. The ceremony was conducted by the Rev. Mr. Allen, curate. The bride, who wore a navy blue cos- tume and a hat te match, was given away by her uncle, Mr. Arthur Riley. Mr. W. J. Wild- ing was the best man. The bridesmaid, Miss Mabel Collinge, was attired in a brown costume and a white felt hat. The bride and bridesmaid both wore gold wristlet watches, the gift of the bridegroom. The honeymoon is being spent in London. Before entering the legal profession, Mr. Hough achieved considerable success as an athlete and a footballer. He won many prizes at first-crass sports meetings, and played both Rugby and Association football. At preient he is acting as magistrates' clerk, during the ill- ness of the holder of the office, and as clerk to the Runcorn Board of Guardians and Rural District Council, and as registrar of births, marriages and deaths in place of Major Ashton; who has only recently returned from the Front, and is still on military duty. Mr. Hough has practised in many of the courts of 'both Cheshire and Lancashire. THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS. ORDERS. Tuesday.—Fall in at 7.30 prompt. Physical and Company Drill. Semaphore Class at 8 Friday.—Fall in at 7.30. Class Firing. Range Officer, Lieut. 11. Kitchen. Ambulance Section to parade under orders of Corporal E. Lea. Orderlies.—Corporal W. Rigby and Lance- Corporal Hackwell. J. B. STARKgY, , Commanding Officer.</p>
