        <p>France, was brought to England to undergo an operation, but afterwards returned to the Front, where he served another eight months before being killed. He was 24 years of age, and Was educated at the National School. He worked at Cockhedge Factory for nine. years, but at the time of enlisting was employed by Messrs. Joseph Cresfield and Sons, Ltd. His brother Tom is in the Navy. LCE..CORPL. E. GRAINGER. Splendid Soldier &amp; Thoroughly Reliable N.C.O. A well-known local pigeon fancier, in the per. son of Lance-Corporal Edwin Grainger, ha been killed in action while servidg with the South Lanes. Regiment. His father, Mr. Edwin Grainger, .17, Clifton-street Latchford, has re- ceived ;two letters announcing this fact, but official news is not yet to hand. The first letter, from .Second-Lieutant A. Watts, ran:—" It is my painful duty to inform you of the terrible loss you have recently suffered in the death of your son Edwin, who</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. J. H ICKSON. A Former Rylands Bros.' Employe. Mrs. Bluck, 53, Howley-lane, who„herself has four sons with the Colours, viz., 'Privates Edward Bluck and' John Arthur Bluck, of the South Lancs. Regiment, Rifleman Chas. Dunn Bluck, of. the King's Own Royal Rifles, and Gunner Percy Bluck, R.G.A.—has received official news that Lance-Corporal Jonathan Hickson, of the Lancashire Fusiliers, who for twelve months lodged at her house, was killed in action on the 12th ult. An orphan, nineteen years of age, and a native of Burnley, Lance-Corporal Hickson came to Warrington about two years ago, and was employed at the works of Messrs. Rylands Bros. Ltd. He.enlisted about twelve months ago, and went to the Front in March. PRIVATE F. LONGSHAW. Killed After Only Three Months In France. After spending only three months in France, Private Fred Longshaw is now reported killed. Official news reached his father, Mr. John Longshaw, who resides at Lynhurst, Thelwall ' New-road, Latchford Without, on \ Saturday morning, saying he had been killed in action on October 12th. Well known in the Latchford Without dis- trict and in Warrington, where he was born, Private Longshaw had been in the Army twelve months, and was 35 years of age. He was edu- cated at the People's College, and latterly' attended Grappenhall Church. After leaving school he became a member of the office staff of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., with whom he was connected for 18 years. A</p>
