        <p>BRITISH FARM CROPS. Less Wheat And More Hay This Summer. A preliminary statement issued by the Board of Agrioulture showing the. estimated total pro- duce of corn; pulse, and hay crops in England and Wales in the year 1916 states that the.total production of wheat 'amounts to 6,942,559 quarters, or about a million and a half quarters less than last year. There is a decrease in the acreage under wheat, of .257,962 acres: Barley: with an increased acreage, has given a total of 700,000 quarters in excess of last year. Oats are only slightly better than last year, • with It total production of 10,461,164 quarters: The entire production of bay is estimated at 8,837,079 tons, or fully al per cent. more than last year. Lancashire Wit. It was the great event of the season—the final for one of the workmen's cups—in Lancashire. The rivals, who had hailed from Preston dis- trict, were the happy possessors of a good field, and great was their disgust on finding that they had to play on ground with scarcely a blade of grass visible. Snid one of the visitors, plaintively: • "Why, there's hardly a blade of grass to be seen." "Well," retorted one of the home team, "you ain't come to graze, have yer?"</p>
